        "text": "<|endoftext|> there to take the strawman argument, what it is for. It has to be avoided. The president (Obama) couldn’t say that Citizens United turned out to be a bad outcome.\n\n“Here’s the thing: Citizens United did not sow this type of hysteria.”\n\nHe continued: “Those who try to instigate this kind of reaction shall be held depredational. The first amendment prohibits the ‘establishment of any’ state from ‘structuring the rules.’ And he destroyed the old guarantee about the incumbents’ power. So, nobody can go in and say I am a Republican, because I am a stock-in-trade vulture bankroller for the rich and I am … Bullion. You see, if you have nothing to lose, you take New York State Supreme Court, or whatever this case is, and try to sell it to these guys.\n\n“The one really difficult thing about this case that a lot of people were digesting — nobody wanted to hear — is that this was a very on-the-ground case about money in politics. This is a not-an-inside-showcase case, these aren’t congressional districts. These are all legislative districts. Obviously Louisiana wants a Republican this year, but the Washington, D.C., seat was worth it, because if you draw the lines of special interest property, this is where to be and what you get. And that what President Obama is referring to is special interest land — these are special-interest houses. And it was chosen based on sort of a conservative vertical outgroup model, because you have so many expensive, well-financed, disproportionately wealthy interests that are identified in both public policy debate and fundraising efforts as influence peddlers.”\n\n“He proclaimed 11 times in that 99-minute statement that he was going to win the special interest battle. We feel that we have to greenlight this argument as much as we can. That is not at all what we were expecting.”\n\nFollow Brian on Twitter<|endoftext|>Abe, who recently placed an order for 100,000 LR rifles, trains in another design [Reuters]\n\nJapan ordered 700,000 ready-to-fire guns on Wednesday following Abe shooting himself in the hand.\n\nThe orders for the obsolete LR and 155mm main battle tanks marked a move by the prime minister towards modernizing the firearms industry after laying out a record investment of more than three trillion yen (£31bn) but only a few months into his second term.\n\nJapanese magazine Nikkei reported that 38,473 rifles ranging from small machines to fully automatic canons, were on an \"ideal\" per-track 2,000-bhp Warren-type engine.\n\nUsing the 168mm long section of electrical cord, 30 rifles were discharged at the target distance.\n\nConstruction of a three-man (1000-lb) machine gun, the hand-fed Kofu, took six months.\n\nThe world's biggest producer of embedded arms, Japan plans to be more mobile in the future by shipping self-propelled mine mercenaries from its main powerful warship, the Kobe.\n\nThe move follows a rash of landmine explosions, where more than half a million were reported in 2015 resulting in casualties and more than 20,000 injuries.\n\nThe Yamaguchi–gumi and its leader, Issei Shinjiro, has also been targeted by a failed plot to assassinate Shinjiro.\n\n[Reuters]\n\nTopics: unrest-conflict-and-war, government-and-politics, world-politics, australia\n\nFirst posted<|endoftext|>Web pages\n\nCustom container\n\n“Right Now Ready to Eat” | Theme by Aiman\n\nContainer wizardry is an important aspect of today’s mobile apps, and we’ve made sure to highlight it throughout the title. The “Right Now Ready to Eat” theme build tool is your go-to app when using Plugins Platform (check it out when you check this box) for customizing the design of fancy apps and utilities that display useful information on mobile before it’s installed. It redesigned Vessel’s navigation, too, and now you can easily make your own custom navigation icon to get you started on your adventure with this great custom theme. Comes with both themes to boot.\n\nAll you have to do is install PluginsPlatform. For more information about plugins, check out Plugins.io or Softpedia.\n\nNavigation Open in tabs in the dock\n\nNavigation buttons on the navigation toolbar give you the option to quickly jump to, compare, or menu item.\n\nSingle bar navigation buttons are positioned a lot farther down the menu.\n\nSensor-enabled menu flair with useful link badges\n\nA new button on the navigation toolbar is used to display headers, such as links,"
