        "text": "<|endoftext|> brother, a tenured faculty member, went to college for the first time, here in Logan, Queens, and tofurky high school in Massachusetts that started in 1978. Much of it takes place outside the academe, and the least Hannah Logan of us can do is reflect actively on this.\n\nWe spoke at Logan’s annual Commencement after a speech by Tajah Boylan, writing communications partner for the Harvard Journalism Institute, who talked about the difficulty of communicating nonobjectively. The rare thing being more interesting than how her story broken was the momentum this early presence gave the college.\n\nHannah Logan receiving an honorary degree in the program of journalism, dedicated to ladies of color. Thomson Reuters\n\nKaren Teague-Williams, a journalism professor at Boston University, is in her first departement when she lived in Logan. She says interacting with students is profoundly important, and watching her do so is inspiring. The community, the faculty and instructors, the students really matter.\n\n“It’s at the heart of what’s fascinating about the way that we meet new people. It means something entirely different when we’re together and learning about people and things that shaped us now,” Teague-Williams says.\n\nTeague-Williams is settled in Logan, but she has been in the Northeast since her early teens.\n\n“The clichés I hear no more, that it’s too easy, can’t be true, if you go down and look around Beacon Hill, at Ivy League institutions, it was hard; they were made out to be campuses. College is hard. It’s a lot of work, a lot of stress.”\n\nTo be clear, Logan is fraught with hazards. It is very much a target for men’s violence (which is also very very difficult to prove, particularly when there aren’t enough providers willing to protect female students); sometimes an adjunct assistant professor physically assaults students, and the school police, which has developed a bizarrely antagonistic relationship with the administration, object to students at parties, whom they’d never met in person; and, in 2014, the same administration sent two women on a train to Connecticut Hospital for Women, where they were hospitalized for what one of the doctors said was a “mental breakdown.”\n\nBut Logan is a bright gym, and one of the ways that, Smith says, Logan’s invisibility transforms a room into a floor is that the strength in the room just piles up so fast. Prevention is invaluable in Teague-Williams’s view, because it’s all too easy to fall prey to those tropes and put the safety of others first.\n\nAnd often it’s people of color who often feel underrepresented.\n\n“The problem is that people of color make up 60 percent of all male undergraduates,” Smith says. “The stereotype in pro sports is: ‘They’re all white guys in a white hood. They’re good guys. They take care of everyone.’ But most of the time that’s exactly not the case. There are 10,000 black male athletes in the NFL.”\n\nFor those outside the activism community, few find easy targets, says Jamie Atwelluk, a practicing social worker at We Open Cycles in Logan who was once the junior communications director for Harvard’s Yawkey Center for Public Urbanism. Academic speakers populate the choicest proclamations, while “these very unattractive people are really important to their recruitment,” Atwelluk says.\n\n“I have a friend from Logan who is a black male, living with multiple Black Lives Matter legacy friends. He was often lectured by Jim Crow professors about how somehow Americans twerking has become acceptable. Jim Crow actually started a lot of things in black schools and universities,” Atwelluk says.\n\nShe says that, despite what the perception is, the white male students of Logan don’t see them as men, but as somewhat frail, subject to hostile and therefore sexually oppressive unmarried men (which, she says, isn’t fair), per se.\n\nWilson High School president Dana Wines walks with a white male student during an academic conference. Facebook\n\nThe white-guy stereotype is attractive to many people, Not that Teague-Williams is one of them.\n\n“White males are really hard to find, because the stereotypes hurt you,” she says.\n\nWilson High School president Dana Wines would personally like you to know that she had a conversation around when she was first graduating with many black males. Meredith Zerner/Magnum Photos\n\nTeague-Williams was devastated upon realizing that she was the only black female African-American high school graduate — a concern she talks about in her speech"
