        "text": "<|endoftext|> led by Lachlan Murdoch, is under investigation by the Senate health select committee over its links with the ambulance service.\n\nA US official described the use of Facebook, Twitter and other social media sites as a key service used by the private health sector, as \"compelling\".\n\nEarlier this month Australia's top doctor hospital group estimated that 15% of accidents occur through social media and text messaging.\n\nIt also reported more than 58,000 calls placed to its emergency service seeking treatment via Facebook in 2014-15, compared with just 144 million a year earlier.\n\nPicture: Elizabeth Dyson/Flickr\n\nTelecommunications giant Facebook believes it has an answer as it wags its finger at the health insurance industry and forces its users to access services which they may find off-putting:\n\n\"My typical social media strategy is to mostly get out and about on Thursday, having a drink, going to a movie, talking politics with friends, commenting on things, meeting with with others, and generally getting the message out to others around the world,\" product manager, Jake Spalding told Australia's ABC. \"That's not always involved in the way we do it on mainstream social media.\"\n\n\"I'm drawn to the intelligence on the internet that leads me to use Facebook,\" said Queensland-based hooker Chrissy Teigen. \"It was first a private Facebook feed, then it became a public one.\n\n\"And I think that makes it easy to sign up for sending updates from my friends.\"\n\nUnder Health Minister Peter Dutton, Facebook has banned the use of its US partner Apple (AAPL) as it tracks account behaviour and had sought a \"sting\" into mobile phone and internet encryption.<|endoftext|>Professional tennis player Mirko Cro Cop will spend 6 weeks in South Korea until July 16 without making a pitch on the grand tour.\n\nCro Cop, 51, will travel with Stephanie Seymour, manager of children’s services to Seoul from 3-11 July, who said that conversation is already probably just as important as opportunities to talk about future events in the US.\n\nAs well as staying away for legal reasons, Cro Cop will either face no further action from the authorities or do not even contest the accusations.\n\nGETTY At least one Russian woman says she has just been beaten by Australian shirkers\n\nCro Cop is the seventh successful player to hit out at the scrapping of the controversial 7,000kg tennis match schedule. Pro wrestling superstar Ric Flair had a series of incidents this week with the International Federation of the Fighters bottling up trouble in May; the WFDF is poised to announce a 28-man code for its schedule for next year on Sunday.\n\nGETTY The ICC have declared that it hasn't introduced a new Men's World Team until 2020\n\nCro Cop’s complaints did nothing to assuage fans who also lashed out after the picture of 19-year-old, Japanese Donald Young guiding his World No. 8’s full net appeared. The 21-year-old, a winner in the world and three-time Australian Open champion, was accused of pushing his backboard glass out of frame after an American referee convicted him in 2011 of scuffing the court records off him. Celebrity trainer Conor McGregor got particularly concerned after Young was caught on tape in 2014 faking injuries, spotted unhanding a Ben Brady rib-and-mortar.<|endoftext|>Germany's federal investigators are likely to accuse Qatar of providing material support to the Taliban militants fighting American forces in Afghanistan and the fighting further deteriorated last week when Al-Qaeda took the keys to Qatar's powerful emirate.\n\nAccording to Leaks:\n\nThe sources connected with both the U.S. government and the United Arab Emirates (UAE) warned Sunday that the Justice Ministry that oversees Azerbaijan in Western Europe that refused to sign a strategic zone agreement with it, wants the UAE to provide intelligence services and materials relevant to the ethnic groups fighting on behalf of the United States against the Taliban.\n\nThe source spoke on condition of anonymity because the matter is still under the purview of the Federal Prosecutor's Office in the second phase of its investigation into Qatari support to Afghan insurgents fighting American forces and \"the situation recently in Afghanistan.\"\n\nLike a reliable Yasminean organisation for the Taliban and al-Shabaab al-Qaida, Qatar was involved in partnering with Al-Qaeda in different parts of the country and was reportedly providing weapons and training to the group, as well as to other groups fighting against the United States.\n\nA Foreign Ministry source also told Leaks:\n\n\"Qatar made a pledge not to cooperate with decisions by the United States Government and supports their military actions in Afghanistan. Again it is important to remember that Qatar is enjoying tremendous economic and political benefits from Afghanistan.\"\n\nThe source indicated that Qatar and Turkey are being actively involved in breaking the back of Taliban rule and Al-Shabaab al-Qaida, which has"
