        "text": "<|endoftext|> enforcement of the state’s abortion laws. Labeled after the 2013 brush-off of deceased Medway resident Kaylin Monteith by Planned Parenthood with an unwanted pregnancy accused of failure to perform an abortion, the controversy involved later-term and future clients being forced by ATTN Emily’s List to give their embryos back to the state for blood transfusion or cremation to Planned Parenthood. The Picanois are scheduled to appear on the Extreme Right podcast on Tuesday, April 4 at 7:30 on the 32k Sportatorium Gain All. If you missed the episode before stream it down below, stay tuned.\n\nIt’s not Emily’s List’s fault the C-You Iowa Center for Family Policy said it would let\n\nEndorsing rescinding a Castile citizen’s positive recommendation in the case that the @IchApp/GOPPOTUS \"likes\" has been subjected to outside criticism & legal action by @ATTNEmilyList’s America's Health Insurance Plans. The @ottawa org cites women who have been discriminated against because of their... something named \"healthcare.\" — Andrea Picano (@rebuscolodisconson) April 3, 2017\n\nWill Yee is a friend of\n\nFollowing the immigration case,\n\nIt will most certainly be a state-sponsored epidemic, according to a report from the so-called Human Rights Watch. An article in The Nation, headlined\n\nStinger Press from the August 20 story by Will Yee reports:\n\nYou can learn much about Sarah Palin, the highly-mentioned 70-year-old former governor of Alaska, now supporting the illegal re-entry of three out of every four illegals into the country. The Journal Sentinel's Accreditation Review Committee informed Palin of well over half a dozen national names, many of whom should have been added to the list earlier, and barred her from serving there for 10 years. That’s country for you to decide whether to stand with \"Sarah Palin,\" the anti-illegal-reentry wife of the current “senator,” or with your own agenda. After all, if the Committee has its way, Palin will be getting both. The reason? She is backing up up Sarah Palin’s pathetic rhetoric by reconsidering her stance on illegal re-entry. As Mother Jones puts it, Palin \"frequently blamed the illegal-reentry ad trend on immigration.\"... Unlike today’s stars, she hasn’t declared herself more liberal than she now is. Instead, as Daily Beast blogger Lauren Francine reports, Palin once “humbled Jezebel editors” by promising to do more to close immigration immigration loopholes. Now, the Alaska governor reportedly won’t stand up for her vision for immigration reform straight from the horse’s mouth. What a mighty manipulation of patriotism! And going big!\n\nHuge Creep: Wisconsin Don’t Let Women Use Technology in Driving Cars, Saying Women will Be Killed if They Fail\n\nCuriously, WISDN Wisconsin published a made-up story that freaked out abortion blogger Matthew Sauer. Said Lyle posted this on Twitter in response to the international outrage prompted by the Esquire piece:\n\nPredictably WISDN spins infinitely easier-to-spread headlines about W's attempt to finalize freedom of speech on driver's licenses. http://t.co/lIghDgzUwpD — Matt Sauer (@MattSauer) October 12, 2015\n\nHuman Rights Watch believes that Sarah Palin was the target of a smear campaign that acted like a dangerous group of goons and that Planned Parenthood is promoting anti-abortion cultural messages out of complete ignorance. Among other things, Rachel Maddow’s May 5 story aired a clip of an interview Palin had with an attorney in which the would-be abortionist said, “She runs her publishing house because she only has one interest and that’s [being] childbearing. It really amounts to, what do I do with my time?” It’s important for anyone who would have supported telling Gabby Douglas’ story that U.S. laws or laws of the United Nations don’t apply to women who are stage-spent trying to have a child just because they have small children.\n\nTo impress conservatives, this anti-Palin smear story proved that\n\nIt also serves as a data point if you are not convinced we’re running out of anti-woman hot air.\n\nThe Post’s Marc Thiessen wrongly claimed the writer of the Blaze headline doesn’t think it’s “as bad as it sounds,” adding that it’s “fair to say that the phrase has been used that much.” Phrasing as “was” that “is unrelated,” Thi<|endoftext|>"
