        "sourceId": 529,
        "text": "<|endoftext|> terribly valuable commodity. Sarah Freix\n\n\"With them coming in, we saw their services more meaningful in all cultures and assets they could throw [to raiders],\" Gadschewitz said, adding that the entire operation's hash value was under $50,000.\n\n\"It was considerable damage, staggering. [They were] incredibly skilled, but it only happened once.\"\n\nAccording to British police and military data, the drugs and crystal were found in nine different containers, indicating over $100,000 was stolen out of the North African country through the shipment mechanism.\n\nThe United States was reported to have exported 120 kilograms of crystal meth in 2012.<|endoftext|>Georgia Tech junior Ian Stone cracked the Top25 ranking this coming weekend, with his dynamic offensive line getting a powerful push in the MAC title game against Ohio. Stone also releases the Tulsa Technical Arts to the world as the Hogs wrap up the NIT schedule at Big Baller Brand Stadium in Columbus.\n\nStone was a standout for the Aggies in 2017 and was a late enrollee of the Yahoo! Sports 200 at black's first-ever Bobby Dodd Stadium. From there on out, Stone flashed his enormous potential and would be a dominant force on the offensive line if he could carve out a little more than a year at the FBS level intact.\n\nStone looks like someone who thrives in the vacuum of the spotlight; however, his most noticeable aspect as a player was his work ethic. Throughout the evaluation process at Tech, Stone expressed a desire to become more of a vocal leader and served as a source of pressure to gain recognition for his talented and steady demeanor.\n\nStone's teacher, Claude Ellis, would love nothing more than for married women in his class to drive an electric car. He managed to obtain what most FBS quarterbacks would shun – however highly necessary to success at the next level – by leading by example. With this in mind, Stone was forced to be an icon for his high school program all over again.\n\nElliott finally learned Stone's message of being a leader by rushing for 1,069 yards and 13 touchdowns last season. This season, Stone is having a fantastic season on the offensive line, completing 72.1 percent of his falls while posting a passer rating of 80.4. A perfect 100-point season is one he is likely to have for the remainder of his playing career.\n\nIt is the ultimate reward for a prospect in the nation’s third-largest state who considers himself a graduate of Tech and now expects to play elsewhere after a successful senior season. Take this as a personal acknowledgement of all that the Bulldogs have accomplished over the past 17 years as one of the nation’s best programs, and the way he has matured into a leader.\n\nIan Stone is now the most interesting player in America during the college football season. His candidacy could define the report card for the nation's best FBS offensive line entering the Big 12 Championship Game for the 2012 season.\n\nAlabama Ala Hibberd Track and Field Champion\n\nShaun Hibberd\n\nTober City, CA -\n\nApril 25, 2017Shaun HibberdBefore his junior season at Alabama, Oklahoma State football coach Butch Jones stressed his desire to recruit players of potential in the state with plans to bring in big names in Alabama and Oklahoma State. Several notable Hokies such as cornerback Hilliard Scott, safety Tosin Robinson, offensive linemen Trey Ingram and Darren 'Tiger' Haddadi, and running back Chrissie McKenzie could all end up taking advantage of a recruiting explosion happening in the region. Many recruiting experts felt the Tide's fast growth was a prime reason the program benefitted so much from Jones' leadership at the school prior to O-line coach Mike Riley leaving in 2011. With Jones believed to be world class at his high school, Hibberd is one of the few potential candidates in the Top 25.\n\nHibberd is a two-time CCU all-state sophomore defensive tackle with over a 300-yard, 14-sack, and eight-assist season, a combined 17.5 tackles for loss, 4.0 sacks, and eight interceptions. With that pedigree, there is only a slight question as to whether or not he will succeed in the NFL. However, with a pair of prominent playoff performances in three seasons as a senior, I am convinced he will.\n\nAlabama Crimson Tide AAHS 2010 Singleton\n\nJamison Yates-Smith\n\nOle Miss Rebels\n\nRachel Newton\n\nChez Urena-Peters\n\nJaelen Valdes-Davis\n\nPaxton Monkins-Travis Reynolds\n\nSam McBride\n\nDixie Ware\n\nKent Anthony Watts\n\nGeorgia Tech Yellow Jackets AAHS 2009 Afoxee\n\nNovember 2, 2017Sam Houston State PC\n\nGeorgia Tech PC Pleased with the all-around improvement that freshman Taylor Budun,\" hopes and leadership\". -Gregor McGu"
