        "text": "<|endoftext|> petition.\n\nThe register-order demands include unlimited contact stories by race and related individuals that must be posted by 2020 or there will be an appeal of the NICS data. The applicant also has to submit an amended affidavit, and any corrective “weeks” decreed upon such decisions would fail to satisfy the process standards.\n\nState Sen. Scott Fitzgerald, D-Bergen, the incumbent Republican and anti-voting board chairman, backed the request.\n\n“If I were selected to run for the Senate, I would work very closely with the voter ID project to protect the safety and security of American citizens,” Fitzgerald wrote in a statement. “These reforms are designed to ensure the integrity of the voting process and their end is to strengthen the voting rights of all New Jersey voters.”<|endoftext|>A Maine nonprofit has stepped in to help the homeless qualify for free aid, asking people to travel by air to go into Portland — no matter where they are and physically.\n\nThe Maine Community Network's Southwest Portland Digital Programs says it has found 13 air fares to get people to New Orleans, San Francisco, or Miami, Miami Beach, Florida. Albamians from all 50 states and from all 50 Hispanic and African-American communities can go to Portland.\n\n\"It's not that we're just taking people through, it's that we're doing something about the homeless,\" said Rebecca Currill, lead of Southeast Maine Community Network.\n\nCurrill points to the Penobscot River rescue center where more than 2,000 workers are trying make a difference. She says the center will be launching a fellowship program to help the first 300 Oregonians on a life change by offering cancer oil, diabetes medication free of charge.\n\n\"In many parts of state government people are afraid to take the phones away,\" Currill said.\n\nFive bucks gives 20 cents a mile to Todd Lopez along the Sea Creek Parkway, but he's spending his $125 monthly income on life support. For Valerie Lopez, the AA program isn't enough. She didn't come to Portland to find help, she needed to go the other way, back to Mexico.\n\n\"Flowing to the bottom of the ocean. If I was to die on the beach in-between there would be maybe a game catch, a treat, a movie I would be thankful just knowing, just knowing how much I owe them, and just being grateful and just grateful, thank you very much to make it happen,\" said Lopez.\n\nThe group is hoping to use the money to pay off bathtub leaks or tampons, they also have medicine which they are applying for a trust to get money to buy a nesting spot. All donations go directly to the Bay Area Citizens' Immigrant Fund, which has sent the money to the Portland Emergency Relief Fund and still remains optimistic that Jordan Lopez's medical bills will soon be paid.<|endoftext|>President Trump said on Monday that he’ll likely allow Puerto Rico — twice the size of California and one of the hardest-hit states by Hurricane Maria — to remain in the hurricane-ravaged island until emergency repairs can be made and federal help can be provided.\n\nTrump spoke by phone with First Lady Melania Trump on Monday to express his desire for Puerto Rico to remain on its current State of Emergency and revive federal disaster relief spending.\n\nTrump is also urging aid donations again to be distributed the same way the state’s emergency authorities have for the past year.\n\nADVERTISEMENT\n\n“The United States Government will work with Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, and all relevant states to aid the people of Puerto Rico,” the White House said in a statement on Monday.\n\n“The President stressed to the Secretary of the U.S. Virgin Islands why Puerto Rico needs assistance, what the responsibility of rebuilding should be, and the Department’s continued commitment to restoring life to the island.”\n\nPuerto Rico has been fighting to pay its debt for nearly two-and-a half years, with the help of two fifth-party presidential administration candidates.\n\nThe U.S. territory faces a $74 billion debt binge that the government says threatens the island’s credit rating. After weeks of heavy rains and ongoing flooding, the island has begun to rely heavily on below-construction river tunnels to transport food, fuel and other essentials.<|endoftext|>Plutonium-238 (P-238) seeped into your water bucket near you last week, even though firefighters had been put at risk by its dangerous overheating risk in an underground power plant. Hold on that thought. Strangely enough, it’s still far from being released in the soil.\n\nThe much-anticipated decrease into power with nuclear power is more of a challenge: cells are more volatile and leak out quickly (see diagrams above), so there’s a whole field of non-return-precedent research and tests"
