        "sourceId": 943,
        "text": "<|endoftext|> is ready to dive into marketing these games completely!\" JellyRoll says. Plus, the studio has a sequel working on the PLAY engine – a big thing if games are never released with JellyRoll's $1 million commitment at this point.<|endoftext|>Richard Armitage: 'A Seinfeldian In Maverick' - Question Meets Results\n\nEnlarge this image toggle caption RatPac Entertainment RatPac Entertainment\n\nRichard Armitage is known for his prolific tilts on pop stars like Corinne Bailey Rae, Alan McGee and Paris Hilton, including three Oscars — both of which he won — in which he wasn't on the stage.\n\nThe actor, 75, originated the accent 'Hee hee heh' and 'GA, ls, heh' that actor and actor-mentor Mark Wahlberg would call \"Hee balesale.\" It was also his signature.\n\nJordan Lillibridge on Sirius XM and his younger brother, Brian Lillibridge, talk to Armitage about it all:\n\nInterview Highlights\n\nOn making the list\n\n\"There's a six-month period — that's four to five months — for us to come up with the idea and decide what we want to do. \"First of all, I look at a track record of where I want to go and where I would like to go in the theater. And second, I look at what the business, what the audience wants, the economy and the environment. And I think that once we are culled enough — and kids are coming up as kids throughout all the categories — we figure out what the demographic for the movie world is. Well, it's 13- to 17-year-olds. We will find ways that they can make us a successful dollar and they will be able to convert their birthday money into the movies, depending on what they have in the bank.\"\n\nOn how his father Dennis Feltgen got into film\n\n\"He did film school and became an assistant editor at SKA Operators, the worldwide distributor of false teeth. And as I recall, Hugh was a fan of that, and he was very enthusiast about advertising. He said, 'Are you sure you're going to start a documentary business in another 30 years?' And he was an angel. He and the producer and I went out and shot there at the age of 27. We eventually moved there to South Africa so we got better control over our schedule. \" [It was] a private operation for Dennis. It was 1955, and we all had a New Years' Eve at his house and then we moved in as an associate so we could do a full-time movie industry there.\"\n\nOn how he helped Kenny Loggins get on the red carpet\n\n\"We came in with nothing, because Kenny knew what we wanted to do. And he said, 'Keep going with'sixty seconds or sixty seconds and then take all of \"sixty seconds and all that.' And I said, 'Hey, go ahead, but 'First.'\" [And he said, 'A lot of people want to do TV, but people like Mr. Loggins, he'll be done up really quick.' And it was an amazing experience. It's a wonderful time to be alive. We wound up talking to lots of people. He even heard about it on \"Jeopardy!\" We found out that Kenny Loggins was 31 years old when we did the movie. And he told us, 'I'm going to be in the movie, and I'm going to do a 30-second appearance. I'm going to go get a drink. We're going to be inside.' He was two-years-old.\"\n\nOn what kind of career advice he would have\n\n\"Well, the actors have all had success. I've been involved. I've been a recording artist. I've had a business. I have successful products and I bring a much greater listening public to the movies. As soon as I began really taking care of my personal life, I was able to be successful. I knew what I was doing was the right thing, and I was truly able to control my own life.\"\n\nOn his love of toys: 'I don't think we make any, like, pay-grade 'em up' efforts.'\n\n\"I just think that's a wonderful thing. It is a tremendous art form. It's true. But for every 'Sesame Street' adaptation, you end up with one where somebody gets hurt.\"\n\nHe describes digital media as \"essentially an inferior form of film distribution\" because it is \"written on the cutting edge of technology and profoundly nanotargetable.\"\n\nHe credits and awards sauce to a brighter and more successful playbook.\n\nOh, and once again, he left \"Sesame Street\" for \"NSYNC, the network that's the best in the land.\"<|endoftext|>A video of the fatal shooting of Broadway resident Veronica"
