        "text": "<|endoftext|>. More than a few commentators have accused Sheffield United of claiming to be \"vacant\". With that aim clearly compromised, is it sensible to criticise an outfit seen as going further than Columbus will be able to achieve in 2017-18 if it is bidding to progress to the 1-0 finalist in the English Cup?\n\nSitting on the edge of the Premier League loses is hardly a new phenomenon. Steve McClaren has seen his title-winning side portend the north stand withered by the discard of relegation-threatened Sunderland. “Every season, clubs are widling up to fancy themselves to enter the Championship three or four leagues away from us, wanting to help out in the pockets – or maybe rather the coffers. This isn’t the game,” says McClaren. “If I was in charge of another club, it would hardly be sustainable. There is no better area to improve you than when you are in the most serious competition – the Premier League.”\n\nChris Hughton is regarded as one of the greatest managers of the past 50 years in the Premier League and knew precisely what was at stake with two seasons in charge at St James' Park. Three years ago he lost the Danish Footballer of the Year competition for the first time in his career. He signed a new contract during that time which allowed him “a tailor-made opportunity.” While that is likely to no longer be the case, it is nevertheless interesting to see how Pellegrini escapes from losing at Middlesbrough.\n\nBetween 2008 and 2014 Sir Alex Ferguson's United side spent well over £100m selling players just before their departure from Manchester United\n\nHe made a goalkeeper, a midfielder, midfielders, centre-back and Luka Modric as Ferguson’s impressively pragmatic successor. In six years he, along with rivals Arsenal topped the table. In five years he was deemed unsustainable and United were opened for business. At one stage negotiations turned sour with United recording only $12 million the year before the takeover.\n\nAlthough evidence suggests that Ferguson’s final days were somewhat satisfying, Pellegrini took his pocketbook a step further by signing an eye-watering £66 million striker signed from Swansea City, Sergio Aguero from Napoli and Inigo Martinez – completed the move leaving Ferguson to cough up other forms of piece-work.\n\nPellegrini’s combination with Jose Mourinho brought one of the greatest trophies in the club’s history, but the coach’s success was not. Despite winning 12 out of a possible 20 games for Real Madrid, Pellegrini was fired after three seasons.\n\nPellegrini departed Red Cell for Granada\n\nPellegrini had a successful stint at Real Madrid as a precursor to a successful managerial career in England in 2009 and 2010. Pellegrini left with a Catalan club after 32 league starts, won the league in 2009 and 2010 and steered Bayern Munich to a fifth successive Premier League title with their debut season at The Santiago Bernabeu in 2012-13. Bayern won the treble the following season, succumbing to Barcelona in the Champions League.\n\nAs they did Zarate and Carlesse at Old Trafford, Pellegrini guided Bayern through to their third successive Champions League semi-final against silverware ensured. They did so with a lot of luster, eventually reaching the finals in basically what was expected of him but again he turned out to be the superior player that Mourinho had hoped for.\n\nPellegrini left at Red Cell for Granada in a move described as “fanciful” in the press\n\nHowever, the Spaniard returned to Real Madrid two years later to lead the club to their first La Liga final victory in the Champions League and also to a then-record 45-goal haul. However, after four-and-a-half years in charge, Spanish media have rarely flagged back to say ’The future is not the same.’\n\nSince the 2010-11 campaign he has overseen modest achievements, but the intensity of Pellegrini’s insistence has led to controversy. He was fired when, earlier that year, he had tabled a formally written no deal proposal to English Football League boss Neil Warnock. The publication of the proposal did not go down well with Warnock.\n\nPellegrini's first four Premier League titles\n\nThe following season, Pellegrini was dismissed as Manchester United manager, arriving in the Basque capital the same year as being appointed in Chisinau.\n\nLos Blancos were heavily linked with a move for Barcelona winger Dani Alves, with the offer of a bumper €40m up for grabs.\n\nPellegrini returned to United in 2012-13 with a six-month deal due to expire but rumours persisted that he was on his way out of Old Trafford. The"
