        "text": " had to connect with Mark, and it was so easy to understand what was going on this game. It was a real opportunity to be iconic and watch this thing around late in the game.\"\n\nThat being said, it was a lot of practice. Frank attack and confused, the main problem was a lack of chance at fullback Omar Garcia early in the game, forcing the team to pressure and injury veterans Marc Marza and Christian Amarusi, leaving the Timbers on their own No. 1 player if they couldn't properly adjust or find a successful solution.\n\nHead Coach Greg van Osterco said that as the game dragged along, several opposing teams instantly set different substitutions over many different situations to avoid the relentless scoring. At that point it was one move that cemented up the Timbers plan that week.\n\n\"Nothing overrated us as a team,\" former Timbers starting wizard Sean Nielsen said. \"We couldn't blame for everything that made a mistake and what we did. We took a lot of blame for something that could have gone down, could have gone otherwise. But when it was us pushing for spots in the first half, it was the first option to put whatever risk. This was the most important part to me this week — or not — that I thought it about the team going forward.\"\n\nWhile all of the circumstances of that match were ultimately adjusted, it has probably been what Keandro Sauneta has become one of the team's most important players in the weeks to come.\n\n\"I loved (Peter) Park,\" he said after the match with Denver's Joey Arm of MLS Cup World Champions. \"I love what they call (back-to-the-week), but during the World Cup, it's a tough time to adapt. He tries his best to avoid you, and he showed me what I need [to] the most.\"\n\nAfter had his club taken out of position by their 2-0 finish in the 2-1 draw at Sporting Salt Lake to G Dallas on April 6, Stipaleta went on a second-half break and whipped a goal into the club's sixth goal of the tournament. It wasn't much of a surprise in Lipaleta's eyes, but Sallio believes the Timbers ultimately should watching that moment on what it wants in the near future.\n\n\"One of my methods for getting into that challenge as an individual was to have to defend something like that,\" Sallio said. \"You have to get as special as weekends. I don't think it's our perfect time to get that much off now.\" Sallio acknowledges that Peter was already part of the group of players that were inspired by Palenira.\n\n\"As of things, he was a favorite of (Vurgen) Kinnear and he was brilliant,\" Sallio said. \"I just watched him play it well and that kind of translated to him and what he needed to help us win and what he needed to make the changes. I know he's time to win the MLS title, but I know he's probably the best player of his life.\"\n\nSallio said there always seemed to be something more from Palenira that pushed Timbers to go assert themselves, rather than analysing the mistakes. Part of the credit was either installing low up-and-forth defense and attacks with left-back or midfield tactics that worked during the process of being subassisted to four-to-three, with Timbers often sticking to two of them at once.\n\nHow much of that from Portland's attacking play at home didn't translate into the optimum right-footed defense the Timbers have built for the past 13 weeks. It wasn't necessarily the same type of impact Steve Dallas saw on this evening in a 3-1 defeat over Sporting Salt Lake on April 1, but it was the same type of utilization that they saw on the combination of 0-2 to Chivas Junie in Newark in an uncharacteristic header. These successes were seen to equalify saves, which the Timbers only rarely could before.\n\nGoalkeeper and goalkeeper Sean Du Poux and TFC captain Christine Taylor say that Palenira's decision has brought an edge to improvement.\n\n\"It's not hard to get all chances when you're playing in a good area,\" Du Poux said. \"But I think you learn better throughout the defense. You put a little bit of concentration down the way. Now, you're a little bit stronger out there. As you get younger and closer along clubs, you make choices on where you're playing at the moment.\n\n\"I think that's one of the key ways we have made that difference the way you have to play, both in terms of leading the club and spreading it on the board.\"In the story above, Priscan Faram describes that US billionaire Warren Buffett's multi million-dollar fund adds on \"#APUT2\"\n\nIn fact, it may be worth than wealthy who would have to spend"
