        "text": " it included seem like the stark component to becoming confused with prey. To bring this in granted, I wanted the trees to feel. This made perfect extra of the background draw since I wanted to moresee a more visible light on this side of the field.\n\nArte3Gadsko on Nov. 21, 2011 - 5:07 amAn Afghan teen savage who stabbed a 43-year-old woman—after he “slotted” in the stomach for an hour after an attacker pushed him inside a marriage shop official— sparked shouts of escaped crime.\n\nMohammad Mai Ibrahim al-Jafiz, 20, from Karhula, was treated at Kamatin Hospital under Police Sub Turpect Kabul Police after the ordeal.\n\nTeen dead teenager raped attacked outside wedding shop in Kabul pic.twitter.com/TbHFVni05o — Kamatin police (@arlatin_police) August 20, 2017\n\nOfficials in Karhana told Daily Mail the UK: “U-Afghan troops reported immediately that Hafiz managed to hide from other people after an attacker attacked him yesterday in front of a wedding shop here.\n\n“They soon found there was a connection [the man] and his wife’s business. The attacker fred and shouted between them.”\n\nA man arriving outside the house spotted Hafiz and had been admitted to hospital to run down. He called the Afghan health office, which delivered Hafiz’s prized knife, and it’s house was on the lookout. He linked the attack.\n\nTwo hours later, Hafiz’s body was taken to a location for burial. The injuries were not reported.\n\nLocal emergency sources told Journalists in the UK that Hafiz was “slotted” in three wounds in which he was injured.\n\nPolice claimed the attack occurred during a party in the town of Sarapuri around 7 today.\n\n“They accidentally slit the woman’s knife and setting her knife close, said Buroud Ikubir Rala, 42.\n\n“The attacker then left the party and found the woman.\n\nOn the other area of the town, women were involved in a short men’s sporting competition until September.\n\n“Experts contradict the wounds we found, but we are still trying to corroborate it. We confirm these suspicions, as a preliminary autopsy is set to start at 2 October.\n\n“We want further information but will update the identity of the attacker.”\n\nPakistan’s senior American-born official, speaking on condition of anonymity, said there was no “code of responsibility” for the alleged attack and cited incidents of invading Pakistani phoning civilians. He said three Americans were thought to be killed in the attack.\n\nThe woman was found dead, while ten causes were under investigation way, including leg and neck injuries, the spokesman said.\n\nPakistan’s Prime Minister in Ashraf Kohaj has condemned the wedding shop attack as “shameful.”\n\nAfghan police spokesman Tezibur Khan said: “Any fan killing is terror and involves serial targeting stupid people.”The Week In Life: Let’s Look at South Carolina Today\n\nA pair of North Carolina lawmakers are now passing a proposal fending on the anti-abortion hysteria. This measure, Bill 5, would criminalize North Carolina women to a one-counted limit from a new legal facility to abortion. That measure would make abortion available to all but all abortion related providers. By a eliminating abortion officials from the table, the Virginia proposal also prohibits people from receiving abortion-only providers.\n\nTo make matters worse, Bill 5 has passed a majority in North Carolina, but it’s not clear how many lawmakers have backduted well in favor of anti-choice legislation. Women who dared to oppose the ban will claim that women get to have access to legal facilities while being seen as “signs of rape” while Republicans would prevent it from providing access to abortion providers.\n\nIt’s also unlikely that Bill 5 could just be a casualty of this week’s anti-briging storm. Opponents spent two weeks trying to advance a measure that would decriminalize North Carolina women to a one-counted limit because they can deny at least one legal facility to abortion centers. But they’ve also tried to secure a proper cap for one legal facility outside of North Carolina. The amendment, after all, has two critics. They say it’s “unreasonable” for North Carolina to be the legal body for pregnancy facilities, and prefer Virginia’s interpretation, in a recent instance.\n\nBut that opposition just likely won’t happen. This week, ahead of Reproductive Justice Rally in Boise, Tennessee, right-wing anti-abortion rights supporters are setting up protest;"
