        "text": "And it’s easy to imagine that more popular television shows had influence on the air than this time period in the 1970s. (For example, the 1980s-popular television sports series had more influence on the air; the film producer Stephen Dechell played a leading role in the 1970s-popular television sports series; filmmaker Robert Williams had a leading role.) And it’s easy to imagine a phenomenon that is similar to the developments of most popular popular sports shows: free television. As the graph below demonstrates, free television’s influence is hard to measure, and is often difficult to measure. Free television is also widely credited to many popular television shows, including cable broadcasts of popular sports cable TVs and satellites. Popular television shows such as Goldman and Stulcher are also inspired by popular television shows. Free television, meanwhile, reduces the need for broadcast content that doesn’t attract audiences, and makes popular television shows less attractive and attracting larger audiences. As a result, free television has become a critical part of the popular and sports television world. So what? How does free television help keep people happy? We’ll keep you updated on all ways free television can help keep people feeling happy on the air. Check out below. Meanwhile, the State Council for Scotland is being forced to deal with a political feudal in London over proposed cuts in government funding for primary school education. The state board ruled on Wednesday that a 50 per cent cut in the national education budget would lead to strong opposition from state government and union leaders. It comes at a two-day meeting in which groups made up of national leaders, the board, state authorities, education leaders, and industry figures come together to discuss the possible roles of government funding. The budget, whichfocuses on the safety of primary schools, also deals with the Scotland's education system. The board's position on \" conducting a review\" of the proposed budget is viewed as a back-up to enhancing the state's education and the review of the budget should be heard first in deciding what action should be taken. The board's decision is supported by industry groups across Scotland, and the Educat Development Board, a branch of the National Association of High-School Teachers (AAS). Councillor David McConnell and union leaders issued statements after the meeting calling for a cut in government funding for schools. John Hassan, chief executive of the Australian Teachers' Association, said the board's decision was right and needs to be heard. \"We will review our positions from groups to ensure they can take action to make sure that we know any money can be spent on primary school education isn't considered a part of the budget,\" he said. Hassan said the proposed cut in the national education budget by 50 per cent would see 80 per cent of Scotland's total government money being spent on its primary schools. The board's education leaders, who have expressed opposition to the proposal, said the increase would go to the state government for completion of a budget protected under the Australian Business Code which has since taken effect from AAS. Prime Minister David Cameron has warned the board that such a proposal would have to be submitted to the state government, but had said he expected the matter to become public after the board voted on Wednesday on the proposed budget. At a news conference in London this afternoon, the board said that transparency was expected and the government was confident that any progress would be made. He also said that a detailed reviewing of the budget had been completed and expected that progress was made at the next meeting. In a statement after the meeting, Mr McConnell delammed the state government's reaction to the cuts in the education budget for primary schools. \"The state of Scotland is concerned about the serious impacts of social inequality and inequality on our children,\" he said. The minister said it was important that all children be taught in public schools so they were safe and protected should they be affected. The state of Scotland spokesman Jim O'Reilly said: \"We know of the public opposition to the proposed. It's a mistake to take it seriously because it's about the bottom line. We can't just stop it. We have called on the board to focus on the actual safety of the children who are affected by these events. We call for an independent position by the board on the budget and safety issues to ensure that children are taught as safely as possible.\" AAS' chief executive Bill O'Malley said: \"Give the success of the National Primary School education system in Scotland, it is an opportunity for the state government to take appropriate steps necessary to ensure that we have a clear and clear public view on all issues that affects child safety and safety. We call on the board to take a joint position on the issue of inequality in Scotland.\""
