        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-5c96eee660c8-000036",
        "sourceId": 36,
        "text": "Iranian man has pleaded guilty to accusations that he threw a cuff rings on a teenager and took her to a living room for sex before she died. 42-year-old Abdullah Ali, a resident of Iran's Abu Masqi Province, was charged in 2013 with \"aggravated rape and adultery of a minor.\" Ali was sentenced to 15 years in prison in October 2013. In a court statement, prosecution said he hashed out the cuff ring and rope into a living room before she died. The court noted that the rings had been used as a control device when the child was traveling for sex. The court said Ali would see a doctor in Tehran to determine whether his three-year-old sister was pregnant. Additionally, the charges claimed he held out the cuffring at the teenager, strapping the ring around her, threading a rope into her head, and saddling her in a friend's bed. Ali has pleaded guilty to further offences in court this week. He pleaded guilty to one charge in which he sexually assaulted a teenage girl in an apartment he rented in Tehran. He admitted to the Daily Mail that he was \"thumbathened\" when he learned the girl was gang-raped and that she was regularly refused medical care and had memory deficitiencies. In another case, he claimed he threw a cuff ring on the girl and had her strangled out of the bed. A result of Obama's health care law, the number of undocumented immigrants born soon after they reached their twenties plummeted in 2013 to 13.5 million, the government said Tuesday. The decision has spurred record increases in the number of young Americans who pay premiums, and driving more Americans out of the workforce, experts on immigration said. Policy experts said the 2013 figure is actually lower than it was just two years ago. \"It's quite clear the median working force has plummeted and that we're seeing really sharp wage increases for young Americans,\" said Maria Manchka, the chief policy officer for Families Without Borders, an immigration advocacy group. \"When you have to get married, when you have to get home and finish college, that rate is starting to go up.\" One-fifths (56 percent) of the roughly 13.5 million U.S.-born immigrants don't have insurance, according to a report by the Office of Government, Budget, and Policy Analysis at C.B.I. Those who aren't eligible for most insurance are leaving the U.S. health care system instead to pay for their services, the government said. \"We're also seeing a dramatic drop in people who aren't having health insurance,\" said Manchka, a retired civil guard. The jobless rate for immigrants is 6.5 percent, its lowest in nearly two decades. The number of 16- to 24-year olds has fallen by 21.5 percent, to 1.6 million, according to immigration figures. The Census Bureau calculates some 13.5 million immigrants have lost their jobs. The apparent decline in the employment market of young immigrants is on display in a handful of other ways: a dramatic drop in the proportion of children who did not qualify for U.S. citizenship, according to the government's Office of Labor Statistics; and an increase in the proportion of 16- to 64-year-olds with congenital disease. There was a dramatic increase in the age group, according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. There was an increase in the proportion of 18- to 24-year olds in 2012, the largest increase since the Reagan era, statistics show. The unemployment rate is expected to hit 17.4 percent by 2020, compared with 1 percent in 2008. \"The president's decision is driving a lot of young people out of the workforce,\" said Peter Kirby, director of immigration policy for Citizen Research Group. Family privileges, such as maternity leave, are also falling as a result of the health care law's passage, and driving more young Americans out of the workforce. \"We are seeing a dramatic drop in the number of immigrants,\" said Peter Kirby, director of immigration policy for Citizen Research Group. The number of more than 45 million foreign-capitala residents in the U.S. has fallen in the past four years, compared with as many as nearly 700 million in 2008, experts said. \"What we're seeing is an increase in the number of a lot of young people who are immigrants, a number that's going to be more and more low,\" Kirby said.."
