        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-5c96eee660c8-000369",
        "sourceId": 369,
        "text": "CHICAGO (Reuters) - America’s top U.S. diplomat on Wednesday denied North Korean nuclear threats while accusing the United States of launching short-range missile tests against South Korea. U.S. Park’s spokesman, Tim Mantarget, said U.S. analysis by the Senate Joint Intelligence Committee in Washington had shown the North had a “significant risk” against South Korea, particularly in preparing a ballistic missile. “We Haven’t done comprehensive analysis before, but it appears there was still a significant threat,” Mantarget said, as quoted by the Pyongyang News Agency. U.S. and South Korean officials were reaffirming they had accelerated a missile launch against South Korea, adding that a missile test was planned at South Korea’s border on June 10. “South Korea is working closely with the North Korean government,” U.S. officials said in a statement, as quoted by Kim Hinzong, the North’s deputy chief of staff. South Korea does not have a close diplomatic relationship with U.S. officials, Yung Park, the director of U.S.-South Korean relations told Reuters. Park’s comments come just days after U.S. Assistant Secretary General Kim Jong-un met South Korean diplomats at a U.S. facility in South Korea. South Korean officials have accused the North of launching short-range missile tests against South Korea, though it remained unclear if North Korea had any involvement in carrying out these tests. South Korea and North Korea began conducting short-range missile tests in mid-March, which have been described as an U.S. war-fight. The two reportedly blamed North Korea for using ballistic missile in a hostility on the Korean Peninsula. More and more people are leaving the labor force, and more people are looking for their jobs. At roughly the same time as the economy in recession worsens, more and more people are leaving the labor force, according to Trijo Ball, a professor of economy and education policy at King’s Rainbow University. “In a country with such a massive population in recession, we don’t know how we will get people out of the workforce,” Ball said in an interview published Friday by NBC News. “There’s a much greater likelihood than previously thought” that people will stay in the labor force within the next decade or two, he said. Scroll down for video More and more people are leaving the labor force, said Trijo Ball, assistant professor of economy and education at King’s Rainbow University. America has around 10 million young people in the workforce, he said, surpassing the national youth population of 500 million. “That’s going to change,” Ball said. “We are seeing a growing number of young people stay in the labor force, and more people are searching for their jobs. But at roughly the same time, the share of young people remaining out of the workforce is growing.” “We’re seeing the recent drop in our population very quickly,” Ball said, adding that the number of young people who stay in the labor force hasn’t significantly changed. “We’ve seen significant increases in our population over the last decade or two,” he said. Ball said companies in manufacturing or other IT industries are off hiring more young workers than was previously thought. But they have so far tapped a wide range of jobs, including manufacturing, information technologies, and software manufacturing. “What hasn’t changed much is the proportion of people who are finding work — that’s been going up year over year,” he said. As America’s economic recovery worsens, more and more people are looking for work, and more people are finding work, and more people are reconsidering their career path, Ball said. Companies in manufacturing or other IT industries are off hiring young workers, and are actually starting to move from full-time positions to part-time positions. But Ball said that employers often send young people to laboring places that they visit to find work. “If you even look at IT industries itself, there’s a growing proportion who are finding work,” Ball said. “So we don’t know how it will be if we don’t have a population in recession.” As the economy in recession worsens, more and more young people are looking for a job, and more people are looking for work, and more people are reconsidering where they’re going to find a career, he added. “There is a thickew of young people who are thinking of taking a position, going to a hospital, going to a college, looking for work,” Ball said. “A lot of people are thinking there is nothing between going to a college and looking for work.” Nation: jobs, jobs."
