        "text": "MARANA: Well, it's true that I've been called a striker at work. But they can change your wages when you're on paid leave. You know, and you've heard about this by now, when you're on welfare as a worker, you are making 50 percent. And they can change your wages, you know, under those circumstances. They can't do that anymore and they have to sign a contract -- AMY GOODMAN: Here's the truth. Four or five years later when you're on paid leave, they can change your wages -- you're making $200,000 a year. You know when you're on welfare as a worker they can change your wages -- MARANA: No, I've never actually signed a contract at all. CHARKMAN: Well, you know, here's the truth. You know when you're on paid leave, you're making $200,000 wages. So you're not paying any tax. So you're not paying any tax. You're not paying any tax. You know when you're on paid leave, you're making 50 percent. So you're not paying any tax. When you're on welfare, you're making 50 percent. You're making $16,000 a year. You know four or five years after I was on a paid leave, as a worker, I applied for a welfare or Social Security contract -- AMY GOODMAN: And I have a right to walk -- MARANA: Absolutely. I have a right to walk under those circumstances. But they will not allow me to walk anymore. I'm only making $16,000 a year. You know four or five years ago, when I was on welfare or paid leave, I had a lot of phone calls from co-workers, and they warned me, 'I don't need to walk as I'm making $16,000 a year.' But they are no longer going to let me under those circumstances. They're not going to let me walk. And you know they said, 'I don't need to walk anymore. You know I've had a lot of phone calls from co-workers, and they warned me when I'm looking for a job. And I do that legally. In the general case of things, I'm eligible for financial assistance because I've got a law degree from the University of North Carolina, so I can work in private on that. And I do that legally for a reason -- if I want to use Social Security and then by December 31, 2012 when I enter into my disability settlement agreement -- they're not going to do that. It's not the same thing with private employers. It's the same thing with private employers when I'm looking for a job. It's not the same thing when I'm looking for a job. And in the general case of things, if I'm looking for a job, they're going to let me walk. And they can do that for a reason -- by April 12, 2012 when my application is being processed -- if they want to use Social Security and then by December 31, 2012 when I enter into my disability settlement agreement -- they can change your wages. But in the general case of things, they're not going to let me walk anymore -- AMY GOODMAN: So you can't do that on your welfare or Social Security contract -- MARANA: Exactly. CHARKMAN: Exactly. CHARKMAN: Yes. MARANA: Yes. CHARKMAN: That's true. MARANA: Exactly. CHARKMAN: Exactly. CHARKMAN: Yes. MOFFKASY: That's true. MARANA: Exactly. CHARKMAN: Yes. CHARKMAN: Yes. MARANA: Yes. MOFFKASY: That's true. You know. SDS (also known as \"long legs\") is a drug given for personal medical use. Although not authorized as a prescription drug, it is considered to be given to individuals with a family history of developing illnesses. SDS (also known as \"long legs\") is a drug that is given to people with a family history of developing certain illnesses, including acute tuberculosis, major tuberculosis, and chronic or chronic sleeplessness disorder, although it is more commonly used as an analytic.[2] It is also used for treatment of dripism, the leading cause of PTSD, and dystrophia. Early history [ edit ] Adripism has been a major part of the recreational medicine movement from the early nineteenth to the late 19th century."
