        "text": "Jordanian President Mahmoud Mubarak was sentenced by a court on Tuesday to second-degree murder offences for strangle and brutally beating a 3-year-old, Zaf Ki Immanizuk, according to the Mubarak’s family. Immanizuk had died of the beating on Oct. 25. Jordanian leader pleas for extension of 10-year sentence Sa’e Ashman, Egyptian News, October 19, 2017 Following the verdict, Mubarak’s family, Omar, released a statement insisting that their leader had made no factual statement about his involvement in the previous beatings, which wounded their two children. The Egyptian news site Weirdak reported that Mubarak had pleaded guilty to second-degree murder and related offences last year after court agreed to force him to extend the 10-year sentence. The site, as expected, did not mention that Mubarak had pleaded guilty under protective circumstances to a violent crime. “This murder is a crime not a crime,” Weirdak reported. “The report states that the first beating occurred on Oct. 25 at 5:54 p.m., and during that time most of the incidents in the report were related to abuse on the parent’s part and the child’s father.” It is also unclear how the second beating of Immanizuk had affected his son, Weirdak reported. Following his verdict, Jordanian President Mahmoud Mubarak said that he had raided the al-Nayami home of their two children, Jabba milia and Zena la in the village of al-Nyami, on Tuesday morning while he was returning from a visit with their two youngest boys. They reported that Mubarak had struck the couple with a blade at their 3-year-old son, Zaf Kiyzuk. “I assailed him that he can’t be hit. That’s not the way to do it. I told the child to sit next to me,” Mubarak said. While the couple was trying to take the son back to the hospital, the parents returned to the scene and the president once again stopped caring, he said. “While attempting to take the child to the hospital, I strangle him three more times, tied him against the wall, dragged him on his shoulders and put him in a helicopter,” Mubarak said. “I tied him against the wall and punched him with a steel blade. I strangle him two-and-a-half times, drew him on his shoulders and put him in an helicopter.” Image copyright AP Image captionThousands of civilians were killed when government forces attacked Civil journalists were attacked in the Syrian suburb of Hodnya on TuesdayThousands of civilian journalists were killed when government forces attacked in the Syrian suburb of Hodnya amid a crackdown on Bashar al-Assad by opposition forces. Human Rights Watch said that at least an additional 21 civilian journalists were killed by government strikes. Speaking at a news conference in Washington, Hodnya Prime Minister Mikhail Hmaniri told reporters the government forces had launched an attack on civilian journalists. \"The attackers targeted the civilians who were attacked,\" he told BBC Radio 4's World to Watch. \"Let's kill them.\" Government forces have been assaulting civilians in Syria since the Battle of Raqta which claimed the lives of more than 33 people. The latest attack on civilians comes amid a military crackdown on Bashar al-Assad, who has called for an immediate ceasefire, and continued into last month's airstrikes, near Assir al-Aqsa Square in Aleppo. Government forces launched the strikes after Sunni groups in the Arab world backed an anti-Assad government of Rama Fatah al-Pshmerga, based in Turkey. The Syrian government said in May that 24 civilian journalists and activists had been killed in the strikes. The al-Aqsa TV Network reported on Tuesday that government forces loyal to Peshmerga killed 17 journalists and nine activists in the incident. The report, however, did not say a precise number of those killed, which is yet to be determined. The identity of those killed has yet to be determined, and authorities could not establish a exact number of those killed. The Assir al-Aqsa TV Network also reported that 21 civilian journalists and activists of Raqta residents had been killed in a airstrike near al-Aqsa Square, in Aleppo Province, after gas canisters came under fire from government forces. by Sam"
