        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-5c96eee660c8-000484",
        "sourceId": 484,
        "text": "As a paid rookie and an undrafted free agent, LeAndre Packling has been pitch well of late, and it’s unlikely that he’ll be on the Toronto Blue Jays for some time. JOHAN FERO/Toronto Star/Getty Images The Tampa Bay Marlins’ 131-114 win over the San Francisco Giants on Sunday night was a significant win for the team, and it’s likely due solely to the positive results fans saw during their first six years with the Marlins. By far, the greatest improvement has come from LeAndre Packling, who posted a career-high 16.5 WBS/+ when in 247 games with the Islanders. In 202 games with one year left on his contract, Packling has posted a career 6.1 ERA with 107 home runs and 65 RBIs in 26 innings pitched for the Marlins. In 193 games last season, he hit.180/.359/.429/20 with 144 home runs and 53 RBI. In 561 games last season, he’s posted a career 3.0 ERA with 107 home runs. Packling’s ERA has continued to drop during each of the last two seasons, and that’s likely due in part to his changeball, but he’s been a pretty good hitter of late. In 102 games last year, he hit.239/.367/.468 with 19 home runs and 47 RBIs.Those results haven’t been bad, but he’s also been a good offensive pitcher and has been staying on the NL’s injured list for some time. In 122 games in 2016 he hit.284/.352/.50/1 while maintaining a per-base walk average of 2.9. In 163 innings pitched last season, he had just 8.1 WBS/+. Packling’s biggest improvement has been in his health, but he’s also pitched well in the outfield and has been pretty productive.His ERA has continued to drop during each of the last two seasons, and that’s likely due in part to his changeball, but he’s also a pretty good offensive pitcher and made some decent appearances in the bullpen. Numerous Marlins starters such as Matt Ruben, Roberto Santos, Henrik Gomez Jr., and John Hayden have ended his contract.As a paid rookie and an undrafted free agent, LeAndre Packling is pitch well of late, and it’s unlikely that he’ll be on the Toronto Blue Jays for some time. He’s also been losing time in the outfield and has been forced to move away from Tampa Bay to elsewhere after revolving on the NL’s injured list this spring. As a result, the 2016-17 season has seen the Marlins lose a large number of key starters, endangering starters like Packling, Ruben and Santos. The Jays may be on a roll in 2017, but it remains to be seen how far they have come. WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The pilot suspected in stabbings and bombing attacks for a powerful U.S.-backed rebel group has been extradited to the United States, the State Department said on Wednesday. Mohammed Yayev, 56, is seen outside his U.S. Army prison during an extraditiond operation in Atlanta, Georgia, U.S. September 27, 2017. REUTERS/Carlos Hill Mohammed Yayev became general commander of the U.S.-backed rebel group, Jabhat al-Nusra, after the death of a group leader, an official told Reuters. Yayev was general commander of the rebel group’s eastern branch. He fled to Syria in 2011 after his arrest descended into a three-year standoff. Officials said he had been wanted in connection with the assassination of Bradley Manning, an American patriot. Yayev, suspected in carrying out bombings for a U.S.-backed rebel group in the early 1990s, has not been designated a terrorist by security authorities. Yayev, who has not appeared in court, is still general commander of the rebel group’s eastern branch, the official said, adding that his extradition was being requested from Khalip Mohammed, who is considered one of the top opposition figures in the country. The State Department said the former U.S. Army pilot had spent 27 days in custody while awaiting the results of his extradition. Legendary: First name: Giant flying color"
