        "sourceId": 506,
        "text": "“Two, three minutes into the incident in the Fort Hill area there was a large confrontation between users of the traffic on either of the two roads,” said Tom Casey, spokesman for the U.S. National Guard. Little said he witnessed the gunman roaring along Interstate 45, which links Fort Ellis and Bonawe Air Force Base, about 41 miles north of Fort Ellis. Johnson said Little saw the gunman roaring along I 45. “It appears someone is trying to help him,” Little said. Little said the gunman also drove toward a vehicle that was in the middle of the highway, but that it is not known where it landed, due to the lack of traffic, Little said of the confrontation. Copyright  2019 The Washington Times, LLC. Click here for reprint permission, as necessary. A police soldier has been rushed to a hospital after suffering a gunshot wound after being shot inside a street van riding by wounded officers. Sergeant Luis Abey, 37, was shot in the back of a street van Saturday afternoon in the 33,000 block of South Williams Avenue, a statement from the King County coroner said. After a blood test was conducted Saturday, Abey faces six convictions of fourth felony assault with stolen property, fifth felony possession of a deadly weapon and seventh felony charge of burglary. Abey faces five convictions of second felony burglary and third felony assault with stolen property, according to police. Sergeants Karl Vuri, 41, and Eric Perez, 34, of South Williams Avenue, were summoned about 9:00 p.m. Saturday morning, Lt. Col. John Ottberg said. Police later arrested Tisser and Perez at previous addresses for property possession, officials said. Perez was released, Ottberg said. NYPD Defensive Officer Jonathan Tisser remembers stepping outside when he heard shots fired. \"I thought it was going to be another target shot, but he just kind of stravelled and kind of ran,\" Tisser said. Tisser said the gunman \"got off his feet,\" but wounded officers managed to get to the scene, shooting him multiple times, he added. \"He just went up there and ran,\" Tisser said. NYPD investigators said no officers were on the scene when the gunman shot inside a street van. Division Commissioner Jan Allen arrived at the scene about 8 a.m. Saturday, about 15 minutes after the shooting. \"I walked outside and told him if we were going to make it to the hospital, they'd send us here if we wanted to get there,\" Allen said. \"He said, Damn, they said we can leave.\" Police fired the officer after the second shot, Allen said. Sergeant John Coultt, who led the scene, said he didn't know where the gunman was dead. \"My understanding is he was shot in the back of that van,\" Coultt said. \"I don't know if he would have been over here had he not have been there.\" New York Emergency Manager Jonathan Tisser was walking near a wounded man's parking lot when he was shot about 5:15 p.m., police said. Tisser was stab in the legs and taken to San Pasadena Medical Center, where he died from a wound. He was then taken to Wilkers Hospital, where he was scheduled to be evaluated in the evening, police said. The officer was taken to West Port Hospital where he died from the injuries, Tisser said. The officer was taken to Wilkers Hospital where he died. \"It was a violent encounter, to be sure,\" Tisser said. \"We don't know for sure what happened.\" Authorities were searching various social networks and the Internet for clues to the gunfire. A stolen car, which the FBI said has been carrying out anti-rug operations, was found in a gunfire shot inside a stolen home early Saturday morning. A stolen car, which the FBI said has been carrying out anti-drug operations for a long time, was found in a gunfire shot inside a stolen home Saturday morning. The car was stopped by a law enforcement agent while driving inside a home in the 47015 block of South Williams Avenue just north of the shooting, according to the bus stop, which is located less than a mile from a road. There are reports of shot wounds inside the home, which was demolished early Saturday, according to a press release from agents with the New York State Sheriff's Office. Want to learn more about police? View"
