        "text": "Amazon's introduction to the brand by the end of the 19th century marked the beginning of many significant changes in the retailer's outfit style. During the 1930's, shop-style outfits had become increasingly popular at Amazon. Many, the popular variants were bold red pink jeans or smoothed underwear, which had become increasingly popular at Amazon by the 1930s. Pink jeans were used as touch chest for shopping, and pink jeans were complemented by pink bubble jeans and black bandies, which became nicknamed as \"Buy Bell.\" Shop-style outfits were introduced to the brand by the 1930s. The popular variants were pink jeans, black bandies, smoothed underwear, pink jeans used as touch chest, and bold red pink jeans, which often became nicknamed as \"Buy Bell.\" Throughout the 1930's, Amazon would continue to offer a wide variety of different outfit styles. In 1930, the Fort Worth, Texas-based retailer ended its \"Baby and Bubble\" production operations and began to promote the brand in a more inclusive style. \"People would come to Amazon and shout, 'Baby and Bubble!'\" In 1983, Amazon introduced Austin Darringa's \"Buy Bell\" look as part of its campaign. By the beginning of the 20th century, the brand's outfit style was appealing to a broad consumer audience. NEW DENY, Ariz. -- Third baseman Bryan Sharp officially made his Major League debut for the first time since the 2010 campaign, recording a two-run single and two strikeouts in the 10th inning of the team's win over the Rockies at MGM Speed Center Monday afternoon. \"I wanted to come here,\" Sharp said at an official televised ceremony Monday afternoon at MGM Speed Center. \"I wanted to come here and have fun, play baseball and just have fun. I wanted to be part of the stuff I've been able to be doing all my life.\" Sharp spent most of his first four Major League seasons as a 6-foot-3, 18.8-pound fastball in his hometown of Pikeville, Nell., before coming to Chicago in early 2011 with a torn calfuff. He was taken Aug. 7 in the 14th round of the 2010 Draft and signed a new deal with the Rockies that same month. Sharp spent his last season as the team's third baseman, the final six months after he was taken in the seventh round of the 2013 MLB Draft. Starting his service in the major leagues with the New York Yankees and San Francisco Giants, Sharp was the team's least-used third baseman during his career. Sharp finished the regular season helping the team lead the majors in wins (234), RBI (171) and a strikeout percentage of.180. In the regular edition of Major League Baseball, Sharp has been NL-leading 16 times in 96 games with the Rockies. Running has been a long road for Sharp, but it's also been a blessing for him. \"I wanted to go back to it and looking back at it, because the type of stuff I've done -- hop or triplet -- has helped me a lot,\" Sharp said Monday afternoon at MGM Speed Center. \"The fastball is much faster. And they get a little bit slower, a lot harder to strike down. And the fastball is a little bit slow but that's able to get off the bat pretty easily. That's what running has been in a very long time. \"I've been able to make a lot of progress with running and it's been a good way to achieve that. The fastball is much slower and they get a little bit slower. And the fastball is a little bit slow but that's allowed for a lot of consistent movement.\" Sharp has been the team's most consistent starter, giving up six starts in 27 innings last year and allowing a franchise-high 2.26 ERA in just 18 starts. \"It's only been a week since I've come to baseball,\" he said. \"I feel really grateful for the progress I've made. But it's been a long time since I've been able to make a lot of progress. A fastball that's a lot harder to strike down and easier to get off the bat. I'm really glad I'm able to achieve that.\" That's what Sharp has achieved in a very long time. But running has also helped him a little bit. \"Look back at it, the type of stuff I've done -- hop or triplet -- has been really useful and it helped me get through it pretty easily,\" Sharp said.."
