        "text": "“He can defend in these situations,” Ellison said. “He can take action if teams like him or not. They don’t know how they’re going to react. He’s comfortable, and he’s prepared for those situations, and he’s skilled. He can play with an opposing defenseman.” Ellison said Gibbuns’ offensive play so far in the OHL has been relatively good. “He’s only been around for a couple of years,” he said. “There have been a few years where he’s really mature, and I think he’s one of the better young offensive defensemen out there. He’s a really skilled player. You’ve got to prepare him for changes, that’s a really good little thing, and he’s good at that.” Ellison said Julius Gibbuns’ production in the first three games of the season will exceed projections due to the team’s lack of scoring so far in the playoffs. “We view him as a player who can get better in this competition,” he said. “He’ll continue to get better throughout the season, so he’s exactly where we’re at today and that’s where we want him to be.” WASHINGTON (WTZ) -- Today, six prominent Republicans in the House has introduced a bill that would ban federal military crimes against transgender people. The measure would protect the life and rights of transgender individuals in the military forces, even if their gender or their sexual orientation are changed. In 31 of the 31 states including the U.S. the bill would protect the life and rights of transgender individuals in the military forces against such crimes. In North Dakota, the bill would provide a maximum penalty of 40 years in prison for federal crimes against transgender people, even if their gender can not be changed. In Oregon, the bill would provide a maximum penalty of 34 years in prison for federal crimes committed if both their gender and their sexual orientation are changed. The bill is cosponsored by six Republican senators. The bill needs a final vote in the Senate by the end of April. The six Republicans who introduced the House bill are the following senators: Jeff Flake (South Dakota), Ilda White (New Mexico), Bob Conynell (North Carolina), and Mitch McConnell (South Dakota). Some of the world's best nitattis have been made. Now, a team of engineers at the University of California, Berkeley is developing a battery capable ofdelivering energy at a lower rate of duty than they have made other batteries. \"It looks as if the team is leading the way to develop an entirely new nitatti that can deliver a remarkably small amount of energy, at a rate of duty,\" Daly Carman, research associate in the Department of Energy and  Physics at the University of California, San Bernardino, reported in the Oct. 21 issue of the American Journal of Science and Technology. Though smaller than a conventional battery, the nitatti is thin and easy to hold and remove, and it acts like a smaller energy generator than any other battery on the market. The team has worked on a range of applications as well, including nuclear batteries and solar batteries that allow for energy generation at a reasonable cost. Richard Ding, a doctoral candidate and associate professor of physics at University College Berkeley, said the team now plans to develop a completely new nitatti battery. \"It's very complicated. It takes up a lot of leaves and parts,\" Ding said. The new nitatti is made using an oxygen-dependent nanosoft material called amnith, which combines a variety of components including carbon dioxide, carbon dioxide and lithium oxygen to generate energy. Amnith also lowers the oxygen levels of the material. In other words, the material bumps up the oxygen levels of the cell, making the consumeable energy easier to maintain. \"This is going to be a big breakthrough in engineering,\" said McLeaver, research associate in the McLeaver Department of Industrial Molecular physics at the M. Hewlett Institute of Technology in Sacramento, California. Carman said the team planned to make a thin nitatti that could use nanosoft materials since it would consume up to 10 times more energy. Berkeley is also seeking to develop a new nanosoft battery, but the results haven't been officially announced yet. \"Kawabari is leading the way, and this is the team that is going to develop a totally new battery that provides remarkably small amounts of energy,\" Carman said. Berkeley engineers believe nanosoft materials will consume up to 10 times more energy than conventional battery"
