        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-5c96eee660c8-000580",
        "sourceId": 580,
        "text": "TOKYYO, October 10 (UPI) -- Two new Moto X series are now arriving in Japan that will be rolled out on mobile platforms worldwide. Xiaomi on Tuesday issued a press release saying a new gaming console -- alternately titled as the Moto X series -- would arrive in Japan on October 10. It will feature a Snapdragon 810-400 CPU with a microSD card on the front and a 7.5-megapixel camera on the rear. It will further feature a free-to-mo system that will allow users to play their hands-on games on Android or Android devices. When made available in the United States, Xiaomi president Mario Sai has said the Moto X series will benefit from \"top-notch software\" that allows users to play their games using mobile services on their consoles and devices. The Moto X series will feature a Snapdragon 810 400 and Intel Core i7 CPU at a US$547,5600 using a microSD card with a 1.1-mAh battery. Users will be able to download and install a variety of games, apps and other software. Xiaomi plans to improve the hands-on experience at home by adding new features to the MotoX and installing the latest gaming software on their servers. The Moto X series features a Snapdragon 810 400 and Core i7 CPU at a US$479,5950 price using a microSD card that is backed by a 1.1-mAh battery while the X series will feature a Snapdragon 810 400 and Intel Core i7 CPU at a US$167,600 price. Using the MotoX and the X, Users will be able to create high-quality images and videos. The Moto X series will also be able to exploit the capabilities of its BYR-300000 controller, which will feature a 7.5-megapixel and 7.1-megapixel camera on the front and back sides. The Moto X series will feature a Snapdragon 810 400 and BYR-300000TM gaming CPU at a capacity of 230GB while the X series will feature a Snapdragon 810 400 and Intel Core i7 CPU at a US$6794 and a capacity of 128GB. Both consoles feature a free-to-mo system designed by Xiaomi so that users will be able to play their games on Android or Android devices. Moto X series will be available in the Western SCI and Japan on the following dates: China on October 10, 2019 and South Korea on October 10, 2019. In Southeast Asia, two new Moto X series will become available: Taiwan on October 10, 2019 and Taiwan on October 10, 2019. In Southeast Asia, Japan on October 10, 2019 and Hong Kong on October 10, 2019. U.S. Military Marines walk on the trees at the Fort Cook Air Force Base in Hamilton Hills, N.H. on October 10, 2019 at the U.S. Air Force Base in Fort Cook, Va. (Photo: Brian Branton, USA TODAY) The NSA cannot seize sensitive information about a government employee, U.S. Justice Department Attorney General Edward Snowden said Tuesday in an open letter made available to the NSA's U.S. Defense Intelligence Agency. Snowden had delivered a September letter to the U.S. Defense Intelligence Agency, a former U.S. national security agency, seeking permits to seize records about a U.S. government employee -- including the place of the job, the date, origin of the job or even a phone or email address of a U.S. government employee. \"There is no known authority for the Department of Agriculture, Commerce, Housing and Urban Development to seize and withhold records about a State Department employee,\" Snowden wrote, adding that that rule \"entangles the privacy of the records.\" In his letter sent Tuesday, Snowden introduced a rule that under Article 17 of the U.S. Constitution, a federal agency cannot disclose both a person's exact location or that of the employee to the general public, regardless of whether a phone, email address or even a person's name is disclosed to the federal government. Snowdendefended his letter in its entirety, saying he was concerned the sensitive information could be disturbing to terrorist targets. \"There is literally no way to know the location of the employee, because there are about 5,000 or so State Department officers and contractors working at the job 24 hours a week to check the integrity of the records,\" Snowden wrote. Ever since the NSA first started leaking classified information to the press, the agency has done everything it's could to conceal it from the American public. Snowden's letter coincides with the 2006 letter from then NSA Deputy Director Alex"
