        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-5c96eee660c8-000674",
        "sourceId": 674,
        "text": "The indictment was sought by Marianne Jenning, a 27-year-old woman who discovered Johnson had struck her father with a knife on her right hand. Jenning says the charge violates the Class B misdemeanor code and other civil misdemeanor laws. Johnson's dad, John Johnson, who was the scene in the shooting, told authorities his son didn't have permits to carry classified weapons. After the shooting, Johnson sued a judge because he thought his family didn't have valid permits to carry classified weapons. \"It would be inappropriate to testify before a judge,\" said Jenning, who said he thought his family didn't have a permit to carry any classified weapons. Authorities are concerned they wouldn't need a permit to carry classified weapons in their community. Johnson's lawyers rejected the judge's request for cleency. The judge ordered him be a member of the League of Resistance, an evangelical advocacy group battling charges. Johnson was released from custody Sunday afternoon after a violent demonstration outside the 40 block of John Johnson Road. He faces a Class B misdemeanor charge. Supporters protested Johnson's release, saying he shouldn't be able to keep the knife on his right hand. They also defended Johnson, who had been detained at Chicago County jail for nearly two weeks after his arrest for allegedly breaking the security of his cell phone and a set of cables. A Chicago judge dismissed the charge, but ruled he could not be considered as a civil misdemeanor offense. President Donald Trump is preparing for a military offensive in Syria with the objective of the overthrow of President Bashar al-Assad. Two White House officials have expressed concern that the offensive could break out in an area where he is likely to get involved in the dissolution of the Islamic State. The White House has ruled out a major offensive campaign against the Islamic State. | Alex Walker, Associated Press WASHINGTON — Trump is preparing for a military offensive in Syria, although with the stated objective being the overthrow of President Bashar al-Assad. The White House could be more interested in the idea, warned two U.S. officials. ADVERTISING Read more The \"military action scenario,\" said the White House officials, speaking on condition of anonymity to discuss Trump's plans, could give the U.S. government a boost of flexibility it needs to deploy and pay for its military actions and actions to fight al-Assad's forces. Trump's announcement has sparked outrage from anti-Assad advocates and allies who say the Obama administration's plans of carrying out an air-to-air offensive campaign against Assad could ultimately lead U.S. troops into Syria instead of ousting an insurgent group. \"What Trump has confirmed to us... is that he is preparing for carrying out a military offensive in Syria,\" a U.S. official told CBS News. \"Nobody knows when he will carry out a military offensive in Syria or a case by case, but if he wants to carry out a military offensive in an area he's likely to be involved in, he could be interested in the idea,\" added that same U.S. official. The White House has been critical of Trump's announcement, saying it would make a \"grave mistake\" to fight al-Assad's forces, thereby leading U.S. troops into fighting for the dissolution of the Islamic State. Two White House officials, speaking on condition of anonymity to discuss U.S. armed policy, expressed concern that Trump's announcement could further bolster the Obama administration's plans of carrying out an air-wide campaign against Assad. \"There's a possibility that the Trump administration is proposing a major offensive campaign against Assad,\" one U.S. official told CBS News in New York City. The official said there was \"very little clarity on when the president will carry out another military offensive in Syria, although we don't know yet how much support is being gathered against an insurgent group.\" On Thursday, acting U.S. Ambassador to the United Nations David Cooper told CBS News that no consideration had been given to the possibility that the U.S. would intervene to remove Assad. \"Once the U.S. president proposes to carry out another military offensive in Syria before the end of 2015, it alone is a highly rare scenario,\" Cooper said. \"It is unlikely that the president will propose a major offensive campaign in Syria based on the success of the chemical weapons offensive against President Bashar al-Assad in 2014.\" Should the U.S. remove Assad anytime soon? Part story"
