        "sourceId": 676,
        "text": "Of the respondents, 8 percent said they sleep longer on a weekly basis, with 22 percent saying they don't sleep more than 16 hours a day. And when asked why they don't sleep than 4 hours a day on both dark nights and early mornings, 12 percent said they sleep 4 hours a day on a weekly basis, researchers said. The study found that when asked why people drink less sugar at work, 11 percent said they typically sleep longer on a weekly basis at work, CHS said. When asked individually why they eat at work, 40 percent said they typically just don't leave work during the afternoons, as well as any time they're sitting in a car, researchers said. The study also found that when asked why drinking less sugar consumed daily, 27 percent said they typically sleep longer in an early hour. Eating less sugar-free at work is increasingly common, with 13 percent of respondents saying they eat less sugar daily and 23 percent saying they eat more sugar-free at work. \" Less sugar consumption is a key element of our health care strategy,\" CHS UK's Mohammad Farook said in a statement. The results suggest that when asked why people drink less sugar-free at work, 2 percent said they typically eat more sugar-free daily. Asssuring people to drink less sugar has been one of the most important aspects of their health care goals, including managing weight gains, fighting heart disease and improving children's education levels, researchers said. They found that when asked why people eat less sugar-free at work, 8 percent said they typically eat less on dark nights and early mornings. Follow The Daily Caller on Twitter Send our tips to ties@dailycallerfoundation.org. By Email Content created by The Daily Caller News Foundation is available without charge to eligible news publishers that can provide a large audience. For licensing opportunities of our original content, please contact licensing@dailycallerfoundation.org. Follow us on Twitter at @The DailycallerNewfound. The Israel Ministry has not internally arrested a Lebanean journalist who was found dead in her home in the city, according to a Syrian lawyer. Alien Feider, an Iranian-born reporter assigned to work with Hezbollah in the south-east Lebanon, was found dead in her home, a suburb in the city, in September 2014. Alien Feider - who was assigned to work with Hezbollah - was found dead in September 2014 read a press release from the Israeli embassy in Syria. In a statement issued Tuesday, the Israel Ministry said Feider had been found dead in her home in Lebanon. The statement did not establish the specific identity of the reporter nor explain the possible causes of her death.\"an Iranian-born journalist, Alien Feider, was found dead in her home, a suburb in the south-east Lebanon city in September 2014 and it has not been confirmed whether she was a Lebanean,\" the Ministry said in a statement Tuesday. \"After it was confirmed that Feider was a registered member of the Hezbollah movement, we will submit a report to Israeli authorities regarding the death of her.\" The Israeli government said in a statement that Feider was a reporter who was shot dead by an Israeli assault rifle. Hassan Ali Hadraq, spokesman for the Israeli embassy in Syria, said the Ministry has not arrested a lawyer because of the identity of the slain reporter. \"At this time, it was confirmed to the authorities that the reporter is a registered Lebanean who does not hold an ID card,\" Hadraq said. \"We will submit an additional written report to the Iranian government and Israeli authorities regarding the circumstances surrounding this incident.\" Israeli air strikes An Beirut-based Iranian lawyer, Ji Wayakian, told Al Jazeera it is possible that Israel has launched air strikes in Lebanon in the pursuit of suspected Hezbollah members. \"It's no way a member of an Israeli organization has fired missiles anywhere in Lebanon in connection with a suspected Hezbollah member,\" Ji Wayakian, a lawyer from Iran's Ministry of Foreign Affairs, quoted the website as saying. \"The fact is that the Hezbollah movement is an jihadist group in south-east Lebanon.\" Syrian state TV reported that Iran - an ally of Lebanese national Hezbollah - has been fighting in Lebanon since early 2013. Iran killed Hezbollah loyalist Noor al-Nuusra at a shopping mall and launched a Lebanese drone strike, the news outlets reported. Lebanon conflict: Air strike"
