        "text": "“The new agreement is aimed at addressing important issues in regional affairs regarding the establishment of an Israel-Pales Union,” he said. “The fact that the two countries share territorial affairs can be key to the peace process. Even more importantly, this agreement will help give the Palestinian Authority an opportunity to advance regional and tactical integration. “It also provides the Palestinians with an alternative opportunity to return to the historical tradition of being a Palestinian state, instead of being an independent nation. This agreement will undermine Israel’s policies toward Palestine and allows the Middle East to return to a strong Israel- Palestine partnership, while restoring at the same time, the political and tactical unity of the Palestinians,” said Dmitry Riyekov. Ronald Hussain can be contacted at rhussain@dailyilynews.com. WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States is significantly expanding its involvement in the fight against Russian-backed rebels across the Middle East while maintaining a more active stance against Russia’s aggressive policies in the region, a senior U.S. official said. Russian President Vladimir Putin delivers a news conference during a visit to Tel Aviv in Washington, Russia May 2, 2015. REUTERS/Tom Orban The administration supported the war in the Middle East in Syria and Iraq after the Islamic Republic scaled back a more aggressive stance against the group, which human rights activists fear might bolster the Islamist movement. “It has been very clear to me that the U.S is significantly expanding its involvement as a regional partner in the fight against Russian-backed rebels in the Middle East in Syria and Iraq, while maintaining a firm commitment to preventing an armed conflict on both sides, regardless of outcome,” Leon Montudo, U.S. secretary of state for the East and Central Asia, said on condition of anonymity. The United States vetos Moscow’s pleas to impose economic sanctions on Iran in violation of international law’s human rights obligations, and will not ease the sanctions sought by the Islamic Republic. But Putin’s rhetoric about Russia’s support of rebels in the Middle East differed from his position in recent months, when the Russian government sent more troops to Syria, Iraq and the Middle East to help fight Islamist groups. Putin, who last week held talks with Turkish Prime Minister Recep Tayep Erdogan in a U.N. Security Council climate summit, was impulsed by Russia’s refusal to attend the talks. In a call for greater burdens on the United States to restrain its ties with Moscow, he was staunch against criticism that they might fuel the rise of a Russian-backed rebel movement across the Middle East and that they play a key role in the fight against ISIS in Syria. While primarily championing the use of force, Putin has focused his energy around peace talks with Syrian President Bashar al-Assad, which have increasing value to regional stability. Furthermore, the U.S. will continue to maintain a strong, active position in the Middle East as a counterarbiter in the fight against Islamist groups,” he said outside the Pentagon, speaking on condition of anonymity. Putin’s office did not return requests for comment. Syria’s border with Russia was often spoken of as a learning ground for rebels fighting the Islamic Republic in the Middle East. Rebels have frequently accused Moscow and Washington of eliminating a military base in Syria, which lies 640 kilometers north of Deir al-Khazal in Zukabad, Iraq, as part of Moscow’s crackdown on IS. The move has raised a division over Russia’s military presence. Russia has said the move will erode rebel groups in Syria and clear the way for forces fighting the Islamist movement in Syria, while Western leaders have called for reforms in Syria and Iraq, particularly a war against Islamic State. The United States has often called on the West to cooperate with the international community. But he has frequently questioned Moscow’s role in the Middle East in light of the contributions it makes to the fight of rebels in the region. “There will not be an expansion of force in Syria,” said Daniel J. Varvinsky, a Russia expert at the University of New Hampshire’s Brennan Center for International Security. While Putin and members of Congress have given broad support to U.S. armed forces in the region, he has been particularly critical of Washington’s longstanding support of Moscow along the border with Russia and its push to send more troops into Syria. Between October and February, Washington sent more troops to the eastern Iraq city of Deir al-Khazal, which is bitterly fighting alongside ISIS, but which remains under full Russian control."
