        "text": "No one blocks the field any more, no one runs the ball down, no one makes plays. It is one of the worst moves the DLC has ever made. They can't defend their tight ends -- at least in the last few cases! It's not clear how the Seahawks do that, just one tip: \"Don't leave for the car.\"Interested in more Great New York Times content? Like JennyNYC (https://t.co/2khM6gI) on Twitter (@JennyNYC). Follow Jelly on Twitter or Instagram (@JennyNYC). Don't let it get worse next time we see this. Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Numerous airstrikes were carried out on civilians in the Yemeni capital on Saturday, leaving several people injured and damaged, the US-led coalition government has said. At least 40 people had been killed by the airstrikes launched by militants from the Jabhat al-Nusra Front, Syrian embassy spokeswoman Gulmi Harman said, adding the Jabhat al-Nusra Front has been held responsible for 85 total civilian deaths. A statement said the warplanes killed 10 people, including 22-year-old Mohammed al-Haziqi, 20 Sufou al-Mioui and 20 Ibrahim al-Manaui around 4:40 GMT. Two warplanes also fired teargas on civilians. The Human Rights Commission said a total of three civilians were killed when the airstrikes carried out by the Jabhat al-Nusra Front opened fire on Yemeni streets. \"Fairstrikes carried out by the US-led coalition signaled that the safety and security of civilians in Yemen is of grave importance,\" U.N.'s Human Rights Commission said in a statement. Ambush claims death of civilian Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Some of the victims killed were hit by warplanes One victim, 25-year-old Said al-Haziqi, was shot dead by assailants in the village of Ahrar al-Azbar around 1:45.00 GMT. The third victim, 29-year-old Suoud al-Haziqi, suffered multiple gunshot wounds and is in critical condition. The fourth civilian who died, 25-year-old Said al-Haziqi, was rushed to a nearby hospital after snipers of ammunition and explosives opened fire. The fifth victim, 29-year-old Suoud Kasaki, was also shot down by assailants in the village of Ahrar al-Azbar around 1:45 GMT and his body was damaged. Syrian prosecutors say there were several airstrikes carried out by militants, including Kasaki, who is with the Syrian Revolutionary Protection Forces. It is unclear how many of the victims were killed. The United States embassy in Yemen said it was searching for those responsible for the strike and would try to capture the militants, including the people still missing. 3:20 GMT - Today at least 40 civilians were killed by militants from the Jabhat al-Nusra Front, Islamic State says. The incident follows a earlier terror attack in which 45 people were killed. U.N. The Office for the Protection of Human Rights says a so-called \"single battle\" occurred in the province of al-Abbas, killing 34 militants and wounded 19 civilians. The Office for the Protection of Human Rights says the Jabhat al-Nusra Front has been held responsible for 85 total deaths. A so-called \"small battle\" occurred in the province of al-Abbas, killing 34 militants and wounded 19 civilians.5:00 GMT - Today at least 40 civilians were killed by militants from the Jabhat al-Nusra Front, Islamic State says. It follows a earlier terror attack in which 45 people were killed in the province of al-Abbas Ibrahim Rasadi, U.N.'s envoy for Yemeni humanitarian affairs, said in a statement that 85 innocent civilians had been killed. \"Countless militants from the Jabhat al-Nusra Front have been held responsible for 85 deaths,\" he said in a statement. U.N. said it was monitoring the incident and said it would update it once the cause of the attack was determined. A spokesman for the United Nations, however, said more than 1,800 people were killed in the fighting near the Syrian Army stronghold of Homs on Saturday.. Image.:"
