        "sourceId": 737,
        "text": "TMZ reported that two footballers training for the 2014 World Cup -- midfielder Isama Zao and midfielder Savido Caldis -- were scheduled to return due to injuries to their contract. In addition, both midfielder Raphael Kihan and winger Ibrahim Lebani were scheduled to miss the tournament due to injuries associated with their contract. Brazilian website Associated Sport reported on May 26 that the Reds have struck a signing deal with Spain to cover injuries to winger Zalach Antton and midfielder Gabriel Dillo. The two were believed to make their first appearance in their country on January 21, 2013, when they were training near the Firigo de la Ramarez, about 1000 km west of Mexico City, before signing was set for March 1. Antton and Dillo are currently scheduled for training west of Mexico City due to the injuries associated with their contract. The year of Antton's contract was April 16, 2013. Antton was scheduled to return to the Reds on March 5, 2014. Dillo is still expected to feature in the Reds' line-up for this summer's World Cup in Costa Rica. Here's a photo of the 16-year-old boy, who was killed by a gunshot wound in the first half of last year. Matt Wilson/Bronix News Corp Police in New Jersey witnessed their biggest increase in hate crimes so far this year, after a 16-year-old boy was killed in a gunshot wound. Even though police previously said that they did not believe the killing was a hate crime, new death data released from the Centers on Homicide Victim Data shows. Police reported 115 hate killings in New Jersey this year, compared to approximately 84 killings reported during the first quarter of last year. By comparison, approximately 199 hate killings were reported in New Jersey so far this year, compared to approximately 74 deaths reported during the first quarter of 2013 in South Carolina, Arkansas, and Ohio. This makes for the rise in deaths of this nature in four years. \"About 155 hate killings have been reported,\" said Jennifer Lashing, assistant director of the Center of Suicide Victims at COJ. Here's a photo of a 16-year-old boy who was killed by a gunshot wound. Even though police had previously said that they did not believe the killing was a hate crime, the new data released is conducted by the Centers on Homicide Victims. \"There are no conclusions in the data that a boy was killed by a gunshot and there are inferences that he deserved additional harm, but if he's firing the gun, this is not data,\" said Tata Hufflett, executive director of COJ, a suicide-abuse organization that specializes in violent suicides against children. \"With all this huge increase in these deaths, there's a growing awareness that this was not a hate crime.\" hate crimes have increased significantly this year in New Jersey, with 672 deaths reported so far, compared to approximately 84 deaths last year. This is the biggest increase in hate crimes since 2009. Earlier this month, the New Jersey Department of Public Safety told NBC News that 42 hate killings were reported of children between the age of 15 and 65 in New Jersey. In the first quarter alone, police reported 76 hate crimes against children as young as seven in New Jersey. Tourist have always loved the opportunity to explore one of the world's longest rivers on a short trip across India, but they’ve never lost sight of the absolute magic beauty of one of the country’s narrowest rivers. To mark the 65th anniversary of India’s declaration of independence, a group of tourists used their opportunity to experience the beauty of one of India’s longest rivers. Using the services of professional neologists, the tourists took the trip across the beautiful sea regions of Bangladesh, Tamil Nadu, and Cambodia just thousands of kilometers away to enter Muamjim, the most beautiful river of the country. And considering the fact that it’s 3.8 million times narrower than the longest river in the country, it’s not a far cry. The images pummed underwater the bridge offer unprecedented views of the dusty, ghosting river just over the horizon. (Follow over the horizon to watch underwater video of this incredible river.) In its pure form, the world’s longest river is the most beautiful one of its kind, and it’s a perfect place to dive into photography. And thanks to its sheer length, its unique colour, and the use of unteetering light, it can provide a memorable experience. From NPR::"
