        "sourceId": 834,
        "text": "\"I don't really remember how well he was coming into that first season,\" Justin Chadman said. \"He was way better physically than he could've been. He was so good at where he was on the field, and he made a lot of mistakes. He's a really special guy. He had been on the same field all year, getting knocked out of the team multiple times. He was so healthy and he worked really hard and he looked like a guy. I think he did a great job.\" Perhaps the most effective second baseman in the world is Carlos Caras Estera, a former starter with the Phillies during his tenure as the team's second base coach. Estera, who just recently signed a restricted free agency deal, served as the team's second base coach from 1991 to 1992. Ramas is the most-loved manager by the Pirates, where he enjoyed an iconic, extra-game career as the franchise's second base coach and led the Phillies to the World Series title that year in 1995, ending a winning streak that took the franchise into the playoffs for the second season in a row. Carlos Caras Ramas is still at it today and while he has already become one the most effective second basemen available in baseball, he's also spending a lot of time trying to improve the second base system. t.chadman@leverfield.com Ramas is still at it today as the league's hardest-hitting second baseman, and he has already become one of baseball's hardest-hitting second basemen. He's also spending a lot of time trying to improve the second base system, according to pitcher Justin Chadman. The 31-year-old Ramas, who just signed a restricted free agency deal from 1999 to 2011, also was in the top-25 class. \"He was pretty much our No. 4,\" Chadman said. \"He's healthy, healthy and he played a part in keeping our team going, and that was the case in his first three games, which is really incredible. When he batted his first pitch and walked a single first pitch, it was probably the hardest thing we have ever done in our career. He's a talented, experienced, great teammate and he's going to be a big part of what we're going through. He obviously did a pretty nice job.\" Carlos Caras Ramas at the Pacific Field in Tampa Bay on April 26, 2010. Today is more than forty years since he was named baseball's most effective second baseman, and continues to be so. Steve C. Turner/Getty Images C. Turner/Getty Images The 31-year-old Ramas didn't bat for a long period of time, but won his first two games as a starter. As a starter, he played a critical role in the Phillies' success as second base coach. He averaged between 15 and 20 percent on strikeouts in his first four games as a baseman, and he looked poised for a strong season as a starter. Justin Chadman, who has been active in the Pirates system for years, said Ramas is the most impactful second base coach in the world. \"He's improved a lot over his career and he's improved at a high level,\" Chadman said. \"Later year, he batted his first home run and walked a single first pitch, which is incredible. So he's definitely going to another stage in his career where he will continue to improve. I don't think he's been as effective as a starter. He's been taking hits on both ends of the plate. He's had a lot of good moments. He's very healthy.\" Ramas had a bad year against the Houston Athletics, but filled the Pirates' starter position at an important time. His biggest moment came near the top of the first inning when he batted his first pitch, overwhelmed Phillies hitter Petro Amos, and swept Athletics reserve Jorge Luza with his second hit. That year, he was a game-changer and he had some incredible moments. \"He had some good moments in his first couple games, which were incredible as a second base coach,\" Chadman said. \"But as an measure of his health I don't think that's going to change until he gets healthy. He definitely started to get back to normal health.\" A series of airstrikes was launched against the Islamic State in the northern Syria province this morning. Scroll down"
