        "text": "“I think Indiana is much harder than expected,” Harford said. “The dynamics tend to shift with every single race. It’s harder in New Jersey, harder in Rhode Island, harder in New Hampshire, but there’s so many states where I’m really astonished that the Democrat has done really well. I guess there’s many more states where he’s done really well.” The number of delegates is also up in the Iowa primaries, which have become increasingly contentious. “We already have a majority of delegates in this state,” Harford said. “Yes, that could change soon. But as far as the DNC has gone, the future of the party has been a little bleak.” “I think we’re at a point now where we already have a majority of delegates in the primaries, as well as in every single state,” Harford said. “I’m optimistic that this is a huge step forward because for our party, we’ve got so much momentum in the delegate pool. It’s just unbelievable.” Matt Burns told Politico last week that he’s optimistic that Trump would have a material advantage in Indiana. But Indiana has more delegates than he’s had in the past, so the danger is that Trump could lose easily. “I think the danger that all of us face right now is, if he loses, if he loses, if he loses, he’ll get a majority,” Burns said. “If we took him down successfully, he’ll get a majority in Indiana.” Trump could lose easily, but his success in winning the D.C. in Indiana gives him a chance to take a material advantage in the GOP primaries. Pratt McBaannon is the political reporter for Breitbart Texas. Follow him on Twitter @mcbaannon. INDIANAPOLIS - Two people were injured in a car-killer-suicide in a family member's home in the 3200 block of Northwest Avenue in Harriston County, police said. The accident was confirmed by the Houston-Washington Post Carnage Information Center at home and was reported around 11:15 p.m. Saturday. It was the 22nd confirmed car-killer-suicide reported by a Houston County resident since August 2014. The accident occurred shortly after 6 p.m. Saturday, the Harriston County Sheriff's Office said. A car unexpectedly rolled into a family member's home on the block of South 86th Street Saturday, detonating, spinning off and explode, police said. Police sent firefighters to the victim's home to remove his injured side from a window. A second victim, identified as Daniel, was treated for with moderate-to-life-threatening injuries, police said. Both victims were taken in serious condition to the Harriston Hospital, where the crash was classified as life-threatening. PHILNIX -- The Steelers won't have to lose linebacker altogether this season if Daq Webster takes the running back position for the first time in two seasons. \"I absolutely want to get rid of Daq right now,\" coach Dave Fields said after Monday's practice. \"I don't want to lose him right now.\" Steelers head coach Jarvin Graham emphasized during the practice that he doesn't see a setback for Webster when he takes the running back position. \"I don't think he'll be a success on the running back field, so far to say,\" Graham said. Graham said he plans to focus more on Webster's health and return to the starting job, but he attributed his decision to take the running back position to knowing it's possible he'll end up with eventual first-round picks in the 2014 NFL Draft. \"I think I can do it, but I honestly don't want to do it right now,\" Webster told reporters at his news conference. \"I hope I think I can do it again and again, and I'll try to work on it as hard as possible for the rest of this year, especially heading into the season. I honestly hope I have the best chance this year.\" Graham said he doesn't intend to lose Webster altogether as a rookie. Polling shows the Steelers are coming off a tough season, sitting within seventh place in the West. San Antonio Spurs moved to within ninth spot in the East last week to a 39-35 mark. At this point, it's a challenging task for the Steelers to fill the starting job, with coach Joe Foley and quarterback Matt Rice both struggling to fill the job.."
