        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000220",
        "sourceId": 220,
        "text": "Some tax reductions will enable even the wealthiest and sickest people to save on benefits, pensions, and other programs. Republicans will want to focus more on Medicaid benefits for part-family households, taxes for low income families who want to buy a house, keeping the unemployment rates in check, and extending employment benefits to sick workers with the lowest salaries. Employment benefits are a small slice of the savings and need a lot to pay off. But the sick shouldn't want to spend on taxes. It is wrong to argue that taxes are too high. Until recently, the per capita income of most Americans was a small fraction of the national working class. Today, the working class of America is far wealthier than the rest of us. Figures make it all different. On average, the wealth owned by the ruling elite is $334 billion which is equivalent to that of billionaires. The median income of an average working household in America today encompasses roughly $277,000. The problem with the economy is that the wealth of the richest individual will surge to 13 percent of GDP if the cost of a mortgage falls. About a fifth of today’s workforce makes around a third of the median income. But the savings for American households on a small mortgage over a year would be no more than 20 percent of their median income. What we have today is that our debt burden is partially reduced by taxes. Even the ruling elite that were once successful now own millions of dollars in debt. More than half of America's working class families are sick. Health care, education, and pension services for sick families and their children are being cut for the next several decades. Military families are driving down their incomes by cutting military expenditures and devoted less to child care than to paying pension. The working poor are absent. Increasing military spending to give sick families the added chance to drive down their incomes and therefore reduce the national debt burden is a bad thing to do. Such an approach is indiscriminate and no action by Congress to find a solution simply denies the ruling elites the stability they have already guaranteed us. Republicans are woefully unaware of the link between an entire financial system and a state of poverty. Today’s entire financial system is already becoming completely unusable, leaving zero military spending, creating another crisis of social instability, and leaving the billionaires and the ruling elite under pressure to reduce their bulky incomes. The economic outlook right now is equally arrogant. The world is in the midst a financial recession and a highly sporadic economy that is doomed a state of financial ignorance. Even since the beginning of the twentieth century, stock markets have failed to handle the rare events that have plagued the financial workforce. And in recent years, worst-case events like the Goldman Sachs crisis have proven to be extremely rare despite the stock markets of going to nowhere. Is this what happened in the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis as well as a permanent downturn? Let's take a minute. Proposal that a cyclically warming economy is turning American prosperity on its head is morally disheartening. Without a logical plan, a reduction in inequality will drift toward further recession and unemployment. Uncertainty rates for the national working class and the rest of the world will probably be reduced due to warming. Hence GDP growth will slow markedly within the coming decades. Some have suggested that Americans turn their attention to benefits which allow poor workers to spend more on taxes than they on benefits and to earn healthy pensions. Absent such a plan, levels of inequality can be reduced. It's important to refute claims that the rise in inequality is killing as poor as anyone. In the mid-'90s known as the trans-Pacific crisis, the government successfully managed to keep taxes low. Now it's true when massive cuts have been made in the nation's deficit. We don't want taxes to have been low. Since the wealthy and corporations can create prosperity through wage rises and education reforms, tax cuts are an extension of government benefits. The biggest benefit that the poor can gain from is that they do not need benefits amid rising inequality. So why will the wealthy gain by spending more on taxes than they will the working class? How can they gain from GDP growth by decreasing tax rates? It probably takes a while to break down the relationship between rising inequality and a higher standard of living. But the most effective mechanism in stopping the effects of declining standard of living has been the industrialisation of the wealthy and the reduction in GDP growth. True, global warming might be able to have an effect since income levels for the poorest Americans are already lower than they are today. However, stopping the effects of global warming does not leave us with the goal of increasing the standard of living of the working class."
