        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000283",
        "sourceId": 283,
        "text": "Published Thursday, August 7, 2015 On a coldy afternoon in Sao Paulo, Puerto Rico, Mexico, 38-year-old Peter Smith performed one of the funniest feats ever in the SRI Rugby World Championship competition. The custom-designed ball, laid on a hill, stood around 10 metres tall. Another ball, approximately 11 metres high, also stood on the top. For the final lap, Smith and his team watched as a ‘death truck’ drove through an encircled desert near Puerto Rico, motionless and shaken, but with absolutely no clear plan of where to head. As the truck slowed, the drone approached a pile full of debris. It had an engine cable which could reduce the pile to its maximum. Smith launched it into a hole the next day and he took it for a test. The Oncoming IT Championship “After the results of the week, I decided I would try my hardest to complete the World Championship next year,” Smith said. As soon as he tried, he was amazed at a giant church on the top. He said this was the first time his had seen a massive church. Well, as you know, it’s extremely large and powerful. The steel ball weighed around 25 to 30 nanometres in length itself. The custom-designed steel balls can be fired from different directions which allows them to run anywhere in the same area, thus minimizing the impact and damage of the structure. Smith, who has won twice and three in a row, added: “It’s a great honour to have captured the World Championship, something which has a distinct historical significance and owes its pedigree. “I’m determined that my goal will be achieved by the end of the year. This World Championship thrives on the will of the people and achieves the goal of being the enduring IT Championship.” Smith is also one of the best athletes and coaches to have ever taken part in the sport of artificial intelligence. Mark Fisher, a master artificial intelligence coach, has played at the highest level in history between 1915 and 1900. And he’s prepared to put on a historic robotics show and if proven successful, a drone could be taken for a test. U.S. Rep. Paul Ryan (R-Washa) has introduced legislation that bars state police from helping minorities on a DNA search and says their personal records cannot be shared with law enforcement as long as they're driving in a car. The legislation, superchaired by Rep. Al Carter (D-Mass.), who has long supported limiting the ability of dangerous individuals to access their private personal information, comes at a critical time in the debate about whether or not to. It has prompted a wave of lawsuits in the U.S. Supreme Court aimed at keeping off the DNA collection, fueled by allegations of wrongdoing and fraud. According to the New York Huffington Post, the law's implications for public safety have been a cause for concern among states seeking to block the collection, such as North Carolina. Other states have placed restrictions on the collection. A recent study found banning DNA sample sharing could increase the percentage of Americans searching their private personal information by 15.6 percent. As many as 40 states, including New York and Washington D.C., are expected to consider legislation limiting the collection during legislative session. \"The problem we have right now is there is no collection,\" Rep. Ryan (R-Mass.) told BuzzFeed last year. \"We've got to get the job done.\" House Speaker Nancy Pelosi (D-Ore.) said the House intends to study the effects of limiting the collection in coming days. The watchdog group Americans for the Protection of Democracy has expressed opposition to the legislation, arguing that it makes it easier for people to present their personal records to law enforcement than police. \"We're trying to persuade law enforcement to act,\" Pelosi said in a statement. \"This bill eliminates whoever's directly involved in authorizing a search.\" Ryan also has said that he'd rather allow the legislation to move through the Senate, noting that Senate members could be fined about $25,000 and a smaller penalty if it were approved. 'So little searching' The House speaker, Robert V. Ehl. Schumer (D-N.Y.), warned of the bill's constitutional implications and on Wednesday issued a statement calling on it to reconsider its passage. He said the bill's provision preclude \"So little searching,\" ruling out a section of law enforcement's program of DNA testing, used to determine an individual's mental and physical health. A DNA search cannot be conducted without a driver's license."
