        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000413",
        "sourceId": 413,
        "text": "On Nov. 22, 2017 at the Consumer Electronics Show in Paris, the Japanese-based company revealed a major new look to its line of improved mobile devices including the MacBook Z-family and the U.S. debut iPhone, the Macs Air. The biggest changes will be increased hardware components, a 5.6-inch full HD display, a larger display and a shorter battery life, and improved features that exist with the iPad and MacBook Pro (see 2017's Mobile Device Review). Other improvements include a smoother, brighter laptop design, a smaller processor, brighter graphics for the MacBook Pro, and stronger batteries. But the biggest benefit of the new look is the MacBook Pro's usability. Priceing a reasonable $16.99, the Macs Air has seen progressively reduced sales volume over the past few years, attracting an increasingly younger segment of consumers. It's been more than a year since Apple, the best-known maker of the MacBook laptop, moved up the ladder in its cloud business from a lower opticals position. At first, the Macs Air looked like a higher-priced laptop suited for large TVs and laptops. But just days before the iPad's launch, Apple unveiled a new lower-priced laptop called the Pro 8, a variant of the MacBook Pro Z-family. Following the success of the Apple iPad in 2016, Apple's cloud business has steadily expanded, with an emphasis on small devices and a compact design. In particular, the company's cloud efforts have played a big part in the consumer attention, beginning at the Consumer Electronics Show in mid-September 2016. With the announcement of 25,000 MacBook Pro devices (up from 6,000 a year earlier), it's been reported that Apple plans to produce more batteries to power these devices. This has been a major boost because cloud-based laptops now occupying more than half of America's 1.3 billion annual laptops, according to MacWorld. These numbers don't compare to mobile devices, but consumers do say they have seen a marked increase with the MacBook Pro 8. Thanks to Apple's success of the iPad's Cloud Core processors, these systems give users the ability to process images, graphics and large amounts of data at a glance. When devised for the cloud space, the iPad's Cloud Core processors have delivered a better user experience over the MacBook Pro's better graphics. The most significant change to Amazon's cloud-based laptop line will be the 5, which features a larger, keyboard-structured display that can only be reached with a tap of mouse. Six new models will arrive early 2018. The smaller display will be dominated by four 8-inch cloud TVs -- all intended for online viewing or mobile shopping -- in contrast to the 7-inch Pro 8, which features better, brighter color effects. Other features include D&D-certified sensors and ultra-fast storage. The starting price is $399. With the MacBook Pro 8, Pro 11, Lion and 11 more off the market, Microsoft's software business will largely depend on cloud hardware. Rather, Microsoft has focused on its software strategy of keeping laptops in a smarter position than smartphones running the same hardware. Moreover, the company has launched MacBook Smart Devices, a platform that can take advantage of Amazon's cloud hardware and MacBook's newer iPhone-based processors as well. \"With its cloud business in focus, Amazon has a lot of potential,\" says John Kean, a Duke University professor who now leads Microsoft's software division. \"That is why it targets brands like Sonyura, Samsung and Intel as a leading cloud competitor.\" It's likely that Amazon's efforts to deliver cheap, durable HD to laptops -- although retail prices stay below $589 and its HD-C features are steadily rising -- will also help consumers feel more excited to add a HD card to their purchases. \"It's on Amazon's radar race right now,\" says Robert Cook, chief product officer at a e-commerce research firm. Cook thinks consumers are excited about the effort ahead of Amazon's launch. \"We're still looking at our sales volume at this point,\" he says. \"It's on Amazon's radar among all bets from e-commerce makers, so I'm sure they'll continue to stick with it.\" Fiona Goskin, a Galaxy X user, is excited about the new MacBook Z-series and MacBook Pro 8, but argues the two devices should be able to offer more than a clean, state-of-the-art user experience. Goskin compares Apple's new Galaxy X laptop to Amazon's touch-on-the-shelf laptop..."
