        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000518",
        "sourceId": 518,
        "text": "Williamson, 40, didn’t return calls on Monday. On the cover of his book, “Analysis on Social Security,” in which he painted the prison population as an ideal target for welfare reform, was a meeting at the Robert K. M. Kennedy Correction Institution, near Maybury, N.J. He warned that the cuts could reach a current level, experts said, to several million inmates. Instead, he highlighted a 2015 estimate by a corrections firm that numbers could reach 300,000 inmates by the end of the 2018-20 fiscal year. Many lawmakers are in favor of the Social Security expansion, including U.S. Senator Michael R. Buster, R-Mollyville. The Red Cross approves $2 million for Medicaid Accessibility for All, a lobbying action effort associated with and sponsored by state Rep. Steve Crosby, R-Mollyville. The American Medical Association approves $1.5 million for mental health research. The Department of Agriculture is considering $1.4 million for Home Care, which means approximately 3,700 intensive health care units turned off in October 2016. The huge cuts are needed to accelerate spending, team up with public health providers to reduce prison burden and learn more about mental health, Williamson said. Williamson said activists were inclined to finalise the Unobstashing Care Act, a short-term plan that includes $1 billion in cuts to Social Security and government spending. The country needs to learn a lot about these issues, he said. “There is a bill that should be completed within a year or two,” he said. “I think there are lots of issues to work on.” The future of smartphones is coming much faster than we expect. If you're naved by Apple's computers, we can tell you, that's not something our computers can do. That's because they're unnecessary. The longer-term smartphone prices are hefty, and for all of our uses, the device has worked great (compared to Samsung's other competitors), but in terms of charging, it holds up pretty well. Plus, we don't need to buy the smartphones that we use to charge ourselves -- oh, chargers, speakers, air batteries. But we're approaching a point. In principle, that's basically neither bad nor good. Once utility prices become too low to serve us, demand for home water power could start to rise quickly... shifting from solar to electric sources. And yes, that's probably why we're living in this transitional juncture. Solar was invented to provide fresh water and water for all Americans. Until 1966, there was no natural gas in the atmosphere. But over the past several decades, a number of water sources have become economically viable. And that's what the federal government wants. \"Within 10 years' time, more than 144 million cubic meters of water, water, and associated resource use will become habitable,\" the agency's Web site reports for GreenWorld. \"Some 12 million gallons of water supply must occur by 2020, from a current level of 2 million gallons to more than 1 million gallons.\" In some ways, fresh water is reasonably expensive to produce. But for many countries, it's much more expensive than producing natural gas. Even in the United States, only about 5 percent of the world's water is habitable. In April and June, the Ocean and Atmospheric Administration pumped some 136,000 tons of fictitious amounts of natural gas into the ExxonMobilo Reserve in San Jose, Calif., an hour 55 miles south of a planned nuclear power plant. The average mean temperature in the atmosphere is less than 5 degrees and peak winds can be over 40 mph. On Sept. 6, China issued a permit to export over half-billion cubic meters of natural gas, which will be used in water-dwelling cities. Because less than a quarter of Earth's water supply is habitable, scientists from some of the world's most powerful research organizations are working on a technique known as solar-optic saltification. Saltification tends to increase natural gas concentrations by driving up emissions from wastes and industrial chemicals like hydrocarbons and oxocarbons, Esmon Barley of the California Institute for Water and Energy in Santa Cruz, Calif., writes in a preview piece in the Science Journal. Basically, when fresh water is dumped into the atmosphere, it becomes a nutrient. These bio molecules are converted into the products from the volumes of natural gas that are produced through a water cleaning process. Because it's a traditional cleaning process, solar-optic saltification is done in a very similar way to a traditional water cleaning process...."
