        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000576",
        "sourceId": 576,
        "text": "Last 2009 Microsoft announced an open Beta release for PS4, along with a multiplayer version on Windows 10. Just over a year later, it was rumored a full Beta was coming on both PS4, Xbox One and PC, with several interesting reports detailing how this move was made. Now it looks as though Microsoft sort of didn’t wait, and the announcement available for many was just over a year ago. As well as the launching of many gaming titles on Xbox One, there’s now several additional gaming titles available for all to play on Xbox One, although last September’s announcement of the full Beta on Xbox TV went a long way as well. Open Beta type of gaming is pretty good at that, with a few minor ripoffs on surprisingly popular titles such as Star Wars: Generation and Force Flash. Open Beta, on the other hand, still boasts the coveted Windows 10 equivalents thanks to the additional support for both the PC and mobile titles, as well as the massive streaming opportunities it offers. While 2009 was a blip for Microsoft, console games were available on PS4, Xbox One, Xbox PC, MultiGames, PS4, Windows 360, and PC. The downside, however, was that these games ran significantly longer than the individual Xbox One titles streamd by Microsoft, thereby preventing Microsoft from launching a console franchise in its own right. So with the launching of Xbox PC titles, Microsoft utilized this opportunity to stream a larger selection of games, reducing the burden on each device. As a bonus, Xbox mobile games ran a shorter jig than other Xbox games, though perhaps just a bit longer on home devices including Windows (less than on Windows 10, to be exact). Mobile games running on Windows 10 was almost four times as long on Xbox One, while Xbox PC lasted twice as long. It’s nice to see Microsoft make such strides, now that Microsoft has just released its own separate Xbox One version of exclusively mobile games. One thing that stands out in this regard is the ability to enjoy multiple games on different consoles. It’s hard to dispute that, as a Microsoft fan, mobile games took place exclusively on Xbox One. Equally unfortunate, though, we also weren’t that surprised to see the announcements on Xbox Home games. Microsoft wasn’t exactly the first company to actually break into that tiny console space, but its massive home screen looked like quite the first. There are almost as many Velvet, Nvidia, and Nintendo games now available over Sony, Vita, Xbox, Playstation, and other third-party titles on Windows 10, meaning Microsoft is the only one company trying to capitalize on the power of the home screen. There’s much to be said about Microsoft’s entry into the tiny home gaming space, but while Microsoft has been known a proponent of the home screen in recent years, Microsoft appears to have reached out to SteamForce to figure out how best to refocus its efforts on Xbox Home games. Microsoft does have significant sales, but this is still an early stage in Microsoft’s planning to bring the desktop business to an end. Microsoft started off by making mobile games available on the iPhone and iPad, and soon targeted its own release. As sales soared, this move prevented Microsoft from building much of a worldwide presence. Xbox Home games along with X-Men, a multiplayer gaming franchise for Windows 360, first hit the shelves on Xbox last year. Though Home debuted on Windows 10 exactly the same year the game was released, there’s no question that the game hasn’t been completed until Windows 10 next month. With Microsoft returning to Windows 10, it’s great to see Xbox Home making its way to the horizon, even though the game has been around for just over a year. The development of Xbox Home games was so fun and exciting that it’s actually taken quite a few years to make it to market, but we’re happy to see Microsoft make some headway on this front. Be sure to catch a taste below from Microsoft’s official press conference, and check out the full update on Xbox Home games on Twitter. What’s another rumor story like? Leave us comments. A new name of this building is found inside the skeleton of the ancient Zhenjian temple at Kuza Temple. Commonly known as Kuza Kapi, the also known name of this building stretches to about 500 CE when it was known as Hunju, meaning as a ritual building during the Zhenjian period. This building was a traditional rite among the modern day Hunju people, with three large entrances. The floor is organized with a 2-meter-high Hunju-shaped stone, with holes tucked inside to create a stone-like, circle-like shape."
