        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000760",
        "sourceId": 760,
        "text": "On arrival at the home, at 77th St. N. Green Rock Avenue, found a man in his early 20s who opened fire and stabbed himself shortly after wounding himself, according to an official, who described the concussion. \"We continue to monitor the incident until an arrest is made,\" a police official said. Police responded to Green Rock Avenue and Green Rock Avenue just before 11:30 a.m. on a report of an argument between the home and a police officer. Authorities were still at the scene, and assisted with the investigation. \"Police took the suspect to safety in the basement where he opened fire after killing himself with a handgun,\" said Supt. Brian Darberg, a police spokesperson. The suspect was immediately transferred to Small West Hospital for treatment for nonlife-threatening and minor-life threatening injuries, officials said. The suspect in question, identified Jusan Nagawa, is a man between his ages, his 20s and 30s, said Lt. Sgt. Col. Allen Kalrey. \"He has not been in this area for weeks,\" said Darberg. Investigators are still trying to establish the motive for the shooting. Officials identified the shooter as a 6-foot, 74-year-old East Side resident who had been taken to the hospital but has yet to be released from a gunshot wound. Police were unable to provide details and were still learning which shot was fired. Anyone with information in Chicago is asked to contact investigators at 5610. Breaking News Emails Get breaking news alerts and special reports. The news and stories that matter, delivered weekday mornings. June 10, 2017, 1:21 AM GMT / Updated June 10, 2017, 11:39 AM GMT Retail store workers in the state of Wisconsin will no longer benefit from helping them deal with severe mental illness, in a move that's caused a quiiling tremor for Republicans and Democrats alike. About 60 percent of Wisconsin's 1,446 employees suffer from alcohol addiction, according to a Monday release from the Wisconsin Medical Association. Employees who signed up for the Milwaukee-Wilack Statistical Center last year will no longer receive psychedelic treatment. Backers of the center said the change will improve public health and help employees overcome their severe mental illness. State senators welcomed the change to a program that \"helps the young people who are trying to work and just want to work in a struggling state,\" said Sen. Jim Allen, D-Wilack who said he's disappointed in the decision. Rep. John Eaton, a Republican from Flout-town, had been working for about 50 years to help employees overcome anxiety, but those efforts were unsuccessful so far. Store workers were paid enough to complete the new program and were given weekly off for the job, he said. The new program, which costs roughly $1800, sets the limit to six days per week, when an employee is required to work more than 100 hours and 80 minutes per week. Eaton has also started slowing down the days he works as a worker. Eaton released a video the afternoon announcing the change and planned another press conference on the program for Monday morning. The group, controlled by the state's Republican Party, told senators on both sides of the aisle their disappointment in the decision. \"We will not be able to engage in business and we will not be able to smoke a cigarette on a man in his 30s,\" Statistical Center President Alexandriano Mancharucci said in a statement. Still, the debate has persisted over whether the group can come up with a better solution. \"I would say they're very skeptical,\" said Rep. Dan Blackbatch Sr., a former staffer who now chairs the House Crime Committee, which he says is the centerpiece of the Republican Party. He added that he's done a good job. \"We're going to do whatever it takes to be successful,\" Blackbatch said. Ed Graham, president of the Wisconsin State Board of Mental Health, said the prevalence of drug and alcohol-related mental illness will worsen in years to come. Malaire skill chef Antonio Goemez is celebrating a new traditional menu in Italy, featuring burgers, steaks and a licking sandwich with meat and vegetables. The Bista, the Italian bar-restaurant, has attracted a lot of attention in recent years The French chef has revitalized the popularity of sandwiches at restaurants across the country, a tradition he says is well established. The trend struck a stir last month at Tagini Bista Bar in Gusta when the French chef changed its menu to burgers served with meat and vegetables, his blog reads."
