        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_32_nfe-f504867cdffc-000789",
        "sourceId": 789,
        "text": "In swing states like Pennsylvania, Barack Obama holds a 12-point lead over Sanders by a sizable seven points, which means if they fail to gain a significant lead, Sanders will have to go to the polls, which could put the Democrats at the top of the ticket next year. Even if that's the case, each state's success is indicative of how well-understood the Democratic Party has been. While Bush donated $81 million to Clinton's campaign in Pennsylvania, Obama gives another $42 million to Sanders. But going into the primaries in Northern Pennsylvania and other states, Democrats won't see an overall bump, said Ronald Caberson, a professor of media and political science at the University of Pennsylvania who specializes in politics and economics. \"There's plenty of areas that the Democratic Party needs to focus on winning in Northern Pennsylvania, but the Clintons in Northern Pennsylvania are the ones they need,\" Caberson said. \"Within Pennsylvania, the Clinton campaign needed to win in Northern Pennsylvania. The areas that the party needs to focus on are big shake-up states, so it's one of those areas where the Democratic Party has the best shot at the nomination.\" Because Sanders has repeatedly indicated he wants to win the nomination, though, Apple is playing a price on its preferred ticket, and Clinton's poor performance in Northern Pennsylvania indicates that is not a realistic measure of success. Apple has decided to end its relations with Apple as it prepared for the rights it will hold to its soon-to-launch 7.0-inch iPad Pro display. \"Apple, the company leading the development and manufacturing of the new iPad and Surface laptops, today announced an end of relations with Apple Inc. and a renewed commitment to an improved, more efficient manufacturing process for the production of new laptops,\" Apple chief financial officer, Stephen Cookt, told Reuters in a statement. The Apple deal will end in earnest, although the company has not said when it plans to stop it. Apple's deal is it's largest acquisition deal in nearly 30 years. The iPad stock is estimated to be around $25 billion. Apple's acquisition buys the majority of iPad's stock. According to Reuters, an iPad.com stock analytics firm, share in iPad is expected to grow by around 1.7% over the next 20 years. Apple's ongoing investments in it include \"new products and services, energetic start-to-end growth and mass sales of new brands.\" Apple's acquisition is expected to improve the market value of the laptops. The company is not likely to spend the majority of its operating profits on the tablets. About half of Apple's investments will go on sales of Pro-related devices. Apple will report on sales of next-generation iPad Pro products in the second half of 2017. Cookt didn't say any specifics about what the deal means. \"The iPad Pro probably won't be as popular as the next generation of tablets,\" he said. \"It's important that Apple remain focused on research, development and marketing efforts for the iPad Pro.\" According to the report, Apple expects success of the Pro to be dependent on the amount of capital it will spend on new products like the Pro and the iPad, which it says is detrimental to the 40,000-share wealthy company. Apple's report estimates it will cost between $2 and $5.7 billion to secure a full patent right to the company's 7.0-inch iPad Pro display. But that figure only applies to the cheaper devices the company has started offering in recent years, such as the Surface mini and 4.2-inch Surface 3. It's likely more of a optimistic figure for Apple given how much the new devices will cost out. As of press time, Apple said it had only added new features for the iPad and Surface mini as well as canceled a few of the more expensive models. WASHINGTON/Jan. 11 (Reuters) - The Americans are working to persuade militant Libyan Mumar Gaddafi to join the Islamic State of Iraq (IS), an administration official said on Wednesday. Gaddafi was cleared by the United Nations Immigrant and Refugee Commission (UNCR). He has spent more than four years behind bars and more than two years as countermilitary for the U.S. military. He was reportedly imprisoned by the IS in Baghdad. Gaddafi's lawyers want the American government to refuse his eligibility as a public citizen as he is the first exiled activist jailed in the United States since 2011. Over the past 12 years he has crossed the line. He was converted to Libyan with the aim - to build a better society - following his expulsion in February. He is now in detention after conducting an evaluation with the FBI."
