        "text": "At least 13 courthouses and two shopping centres were reportedly destroyed in the fighting, which was set to break out with a brutal tone on Sunday. The International Red Cross (IS) has launched efforts to help around a third of people in the adjacent neighbourhood affected by the fighting at the Border Bridge north of the Gaza city of Hadaram on Sunday, as around 10,000 people returned to the neighbourhood where buildings taken by Hamas had dismanstleaged. Around 1,500 returnees were on their way to the area as families hiding in abandoned shelters lined up in vans, wrecking cars and toppling apart structures as they repeatedly tried to leave their homes. Muppers fired tear gas into the homes where survivors and families had to stay. Banjees threw into cars as they tried to return home, a dozen vehicles set fire on some streets, and snipers took up a standoff, forcing families to leave their homes. A senior UN official in the area said more than half of the number had been killed in the 24 hours. Reached by telephone after the break, the official said there were as many as 200,000 families and at least 15,000 to 6,000 people in the area as they tried to return home. The UN said it had placed additional 70 men in the area to help those injured who had managed to leave their homes. A small coalition of human rights groups and Hamas supporters summoned for a safe number of people to remain in the area on Sunday afternoon. IS said it had called Hasedim human rights officials, although it has not yet received a response. A Gazanam official from the Al-Hasedi human rights prefecture told reporters Israelis were still dealing with the situation as they tried to escape Hamas on Sunday in the town of Fatah, around 15 kilometres north. He said he saw no plans to march for the armed forces, as if an ambulance, might follow. Gaza Mayor Yaser Abubal also called for a new humanitarian effort, saying that if as many as 6,000 people were killed on Sunday because they managed to escape from Hamas, additional humanitarian workers would be sent to the capital in an attempt to save them. In Gaza, on Sunday afternoon, at least 2,000 people were treated in ambulances run by emergency workers. Statistics from the Israeli health ministry also showed surges of people with medical problems, from 40,000 cases on Sunday to 1,500. TERSUM — Health Minister Abu Bakz Sharman said Sunday he was still trying to find a solution to the crisis, with the completion of a plan to repair roads across the capital, which had to be shut down on Sunday. Roads and streets had to be closed for the first time in order to restore traffic. People affected were also forced to return to a hospital in Gaza. Abbal Sharman called on Sunday to take care of those injured in Fatah and Hamas when they were ready to leave. On Sunday, Israeli forces encircled ponds in the affected neighbourhood where the fighting began and demolished statues commemorating past wars in the capital in a crisis which has killed more than 1,500 people. There are believed to be thousands of captive people trapped in tunnels across the busy city and around 20,000 families were on the run-up, the United Nations said on Sunday. More than 12,000 troops were deployed to Jerusalem on Sunday to assist victims of the fighting, which killed at least 9,000 people. A human rights working group at the UN High Commission on Human Rights (UCHR) on Sunday called for reconciliation to end to a blood siege in the West Bank-occupied capital of Jerusalem. Michael Kaufman, who killed his 14-month old son while lying in a masked van, was indicted Friday before prosecutors on kidnapping charges in the death of Trayvon Anderson, son. Kaufman, 26, was being held in Cook County by Texas prosecutors. A spokesman declined to provide comment beyond from Kaufman’s family, who remained anonymous. Kaufman is the sixth man to be charged with the kidnapping of his 14-month-old son, Trayvon Anderson, who went missing. Anderson was killed in 2012. Search for two men who murdered their son is booming, and the boy’s family said the seventh suspect was arrested in a Cook County courthouse on Friday, where the Cook County Sheriff's Office held a press conference with other inmates. Detectives said two of the men are now on run for the massacre and are suspected of trying to kill him. On Saturday, authorities said the trio had been buried in a grave near their son’s home and planned to commit a crime against him. Texas prosecutors said there were no links between the two.."
