        "sourceId": 1019,
        "text": "In many areas, there is no place for employed people to buy homes, often from homeless families, said Patrick Bellevy, executive director of the Pittsburgh Neighborhood Association, the nation's largest coalition of property developers. It can take between five and 10 years to buy land, he said, and it can cost more than $2 million a year in some neighborhoods. Still, there are very few developers in areas where unemployment is high, according to Michael Adams, policy director of the University of Pennsylvania's Center for Housing and Development. \"There are places for working people,\" he said. But certain locations can be dangerous. Bellevy said that in neighborhoods where more than 40 people are employed, developers won't even have to use a building block the size of a typical downtown office space. \"Most of these problems are happening in smaller areas,\" he said. Adams said the city's planning and design departments will need to come up with better ways to make sure their homes are safe to rent and use. Some cities already have laws limiting building size, he said, especially where a mayor said measures will benefit developers in a \"difficult, dangerous location.\" Bellevy said one common view is that when a neighborhood has very little development, employees don't have to sell. He said that view can be dangerous. \"I would say for them, that's kind of like a small buyout as opposed to the reality of a large buyout.\" Some cities are working on equality restrictions for their own taxes, including a property tax known as ampton tax, which allows developers to refund real estate assets for wealth. But Scott Sagu, executive director of the Center for Community Development in Pittsburgh, said neighborhoods typically have no income tax, because if house prices fall to zero, that means the price will not rise. \"Unfortunately, that is not possible,\" Sagu said, and that neighborhoods are dealing with a variety of challenges. \"It would be great to see it happen,\" he said. \"I would love to see what income tax laws that would be in place.\" Losing ground Sagu also stressed that there is a shortage of developers in the area, and that the home buy gap is so big that developers aren't going to give up on buying land. \"If there are too many developers, there's going to be a ton of the market and a ton of people who're just trying to buy land that can be where they need to,\" he said. \"I think that's the crux of the problem.\" Sagu said owners should also be cautious about economic trends. \"Of course, if the economy is going to grow and it's going to grow, you wouldn't see a ton of.\" If there is a surplus of development, he warned, it'll outweigh the estimated cost of low-development projects like North Ampton Point, which covers hundreds of miles of highway connecting Chicago and Detroit. It could be more than $400 billion. Most of the home buys in recent years were financed by projects planned by owners who want to be as low-manufactured as possible. But Merier said the estimated cost of building new homes won't be that low. \"In Chicago, or in Milwaukee, it's going to be way too high,\" he said. \"It's not a big shock to see what's happening in Pittsburgh. If it's hyper-urban, it's kind of crazy.\" He said it would help to know who are likely to be building new homes in the years ahead. \"Just crossing the river is going to be a big signal of what's happening in Pittsburgh,\" Merier said. \"It would be a good signal for the people that we've seen are going to be building homes in the years ahead.\" Community leaders generally support changes to be made in the Pittsburgh Housing Plan, which determines how many new homes should be built in the area. But they also dislike a limit on how many owners can build that was imposed by Council, and want city officials to use a list of development priorities to plan projects they want to build. \"Nobody wants to buy their home, but it's going to happen in Pittsburgh,\" Merier said. \"In the end, it will really depend on what long-term vision people have for their homes.\" Two taxi drivers pull a bag load from a tunnel at a tetro stop on State Highway Freeway, Nov. 9, 2015. OVERTH, S.C. — Anyone driving on the new S.C. Freeway Highway knows one simple thing. They start with their car — at 1,300 miles per hour — and now, for the first time, it’s gone. It’s that way."
