        "text": "BOSTON -- Celtic center Dion Bulievski said he's back on the floor from a left knee injury in his second day of practice, hoping he can play well. The former sixth round pick (41st on ESPN's depth chart) played his first NBA game on the floor on Tuesday -- a game against the San Antonio Spurs. The 6-foot-7 20-pound center has averaged 18.1 points per attempt in his past 28 games as a Celtic, but's pulled just three assists in 17 of his 18 games this past season. Tuesday's practice game wasn't nearly as productive as some of those games. It was the second time he has been in the rim in his last three outings against the Spurs. But even after suffering a hand issue this month against the Boston Celtics, Bulievski said he's still hoping to play well on the floor. “It’s great to see how good I feel with the ball,” Bulievski said Monday. “There were nights where I felt a little tired, unable to draw shots. I don’t know why.” Bulievski, who entered practice Sunday at the Amami Sports Center, missed his last start with a leg injury that knocked him off the rim in just 10 of his 11 NBA games this season, allowing him to shoot at a rate of 9.7 minutes per attempt. But -- since entering practice -- it remains unclear when the center will be back on the floor for tonight's game against the Indiana Pacers. “I’ve only played some games, but not one where I was unable to shoot,” Bulievski said. “It’s important to me that if I’m trying to position on the ball, I don’t want to be able to keep it the same. All those injuries can be uncomfortable with, but when you get something like that, it’s really uncomfortable for you.” Both King, Jackson and Lovedard expressed hope for Bulievski to do well in their upcoming outings against the Pacers. “I think it’ll be interesting again tonight. I’m really great to see how James is feeling about his injury,” Bulievski said. “I’m hoping hopefully he’s in good shape tonight, maybe he can play some more games as well. I think these past couple two weeks that he’s been out was what’s really helped for him.” Bulievski also takes advantage of some of the benefits of being in the lineup and being able to do it. “Playing basketball with guys like [Christian] Landrow, [John] Taylor, and guys like James King is a nice thing,” Bulievski said. “I think it will be really great for me to just get a little better and be able to do it. I think I can capitalize on some of the benefits of being in the lineup and being able to do it. It’s really nice for me to be a lineup player for the Celtics.” Bulievski has only been able to switch to the rim with his teammates over the past four seasons, but he usually tightens the center when he's not on the rim. “I don’t think there was a bias in having him take the rim as hard as he can,” Celtics coach Dion Carter said. “Some of the times he feels like he touches the ball, he's taking shots. He knew he wanted to take the rim as hard as he could at that point, and that's what he chose.” Bulievski, who's appeared in 63 games the past three seasons, suffered a lower leg injury, but didn't see any action until Monday. In April, the Celtics took starting center Daniel Arsson out of the lineup after a long injury battle. Arsson was subsequently given the Celtics' lone pick in the 2014 draft, but was gone through negotiations, according to Celtics GM Greg Thomas. “We took just five, I think the sixth pick,” Boston star Stephen Jackson said. “It didn’t go well, but it was a good play, and he was able to get some shots. Going back to the beginning of last season, he could have done a better job of putting pressure on [Brud] Cunningham, and he could have hit him with his leg. That was really part of the plan to keep Arsson positioned for the ball, but it was really not going to make any difference. It was a ton of different. It was not a big part of today’s plan.” ”"
