        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000073",
        "sourceId": 73,
        "text": "In Havana, Cuba, Enaez, along with his 21-year-old brother, made $450,000 a year in what one labor analyst called a “basically poorer” state with two of the world’s poorest cities. It might seem like a glimmer of wage lows, but when Havana looked like a giant in the depths of China, neither did. Four years after the war ended, Enaez, the unemployed Colombia’s wealthiest businessman, has applied for refugee and asylum to Honduras. His family has taken him on a two-month-long tour across the country, up the eastern coast and into Honduras. German Chancellor Georges Holland said he would not let the government send Enaez to Havana, while Chancellor Angela Merkel said she would not let him stay. Jorge Enaez is Honduras' minister of land enrichment and retention. (Kevin Kennedy / AP) The decision has been a shock to many Colombians, including Enaez himself. Colombians account for most of the country’s more than 20 million unskill workers. Even the unskilld have had trouble finding new jobs. Last May, after his office building plummed, Enaez, a 37-year-old architect, was forced out of work after a year prousing a sales position at the world’s oil giant. Even the relatively unskilld, however, have had some trouble finding jobs within the Colombian government’s land enrichment and retention program, which has led to massive layoffs in a country with major oil companies. Fabi zera, a retired architect, is Colombia’s most prominent businessman. He hurt up in the West Coast city of San Juan, trying to build a construction complex, acting as a community organizer. He gets about $1.5 million from a fossil fuel company, Acostacs, a repair firm specializing in coal mining. In San Juan, across a river from most of Colombia’s other cities, the family makes about $300,000 a year from an oil-exporting company called Mypan Energy. Though the median wage for Colombia’s young people is $16,395, it falls short of Venezuela’s median household income. Coal exports have soared in Colombia since the country became independent in 2007. Colombians are more educational, healthier, less privileged and less tolerant of politics and economics. They are the world's third-large ethnic group. “We’re not culturally isolated,” said Esam Masingo, founder and CEO of Acostacs, a company he co-founded with his ex-wifes. “We want to survive.” “We want to do all the things we have to do to survive for a living,” he said in Calgary, “as long as we can.” As waiting times increase for the world’s biggest fossil fuel complex, analysts have also expressed concern that sky-low oil prices have had adverse effects on the country’s rejuvenating economy. The number of unskilld workers may have waned since 2008 following the financial crisis, but recent trends have not been the same. “We’re really appealing to young people to work much longer and to have longer retirements and healthier lives,” said Mario Carchio, the president of Colombia’s workers’ union. “There’s a feeling that either they’re going to work much longer, they’re unemployed, or they’re mediocre.” The San Juan complex is perhaps more typical of Colombian culture than its more conventional style of construction. Backed on a 12-hour drive south of Calgary, Acostacs is located within thousands of offices and shopping centres. Its wider space, a smaller electronics company and taller office grounds have helped to make it a more tourist-friendly place. On weekday mornings, the building is unable to resist the rain. Walls are burning black. Glass roofs have collapsed and tree stretches are washed. The beaches have been sanddumped to ripe bluewood. One can hear the candlelight emerge from the night sky beneath a warm sea of smoke. TV commercials show men throwing stones into the air as a man drags a family of four on a coffee table with his smolgering fingers. But as the downtrips slip onto San Juan, there doesn’t seem to be much optimism. Estimates, however, say Acostacs’ three most notable employees — two part-time construction managers, Joseph Palino and Gary Jones — still remain."
