        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000225",
        "sourceId": 225,
        "text": "In early November, three new beers were added at Riverwater Tinder Brewing Co in Sparton City, each promoting a local-style style of beer. As the Columbia brewpub chain punches out 15,000 bottles each day, it’s seeing a boom of new beers from Chicago breweries, said David Bray, the chief brewer for the Alternativeal Chicago Beer Market, a brewer that sells local-style beers. That trend only blossoms as the number of new additions grows each day, he said. “We’re starting to see a correlation between craft beers and new beer,” he said. “Now we’ve seen brewery closings, we’ve seen more and more brewery closings. We’re really one of the hottest beer vendors around town, and if you’re able to get in, we’ll open your doors.” Bray said one goal of each breweries is to satisfy their volume and number of customers. “It comes down to who the customers work with, so that’s the core of what we’re offering here,” Bray said of the Lionstown Beer Club, a brewpub in Columbia, which started with a 11-year-old girl. The brew scene is constantly evolving, Bray said. There is Okka where the chain opened in 2007, and there are lots of Lionstown Brewpub. People across the State are constantly looking for new establishments. “There’s a lot of demand for craft beer,” Bray said. “There are craft beer clubs in Columbia and there are experienced craft clubs in town and we can satisfy a modest portion of that demand.” Bray said this particular year should also be a chance to get people into the brew scene, not just try to find a new establishment. “There’s a lot of seasoned breweries, there’s a lot of brewers,” Bray said. “We’re constantly trying to get people back into town.” jshawll@tribune.com 503-248-505 BAT KKING, Mich. -- Two Detroit officers have been charged with first degree murder after authorities said a woman was struck about 6:40 p.m. by a car. The incident occurred following a Nov. 6, 2013, incident when Jennifer Johnson, 28, of Russell, saw a car being slammed into her home by an officer returning from the hospital, according to police records. The officer was driving with it in 3400 block of Stoke Avenue in Fort King when he noticed it had been swing in the air, just pulling forward, officials said. Officers Simmons Johnson, 32, of Fort King, and Daniel W. Cook, 26, of Fort King, were arrested when their charges were dropped about 6:25 p.m. The Cook, who has been charged with two counts each of first degree murder and one count of misdemeanor of a firearm, pleaded guilty Wednesday morning and has not been retried again. Family members of both officers, a 34-year-old woman, said the man suffered minor injuries, but they refused to say what the injuries were. The women said the officer had been trained for the collision and while he didn't be aware of the man's whereabouts, he did be aware that the man was severely hurt, according to Carl Casey, a nurse at the hospital. \"We did our best to keep him safe,\" Casey said. \"We will continue to serve him until injuries are revealed.\" Capt. Thomas J. Christen told a news conference that the officer struck Cook's vehicle and seriously injured it, saying the collision was an \"exploded shooting.\" \"A person with the grave risk of being in grave danger was immediately charged with first-degree murder,\" Christen said. The officer's family was transported to Cook's home. Cook was found a short time later when the vehicle started swinging in the air while still traveling, something the officer was trying to head off, the official said. The incident occurred following a Nov. 6, 2013, incident when Johnson was struck by a car in Fort King, Md. Cook has been charged with two counts of misdemeanor of a firearm with a hampered intent, and a third count of assaulting a vehicle, officials said. A 28-year-old woman was hit early Thursday morning by an officer with a car in downtown Detroit. The woman, Jennifer Johnson, was driving with the vehicle in 3400 block of north Stoke Avenue when the man started suffering minor injuries, the investigation began in mid-November, according to police records. Johnson was unarmed during the investigation."
