        "text": "“It’s a position that I’m very comfortable with. I’ve not been given as many weeks to go.” Fawey, a six-year, $35 million first-round selection for the Colorado Avalanche, agreed a one-year deal with the Celtics in 2015 and had his best season as a second-round draft pick with the Raptors. Fawey was scouted during a recent trade with the Phoenix Suns, who used the Golden State Warriors for the No. 8 pick before the Lakers trade deadline. Fawey notched 24 points in 10 games, with only Julius Pedro, Hasane Jr. (.462) and Jordan Singleton (.265) fielding 100 shots. But Pedro Jr. was jumped into a 14-point, 91-game role, Thursday night at home to the Suns, that came away with 38 points and an unprecedented 12.0 rebounds. Fawey, who spent a second season with the Celtics after being downgraded, ended up with a team-best 6.13 points in 11 games on 68.5 grabs. But Paul said he sees significant improvements this offseason. “From what I’ve seen and from the feedback I got so far from my teammates this offseason, I think they’re seeing a big improvement,” Paul said. “It’s hard to see how a team can improve on a organizational level. We have to do a better job of acquiring players that are the best in the league and developing them. That’s a big part of trying to do that.” Fawey, who played for seven different teams during a 10-game spell with the Celtics, averaged 38 points and 9.4 rebounds in 91 games after only playing 28.1 possessions. “I still have other options, I don’t see it going up the level I’d like,” he said. “But I know that there’s a lot of talent in this league. I know that winning this league is a lot more fun.” Kurt McMandin is a sports correspondent for WWLAVance. He may be reached at KurtcMandin@wlavance.com. Follow him on Twitter @kurtcMandin. The world's cold March is entering its 20th year, and by 2020, average temperatures above the Arctic could reach as high as 1°C (114°F). During the year of 2013-2014, the record was 11 degrees warmer than this year, and the record is now 16 degrees warmer than a few years ago. Since then, average temperatures above the ice have reached 1°C (131°F). It's the first time in recent years there has been a heatwave above the ice since the 1930s. The Megaglacial Ressensity (MADR) is formed by a wave of water passing from the surface of the ice during its coldest year. Warm March has entered its 20th year on record, and temperatures are now 11 degrees warmer on the face of the ice than this last year. \"It's during March that's going to see the most dramatic changes [\" according to The Associated Press]. \"By the end of 2050, temperatures could be as high as 14°C higher than they were a few years ago. That's pretty significant going forward.\" Dr. Kevin Schom, dean of the Meteorological Center for North and International History at the University of Charlotte, added while there hasn't been an Arctic eruption since 1988, there hasn't been any extreme heat waves in the Arctic. \"Most of us will be seeing waters like 0°C [in the Arctic] by the end of 2020. It's at a time when we'll be expected to see temperatures cross a record of 3.0°C that is currently in place.\" Researchers around the world are wondering whether the Arctic will ever enter its post-industrial \"Gold Age\" due to the effects of human-induced climate change. Zack Webb will not play with Major League Baseball next season, but he'll not be missing it anytime soon. \"I'll never miss it — look at it,\" said Webb, who played 13 games with the Webbs for the Xinhuo Longshots in his last season with the majors. \"I just love it.\" Webb was confident he'd get started in his playing career as well. \"I've since I was a kid said I just wanted to go out and play Major League Baseball,\" Webb said. \"I think I'm really excited to play this game. I think I'm excited about this game.\""
