        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000334",
        "sourceId": 334,
        "text": "Employment is projected to be above the peak of the recession over the next three months, but are likely to see only a slightly greater recovery than had recovered during the first two quarters of this year. Employment is projected to climb to the highest levels in present-day history, but a recovery of 0.4% from the recession and a recovery of 1.3% are awaited. The unemployment rate is still slightly below historic pre-cession levels, but its growth will remain close to the level it has been for the past two years until the end of the current financial year. The worst-case scenario during the second-half will be a reduction in total capital gains, with a reduction to corporate income taxes and a reduction in government spending and public debt. Economic growth is expected to continue to struggle during the second-half, but the big-term personal tax rate will rise from 22.1% in 2015 to 37.9% in 2020, with a two-third reduction in government spending pending. Meanwhile, the U.S. budget deficit is expected to swelter to at least a seven-year low over the past year, declining much of the country’s growth and putting many of its budget deficits under grave threat. Even worse news, the national debt will reduce from an average of 1.2% in 2015 to a new high of 1.6% in 2020, with a reduction to corporate income taxes and an increase in government spending and public debt pending. The worst-case scenario during the second-half will be a reduction in total amount of capital gains, with a reduction to corporate income taxes and a reduction in government spending. Unemployment is expected to rise during the first two quarters of this year, but its growth is expected to revert to levels that rise in the past two years as labor activity declines and incomes continue to soar. By the end of the year, the unemployment rate remains below 5%. Despite these trends, household income growth will continue to decline, from a low of 0.4% in 2015 to a high of 2.7% at the beginning of the year. Real-term, economic growth will be slower during the second-half, but will remain steady until the end of this year. A two-third budget deficit will put economic growth under threat, an increase in oil prices and a decline in crude production — a alarming sign of what could come next in the fiscal year as a whole. If you like the content of this article, you can follow Business Inside the Economy on Twitter, Facebook, Instagram, or subscribe to our weekly newsletter. People who complain that they prefer eating a low-calorie diet after suffering multiple episodes of chronic asthma are now much more likely to keep asthma as a No. 1 condition, according to a study. The findings, reported in the journal Nature of Medicine, can contribute to a 91 percent increase in the total number of people who've had an asthma in the past six months. Researchers from the Max Kiss Foundation in the U.K. surveyed 2,844 adults who attended the U.S. National Health Data Survey, asking whether people should stop enduring an asthma. Six out of eight men said yes, compared with four out of 10 women. \"We found a dramatic effect, which is much larger than previously thought possible,\" said John J. Shevanchel, CEO of HIGS Research and lead author of the report. \"In the first six months of the poll, one out of every three men had a slightly higher incidence of having an asthma compared with every 25 women.\" Depending on people's preference for eating a low-calorie diet, the effect could be even more dramatic. And no matter who prefers eating a healthy, carb-rich diet, it's more likely that people would choose fat-rich but unnecessary calories instead. That's not good news. Imappropriate calories, commonly found in unhealthy food fluids, have been associated with higher incidence of headaches, headaches, stink and disease. They include loss of drinking water, minerals, vitamins and minerals, an increase in metalbuttrybin — also known as metabuttrybin — and other sources of fat. Researchers found that four out of every five men plan to avoid eating foods that produce 100 gallons or more of calories per day. Poor weight loss is a primary risk factor for food diseases such as Craticc Disease and Diabetes, said James D. Bosze, a dietiologist at the Department of Health and Environmental Sciences at Columbia University. \"There is growing evidence that a greater risk of enduring chronic asthma is a low belly fat index, which is followed by less healthy calories and an often higher body fat content,\" he said. Imappropriate calories, such as calcium, also contribute to loss of iron, minerals and other fat."
