        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000475",
        "sourceId": 475,
        "text": "Over the past 20 years, the number of children learning the entirety of the lessons in mathematics and visual arts has suffered a steady decline in time (Click here for more) Over the past 20 years, the number of children learning the entirety of the lessons in mathematics and visual arts has fallen Time-wise, time in visual arts has also been on the decline. In other words, the quality of learning has been plummeting since the turn of the twentieth century. However, this steady decline in time in mathematics and visual arts is best seen as one related to improvements in learning. This slowing decline in children’s learning can be explained in part by improvements in mathematics itself, says Andy Peterson, an associate of Mathematics Education at the University of California, NCAA and co-author of the report. \"The weirdest reason for this is that fewer students are going to mathematics and visual arts classes and taking advantage of them week after week,\" he said. However, Peterson says spending time in mathematics and visual arts eliminated established learning opportunities for children. \" Mathema is the most important subject for students these days and this is the problem. But Peterson and others are seeing that and are actively working on finding new resources that can help students overcome these challenges,\" Peterson says. \"In particular, we found dramatic improvements in time in mathematics and visual arts in schools. In 2012, when we surveyed students at schools across the country, the proportion of time spent in mathematics increased from 3.7 minutes per 10,000 children to 1.5 minutes per 1,000 children. We also found that about half of children learning mathematics – nearly 60 percent of teenagers – attend public classes week after week.\" John C. Mitchell, a doctoral candidate in NCAA's College of Economic and Applied Sciences who authored the original report, says that right now a lot of work is being done in exposing more students to other subjects in the curriculum such as mathematics, art and science. \"Enhanced emphasis on mathematics is critical to improving the quality of learning,\" he said. That original title \"An Enemy of Things\" has been enhanced -- in addition to a video game -- by a new gameplay system. And for anyone who's ever played a title like that, it's an added layer to the experience. Indeed, unlike Land Strike III, which is largely about procedural action and dialogue, it's largely about an ambitious adventure. And unlike Land In Order, which was launched on PC last year, the title is about an extremely eloquent and engaging adventure. It's a follow-up to that original Landing Order, which stars Na Ishito as a studio designer and director of Sati Studios. Ishito is grateful to VideoGamer Indigo for bringing that one a reality. Yet there's something inconvenient about Yoshito, who has extensive experience in 3D modeling, that we're still likely to forget as Land Strike III challenges the fundamentals of 3D modeling. (So, what makes a PC such valuable tool? What do I mean then?) You see, Yokia Tanmoto wasn't known as a leading believer in the creation of a 3D modeling world, but when he met up with Ishito -- who to this day is a leading developer of Amazon Gear 360 and PlayStation Vita X -- his initial goal was to figure out a way to produce a video game combining varying tools and techniques from around the globe. He became even more interested in that idea, which he later described to me as putting into perspective the fundamentals of the video game as a whole. \"I soon knew I was going to try to figure this out,\" said Tanmoto, who bills himself as Yokia, from Japan, and as Ishito, who is a console system designer for Sony. \"I started playing video games around in the 90s but, as many of you already know, the last thing you want is a game based on a satirical'story.' It's a visceral story of a world of tools and techniques. It's a summary of the roles we play. It's a description of the tools we use in the game. And it's a very powerful idea.\" As for Ishito, all we actually know is that the game is going to feel like a muck version of the Nintendo Switch and the Nintendo systems, but unlike Land Strike III it is definitely going to have a hidden root. \"Every time I played the game when I was only playing in the United States, I still thought of it as my dream,\" Tanmoto told me. \"This is pretty much nothing but a hidden dream but a desire to clear the way for a working reality."
