        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000504",
        "sourceId": 504,
        "text": "But the violence in Baghdad appears to be taking its toll on U.S. Shiite militia groups and further limiting their support to the oil-challenged regime of Syrian President Bashar al-Assad. “The primary objective is to limit the spread of militants in Baghdad city, while restricting the support of the Assad regime, by carrying out executions and committing suicide attacks,” said Abduljam Al Tonqili, director of the Liberation Campaign, a radio programme on the Syrian’s liberation movement’s Afra al-Arabiya television network. Sunni groups claim responsibility for airstrikes that have taken place in Iraq and Syria and a statement from the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights says militants have also intervened in Iraq and Syria. The United Nations says a total of 38 Syrians have been killed in 335 military confrontations this year. However, Omar Taisev, the organization’s spokesman, said on Friday that Russian forces had conducted at least 200 airstrikes against militants in the Kurdistan region of Sadiq in the last two weeks alone, killing 42 Russian troops and wounding 26 others. Sadiq is governed by a government backed by Ahar al-Sham, a Shiite militia ally. On Friday Russian airstrikes killed a commander of the Shiite Islamic State militia and a 21-year-old U.S. Army veteran as they attempted to liberate Baghdad and stop the Islamic State’s militaristic expansion. After winning military concessions in August, there is deep concern about the Russian push to remove militia groups from north-east of the country. The U.S. militia groups, and particularly those of current Islamic State leader Aytollah Mansour, are also attempting to regain control of Iraqi part of the country. The fighting in neighboring Baghdad and the loss of faith in the collvading country provides a powerful avenue for the groups to break away from their Western allies. The government has hardened the Bitcoin blockchain, enabling an act of tax avoidance, Russia's top regulator said. \"By enabling an on-the-shonkey tax cut, consumers will be able to buy untaxed goods now online and charge an extra fee to cover their tax bills,\" DHS official Sergey Mokanonov said. Stateside tax authorities have so far imposed an unusual level of control over the Bitcoin network, also known as the Bitcoin blockchain, which has been used to crack down scammers and money abusers. State tax authorities have stopped businesses from using the blockchain to allow consumers to buy and acquiesce currency without being charged a specific amount. The crackdown happened earlier this year, when consumers were caught trading in precious, highly- valued securities by using such options as credit cards or investment bonds. The hardened version of the technology enables consumers to buy an array of goods, including currency without charging any taxes. Consumer can buy benefits by paying banks, using payments that can range from electricity to bank notes. Securities and exchange companies have long been alarmed by the latest crackdown. They said their business operations are taking consumers off as countries are experiencing an economic crisis and the technology's use is expanding throughout the world. According to Strategy 2014, a report releasing, the DHS expects access to the technology to reach 16 million people at the end of the year next year. Some 70 percent of Russians will stop using the technology by the end of 2014, and expect it to grow to more than 16 million next year. Officials pushing for a minimum tax cut on businesses said the government is \"cutting\" tax bills for businesses by removing consumers from access to their over-the-counter operations. Securities and exchange companies have long said their operations are taking consumers off. But Sergey Mokanonov, deputy director of the U.S. government's Federal Crime Division, said a hardened version of the technology is possibly the most effective solution for high-profile slander crimes such as tax evasion. Only some U.S. cities and counties have had tax crackdown ordinances, and most taxes can be raised if households with incomes over $550,000 receive minimum or moderate tax cuts, Mokanonov said. Among the companies that recently adopted the blockchain technology is developing a new digital currency, called CoinBitcoin, for online trading. \"Obviously, to reduce the ability of businesses engaging in activities of tax abuse and fraud to trade their securities or assets using the blockchain, we believe that minimum tax cuts should be required,\" Mokanonov said. \"But there is no effective way to ensure that online trading operations can be stopped.\""
