        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000597",
        "sourceId": 597,
        "text": "With so many young rookies still in the NFL, it should come as no surprise that Sean Bieber is utilizing one of his strengths on the back side of the ball, the defense, as he enter the offseason. Carson Jones has signed to an eight-year, $170 million contract and has played a big part in the Rams’ continuing success, while Jordan Harmon, LaMarcus Raffork, Juannec Marshall, Allen and Roy Jazi all became free agents this offseason. Tyler Fitpatrick is still on the team’s game plan, while Newton is on pace to recover from last season’ shoulder surgery. In return on the free agency, Jermain Newton recorded 28 catches for 459 yards during the season and is rolling back into Pro Bowl action. Sean Sanford has made the Pro Bowl strides and has worked himself back into the postseason. With solid offensive coordination and consistent defenses the Rams should continue to excel. In the 2015 offseason, make sure to take a hard look at what players are available that should help the team find some serious success in the future. Over the years, the 2014 Rams have made a lot of progress at every level in football. Their passing attack ranks third in the NFL in their offensive effectiveness. 2015 and 2016 should be a very exciting year going forward for the franchise, as Doug King becomes the first rookie ever to make two Pro Bowls. The United States Former president Bill Clinton is one of the most influential members of this generation of writers and historians. His series of books have gotten a lot of attention, most famously especially his eloquent masterpieces, Beyond the Maritimes, which won him the Pulitzer Prize the past year. But the publication of his world-famous novel The Evolution of the Pre-Revolutionary United States Government since the Turn of the 20th Century challenges the current state of American literature, the political system, and the culture of the United States in the era after the Revolution, and represents one of the most significant and iconic literary achievements of recent generations. The face of American literature has changed dramatically since the collapse of the Soviet Empire in the 1930s. Image credit: New York Times] Benjamin L. Roosevelt and Barack Obama: 1871-1938 Throughout American history, America’s understanding of racial issues was deeply rooted in progressive values for education, justice, social accountability, and class equality. George W Bush and Benjamin Roosevelt largely embodied this vision from the perspective of a nation that was disenfranchised by a black race. And it was not until the Civil War that a deeper understanding of American literary culture was developed following the Nazi occupation and the growing African American and Latino population. Hollywood in the 20th century had helped to overcome racial inequality by producing animated TV series like The X-Firex and Spider-Man. Boston Public Library] Benjamin Roosevelt and Barack Obama: 1907-1924 Despite fifteen years of austerity and sexism at the margins of Roosevelt and his administration until the 1924 presidential election, the United States wasn’t exactly looking like the “ heartland” of Greece, Bosnia, Yugoslavia, Eritrea, Japan, and Italy. That was layered by dissatisfaction in Europe, New Zealand, South Asia, and Africa following the Soviet Empire’s collapse. While still operating as a superpower, the avalanche of American writers sought to pursue the same vision that Benjamin Roosevelt and Barack Obama had defined: achieving their goals by catalyzing economic growth, undermining racial equality, and sustaininging and revitalizing American literary culture. Did everything go well for the people of the United States? Did the greatest writers of all time come to the United States? It is really too early to tell. Here is a list of fifty books that truly shaped the current state of American literary culture. They were written by the most influential writers of all time, and can be read between the ages of fifty and 72 years. Image credit: Los Angeles Public Library] Richard P. Nixon: 1933-1938 Born in 1934, and raised by his family after an enemy campaign during World War II, Nixon was surprisingly popular in the judicial system in the United States, serving as a military judge for over a decade until his death of a stroke. It was only soon after his illness that he reaffirmed his faith in American progressiveism, and decided to pursue a career in literature. As editor and author of Nixon’s History of America, Nixon has written more than 4,000 books, many of which cover the history of literature. He was a supporting writer for popular TV series, including Game of Thrones and The Walking Dead.] Richard P. Nixon: 1933-1938"
