        "text": "However, it is not yet known whether the music will be available for players to download on the PS4, or whether it will work for games in the Battlefield IV or the PS4 PC's Battlefield Arena. This year's Battlefield IV received a major design update, and the majority of fans will likely start playing matches of this game shortly after its release. Super Mario VI already has added a few of the main characters in the game, and in the coming weeks, Nintendo hopes to add an additional number of those characters to games. addition to updating the game with characters like Riyado Takami and Mai Moto, the game will now be available for players to experiment without requiring the ability of Super Mario VI. Gamers in Battlefield IV will have an additional set of classes, a feature that was first announced when Super Mario VI launched on PS4. Players can now try to create new classes to the game, something that developers haven't confirmed yet, but they will be able to create classes for the different heroes in the original game. We'll let you in on that below. On the technical side, Nintendo has also released an automatic certification system that applies to all PC games. We can take a look at what's included within the system in the video below. The characters who receive a Character Class will be allowed to choose a two-class model as need be. The characters who receive this class will be divided in the number of classes they will have. Once successfully creating a class to the game, the player will also be able to select a individual Character Class for each class. The characters who receive a Character Class will be divided into various \"types,\" which the vast majority will be playing for the first three phases of Battlefield IV. Extendations in the Battlefield Arena Finally, some major additions to the Battlefield Arena have been included in this release. The focus on level building will not be bypassed. Instead, players have the ability to view past Battlefield IV demos, meet existing heroes and interact with new heroes. The Nintendo add-on feature was intended to be exclusive to PC users, but it also didn't focus on level building. It's now possible to class your favorite heroes. You can even decide which heroes to add to each class, rather than searching through websites for them. Finally, Nintendo has added new features like Hero Shock, new Hero Combat System and many other features that we can check out in next week's updates. Let's carry on waiting to see what we can expect from Battlefield IV as it launches on PS4 PC. We want to hear your feedback, so feel free to leave a comment about it here: https://i.pimsoft.com/bevegames Backers of renovation argue that municipalities and public schools should be written off. Opponents call for states to send $3.1 billion to fund local public schools. The House of Representatives debated a renovation plan that would create more funding for municipal schools and give lower-income areas better access to public transportation. Proponents argued that more of the money should instead be sent to local districts. Municipalities do not need money to fund local schools. The state spends much of those funds on much-needed services such as improved housing for children, access to libraries and jobs. A version of renovation, currently in negotiating, passed in the House of Representatives in 2010. It could create additional funding of schools for lower-income areas. The proposal currently needs 1,000 signatures and about 8,000 voters. Proponents conceptualized the proposal on the grounds that supporters of renovation want to send $3.1 billion to fund local schools for lower-income areas. Municipalities do not need money to fund local schools. The state spends much of that money on providing much-needed educational services such as improved housing, public transportation and jobs. Florida-based advocacy group, Vote for Florida, put together a resolution to fight the renovation. The resolution calls on all states to send the same amount of money to fund municipal schools. \"Putting massive amounts of money into municipal schools is the most effective way to spend taxpayer money and is one of the most important priorities for citizens of Florida,\" said Kristen Sabassis, director of public school affairs at the Association of State Boards, an organization that is opposing the proposal. Protors argue the renovation would return hundreds of millions of dollars in taxpayer money to those districts. Opponents have advocated for privatizing municipal schools. In an interview with MSNBC, Alex Adams, speaker of the Florida House of Representatives, said privatizing municipal schools would cost districts hundreds of millions of dollars to upgrade the schools and bolster their services. Adams also argued that if the proposal is passed, a greater amount should instead be used to fund municipal schools and to the state boards. Follow this story: @politicpost"
