        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000652",
        "sourceId": 652,
        "text": "AFLORA CITY — The WTO on Tuesday issued an “unprecedented exception” to former President George W. Bush, blaming the “majority of the media” in rebuking his anti-abortion remarks. In a statement, the WTO said most of its members have requested to pull out of such events in the name of anti-life beliefs. “On numerous occasions over the last few years, a small handful of leaders have sought to pull out of such anti-abortion events, in the name of their anti-life beliefs,\" it confirmed. @realDonaldTru pic.twitter.com/4PyXXJ7HT9N — Joy Fielder (@joanfielder) May 1, 2017 In a statement Tuesday, the WTO “strongly disproves these remarks and outlines its organization’s toughest measures since 2001 for the Bush administration,” according to Bloomberg. The statement came within hours after the organization said its members were not actively promoting such events. “In a statement shared with The Wall Street Journal, the organization confirmed that it was actively funding such abortion events, and cautioned that several hundred of its members were actively promoting such events as recently as 2001.” As the leaders stopped short of blaming the media for seeing Bush elected president, the organization called on its members to be “redistributed to the voting box, as soon as possible.” Streaming video on Windows Phone and Windows 10 using users’ mobile phones is the third change coming in December. Windows Phone released a completely new AMOHA feature, dubbed as the “Videoture Streaming Tool,” that allows users to access video on Windows 10 devices without installing software. Windows Phone is undergoing further changes to the streaming feature, but more than a few months from now is still providing users with the added-on video streaming feature on Windows 10 devices. “On Windows 10 devices, Video Streaming Tool is no longer the preferred streaming feature,” Windows Phone said in a statement yesterday. “I strongly believe that software-based video functions should now be disbanded on all Windows 10 devices.” Windows Phone maintained that there were “no changes” to the user interface and interface of Windows 10’s AMOHA streaming feature. But he reiterated in his opinion that all software-based video functions available on Windows 10 should no longer make the feature’s streaming list. Windows Phone also maintained that the Video Streaming Tool does not work on all other Windows Phone or Windows 10 devices, but can be configured to work for all Windows Phone users. “I am a Windows Phone user and therefore all Windows 10 devices need to include the video streaming feature as soon as the end of January,” he said. Microsoft also maintained that cross-streaming software-based video functions on Windows 10 devices will cease support by October 19th, 2016, for OS 8.1 users. By the beginning of October, access to the VideoStreaming Tool, built by Microsoft Mobile Services, had been enabled. While Windows Phone is now officially added to the AMOHA list, Windows Phone said it is no longer possible to stream video on Windows 10 devices without  installing your mobile phone. However, he expects Windows 10’s streaming functionality to gradually come back to normal, noting that Windows 10 is no longer just stopping users from opening certain apps while streaming video on Windows Phone. “Uing Windows Phone on Windows 10 after you have already installed Windows Office isn’t different from using Windows Phone on Windows 10 at a later date,” Windows Phone said. Even though the AMOHA streaming feature is available on OS 8.1 via a small “ Install Windows 10” button in the bottom right of the screen, streaming video can still be enabled via Microsoft’s Option Explorer panel while using your phone. It’s also worth noting that while Windows 10 no longer requires Windows Option Explorer, it does require Windows Option Explorer. BEIJHUNG (Reuters) - China’s renewable energy needs may be larger than previously estimated, understating Beijing’s 2015 goal of adding about 1.5 trillion megawatts of non-modified solar power by 2050, according to a report by researchers at the University of International Studies in the United States. Recent analysis by the U.N. Energy Information Agency indicated that the country’s total renewable energy needs - consisting mostly of modified energy, such as traditional wind or nuclear power - could peak as soon as 2025. “The renewable energy need could eventually reduce to more than half the nation’s total need by 2050, taking into account the benefits of increasing energy consumption or reducing the costs of renewable power generation,” the U.N. researchers concluded in a report published on Tuesday by Reuters."
