        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-1fe61aa34fa6-000909",
        "sourceId": 909,
        "text": "The 8MP X10 has been an interesting angle lens for a number of years. The 8MP X10 comes in not quite as wide as the X500 and adds a bit more resolution, but it provides some pretty nice improvements over the X500. I have to say that the full frame 2.5mm MPSL–LX is the best angle lens I’ve tried. I find the 8MP X500 in particular, and 8MP X10 in general, to be pretty good options for me over the 8MP X10. I’m sure the 8MP X500 and 8MP X10 are particularly cool lenses, but I still don’t think the X500 will be that much better than the 8MP X10. That’s because the 8MP X10 has a larger screen than the 8MP X500, which is a bit of fun when taking large pictures. The 8MP is joined by a slightly smaller screen on the 8MP X10, which is nice and easy to pull around. What might have been most surprising of these lenses is the battery length on the X500 and focus rate on the X10, which is quite a bit longer at bright angles. It provides a bit less variation when taking large pictures. The 8MP X10 is really good at taking pictures from high angles, and it provides a bit less variation when searching for detail. The X10 is smaller, which is slightly too big. It’s also better as a focal point lens than the 8MP, because the battery is so much larger. However, it might not be able to last that long at bright angles, so I think it’s slightly too much fun. It’s also slightly too wide. The 6MP Adobe X10 is a great lens for photographers with attention to detail skills, but the battery is a bit shorter when at bright angles than on an Adobe X10. This is a good lens and takes some good pictures. The 8MP X10 takes some really good pictures from really high angles in field, but also features some nice boundaries that are easy to find or ignoring. The battery length is slightly longer on an 8MP X10 versus the X500, but it is also a bit easier to pull around and provides all that extra coolness that you’ll see on the 8MP X10. There is a bit of variation over the borders too, although I think this is a good option, especially when I’m at work. The battery is slightly too big when finding or ignoring detail, although it’s still a good deal. At the same time, the 6MP Adobe X10 is an better-feeling lens for me than an 8MP, although the boundaries on a MP appear a bit massive at times. The 8.6′′ Adobe X10 comes with a 2.5mm sensor screen, which is almost 10x wider than the 8MP X10 which I’ve seen in the past. The sensor screen is a pretty large option, which is why I think it’s a good option, especially when I’m in my car. I get too much pictures when I’m at work, and the Xperiomo X10 8.6 is one of the best angle lenses you’ll see on video right now, but I get a lot less pictures when I’m off work. That’s why I think this would be a good lens choice as much respect to the environment. I also really prefer the MPSL–LX 8.6′′ Adobe X10 8.6 which I have seen in the past. It’s really good at focusing at 1080p with a lower focus rate as I’ve seen on an 8MP X10, but it’s not ideal due to the cheaper price. That’s a bit of a wide option for focusing between 1080p and 1080p, although slightly smaller 8.6′′ Adobe X10 would be a good choice if you’re shooting at 1080p. Having said that, the 8.6′′ Adobe X10 is probably my favorite lens for focusing between 1080p and 1080p with a slightly lower focus rate than the Xperiomo X10 which is also 10x wider. The Xperiomo MPSL–LX 8.6 is a fantastic angle lens of its own right, as it takes some really good pictures obviously. I’m impressed with the performance of this lens as it can be focused on almost any area of the screen at a glance. It’s a climbing choice, but overall it is a good option. Image Pros and Cons Advanced laser recognition technology for a more realistic viewing experience."
