        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000068",
        "sourceId": 68,
        "text": "SL: Yeah. I'm not sure what to call it, but there's a baseline between women in the armed forces and women in the Coast Guard, and some would say it's just a baseline. And I think I've just retired from service and I'm not sure I'm very active. But I think I'm getting older as well. Advertisement Col. Andre Carter AK: Thank you for talking. AK: You previously served as U.S. Navy Attorney Kelly Hill, representing all of the clients of the Marines. What was it like about coming out of the Navy over the last year and working at the Coast Guard over the last year? SL: Yeah, absolutely. Nothing's changed for me. There's been a lot of change over my career, but I always had the goal of a lawyer. My goal was to be a full-time attorney in the Coast Guard. And just a few months ago, I became interested in working in the Navy. And I quickly became interested in the women I was working with. And I quickly learned that it's not just about the Marines, it's not just about the Coast Guard, it's about the Coast Guard. And upon returning to the Navy I became a lawyer, and I started to become more interested in working with the Marines clients. But I didn't really know what I was doing. As I got older, I became more interested in working with the Coast Guard as a doctor. And I think I've adapted. I've started thinking of working with Marines as members of the Coast Guard or full-reserve lawyers. And I noticed that I also got a lot more interested with the Coast Guard. I'm thinking of working as a doctor now, but I didn't know what I was doing with them. And I just didn't know what I would be doing with them in the Coast Guard. Advertisement Capt. Michael Matheis AK: How have you overcome some of the challenges you have since returning from the Navy? And how have you overcome some of the challenges that you have since returning home? SL: I'm not sure. But there's the division that we do have. And I think the really important division is that women have to compete with traditional men in the Coast Guard while the Navy is competing and women have to compete against traditional men in the Coast Guard. And I think that although a lot of the women who are working for us are getting degrees, because they're working for the Marines, I know a lot of women are working for the Marines. But I do think the transition is good. I think it's frustrating work at times. Sometimes you can just just do a few more studies and get a degree. And if you want to be an attorney, or if you want to become a professor, you can just go do a study and get a degree. And I think it's safer to be a young Marine and work in the Navy, so you have to have an attorney before working in the Navy like you would in any other occupation. AK: Your clients are predominantly women in the workforce? SL: My clients are mainly of women. I know it can be difficult for them to become pro. A lot of my clients are women in their 30s. And if you're going to become very pro as a lawyer, you're going to have to do just a little bit of bargaining just to become pro. But a lot of my clients are actually working for the Navy, they're young men. I think that's a pretty significant gap in the labour market. And the young clients that we have, we have a significant number of young men that's just learning to have an attorney. And when I moved to the USS, there was not a huge shortage of attorneys. And there's a significant number of young people that are just learning to have a lawyer. And I think it definitely encourages young people to be better Navy attorneys, enables them to be better lawyers, enables them to file better cases and be able to do better counsel and all that stuff. I've found that, since I moved to the USS, I've learned about fair practice and the efficiency of the service. And the fair practice principles of the service are out there as well. But in the same way that we have this industry, there's a significant gap between my employees and their clients in the labour market. AK: High-tech industry. Aside from that, what would you consider a high-tech industry? SL: I think it's just a equipment industry. It's a software industry. High-tech industry"
