        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000232",
        "sourceId": 232,
        "text": "In the fall of 2012, congressional Republicans scrapped ObamaCare in the rural part of the country that was previously covered by a single-payer health policy until further opposition from the House and Senate. Republicans and Democrats continued to push through provisions of ObamaCare that would increase premiums for individuals and businesses under the law, despite conservative leaders’ efforts to quickly pass a bill that would do that. But now Goodell, who has pushed back on a plan to raise premiums, may return to action on Tuesday. “We have to see how we’re going to respond,” Goodell said during a hearing this week before a health policy committee. “I don’t have a lot of details of how we’re going to respond, and so we’ll have to see.” For years, the ObamaCare health insurance market has been living in harm’s way — it collapsed in 2006, when the Ryan administration replaced it with ever more of a single-payer system. The White House is urging Republicans to rewrite earlier legislation that called for an end to the private health insurance market. Both chambers of the House have scheduled hearing on an ObamaCare repeal, scheduled to take effect Oct. 1. In addition, Republicans are mulling legislation to pay for potential tax cuts, based on the 2010 Republican repeal of the Federal Fair Trade Act and the Affordable Care Act provisions. Lawmakers have been pushing to remove the law’s tax cuts for years, proposing a 10 percent reduction for individuals obtaining health insurance and an additional half for businesses pursuing private coverage. These provisions are being challenged in the GOP-led House and Senate, and House Democrats are pushing for them to be removed after they were approved by the GOP-led Health and Human Services. If approved, these provisions would be similar to those Republicans had proposed for before they scrapped the healthcare system in 2006, introducing a permanent federal exemptary for individuals and businesses who enrolls in insurance coverage under the law. With a House and Senate majority in the next few weeks, Republicans hope to pass reductions in premiums for health insurance policy holders and for businesses with more than a million employees. And conservative leaders have already approved major changes to the healthcare system for 2012, including no less than $6 trillion in tax cuts for individuals and businesses next year. If approved by Congress, they would increase premiums by about $1 trillion to $1.4 trillion, or nearly a quarter of the more than $9 trillion required under the law. Tax gains are unsurprisingly high for Republicans, but premiums are still declining, as Republicans are pushing for lower profit margins and profit margins for individuals and businesses, as well as lower margins for profits for businesses with more than a million employees under the ObamaCare law. The health insurance premium payments system would also be left out of the equation, with House Democrats proposing to increase premiums for health insurance holders and House Republicans also seeking to shorten premiums even further for individuals and businesses. To help win that battle, Republicans also introduced some other important provisions in the bill that would repeal tax gains and increase premiums for health insurance holders. These provisions include a permanent federal exemptary for individuals with a health policy; a federal exemptary for each individual with a health insurance policy; a three-year exemptary for each passing year of a member’s insurance policy; a two-year exemptary for each member’s health insurance for each individual with a health policy; a five-year exemptary for individuals with a health insurance policy; as well as dozens of other provisions proposed on the bill by the GOP-led Health and Human Services committee, including a permanent exemptary for individuals with a healthcare policy. These have also pushed down premiums for individuals eligible for private coverage. Since the collapse of the Federal Fair Trade Act, health insurance premiums have been declining dramatically, largely by efforts from conservative and Democratic leaders. Republicans have long wanted to introduce a permanent federal exemptary for individuals and businesses who enrolls in a healthcare policy. But premiums have dwindled a bit, and individuals who don’t have a health policy will no longer have to pay federal fees to receive one. And like many congressional Republicans seeking to boost the economy through healthcare reform, conservative leaders remain vehemently against most of the healthcare reform legislation they have long pushed, including Ryan’s health care reform plan, Ryan’s energy reform plan, Ryan’s defense reform plan, Ryan’s stock market reform plan and many other conservative healthcare reform bills, regardless of their party. “The public doesn’t see it going up,” said Michael Mullens, associate director for health-care policy at the Center for American Progress, a conservative thinktank. “They don’t see it going up.”"
