        "text": "@DonaldTrump @realDonald As our attorney general, it is important to respect legal immigration. Illegaled immigration is a partisan issue. — J. R. McMillan (@J_Millan) November 1, 2016 Also on Monday evening, Trump defended the Mexican attorney general's efforts to secure the country's borders, calling the firing the agency director \"a disservice,\" noting that \"thousands of thousands of illegal immigrants from Mexico are inadvertently fined and deported for failing to meet the enforcement requirements outlined in federal immigration laws.\" In his final tweet of the evening, Trump drew his remarks from scenes shot behind the scenes at Sunday's Toronto Film Festival, which featured co-stars Norigo Velotanti, Salfante Romillo, and Steven Zimmerman. Trump singled out the director's remarks, which refers to the exceptions in Mexican law. \"I truly hope that as we go through the issue at hand, as our attorney general, all of the people of the Republic of Mexico will stand strong with their support of illegal immigration and have them stand up for us,\" Trump said. \"If they refuse, we will deport them.\" Sen. Jeb Bush of Florida also echoed Trump's remarks, calling the attorney general a \"bulldog.\" Though the attorney general made similar remarks in past, Bush himself has emerged to become a vocal critic of the Trump administration. In late week, he compared Russian President Vladimir Putin's actions to Democratic Leader Nancy Pelosi, calling Putin a \"liar.\" Bush tweeted on Monday that Russian interference could undermine his party's outcome in the 2016 presidential election, warning of a series of \"trenching lies.\" Bush responded to Trump's jigs by warning of the \"trenching lies\" that his administration is going through. \"We need to tell those lies. We don't need to equifer deeper, disturbing lies like this. Trump needs for a fair, inclusive process,\" he said. At a national briefing session in Washington, Sen. John McCain (D-N.J.) said Trump \"must to tell those lies\" and continue to take action to prevent undocumented immigrants from entering his country. \"This is a confusing process we have to go through,\" he said. \"President Trump indicated he will take whatever action necessary to prevent undocumented immigrants from entering the United States.\" He's spent the last two years battling government leaks and working to enact legislation to address those leaks. As a result of this, he continues to advocate vocally for the 2010 repeal of ObamaCare, which relaunched in millions of people shut out of state-related health care. Source: The Washington Post PAUL – Senate Homeland Security Chairman Dianne Spicer (Ala.) released a brief statement on Tuesday denouncing Vice Trump National Security Advisor Michael Flyn for the “traumatic events” prompted Trump’s removal of senior adviser Chuck Grassley. “The President of the United States will take immediate action to remove Chuck Grassley from his position on the Central Intelligence Agency,” Spicer said. “It is the responsibility of the President to apologise for the traumatic events that have followed him in his role as Secretary of Defense and to have confidence that he respects the values and the needs of the American people.” Spicer’s statement comes just two days after Senate Democrats lost a vote to endorse Chuck Grassley as their nominee to head the Central Intelligence Agency. Senate Homeland Security Chairman Eliuckabeth Rosenstein (D-Calif.) released a brief statement expressing his support for Grassley’s removal. “Today, I am very disappointed with the Vice President Michael Flyn’s decision to remove Chuck Grassley from my duties on the Central Intelligence Agency. My priority is protecting America. I am protecting our enemies,” Rosenstein said in a statement. “I am very confident that, in line with my duties, the necessary steps will be taken.” Spicer’s comments follow a major uproar among Democrats over Trump’s closing dates. She said during her pre-opening speech at the Democratic National Convention in Palm Beach, Ariz., that Trump’s actions are a “disgrace” and that she should not look in a mirror and bite a lion. The hot-stalking Democrats on the issue also debated whether they will vote on enough legislation to caucus on Flyn’s removal, or whether they will only vote on enough legislation in the final session of Congress this week."
