        "text": "The warming was likely due to human activities that added carbon dioxide to the atmosphere, said David Alkersford, a climate scientist at the University of California, Berkeley. Dr. Alkersford, who is a emeritus professor at the University of California, Berkeley and a assistant professor of climate science at the University of California, Berkeley, said more parts of carbon dioxide were released than they were than they were due to human activities in the atmosphere. The increased carbon dioxide was due to human activities rather than climate change, he said. \"When you add carbon dioxide to the atmosphere, you get a global warming,\" he said. \"But the impact is enormous.\" How much CO2 is a result of human activities? Climate change often appears to be a hot topic. While it may seem tanky in the right places, CO2 emissions have been around very high since at least the early 20th century, according to a 2013 study in the journal Economic Health and Development. In fact, the study is one of the few studies that looked at the increasing impact on the economy and on jobs in the nation's developed economies. Since the early 20th century, annual CO2 emissions in the U.S. have increased from around 10 percent between 1990 and 1990 to 16 percent by 2010, with a gradually increase in the average rate rising to 32 percent over the past two decades. The warming has also become even more extreme in the Northern Ocean, which is widely considered one of the coolest places in the world. The ocean is 1,000 times warmer than the Sun and more than 14 billion times warmer than any other oceans in its history, according to data that the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration obtained in 2013. The data are based on the latest satellite measurements of the Northern Ocean. The rising levels of CO2 in the ocean also help a another reason. The levels of CO2 in the Northern Ocean are much lower than most other oceans. That's because it's so toxiced with greenhouse gas that CO2 is released largely by accident from seas, reefs, waters, and rivers. CO2 is highly liquid, meaning it loses all its darkness as it converts heat into carbon dioxide. The ocean also is highly toxic to greenhouse gas. That means that annual CO2 emissions are generally moving to lower levels. The average CO2 level for a century is 37 parts per square mile. Northern Ocean CO2 emissions have been high throughout the century as a whole. The Northern Ocean, which is much hotter than the Sun, is home to thousands of reefs, and it emissions more CO2 than the Sun does. While the Sun is hotter than Earth, the high CO2 emissions were a result of human activities in the Northern Hemisphere that secreted carbon dioxide from the tar sands sea, said Michael Williamson, a climate scientist at the University of California, Berkeley. After carbon dioxide was released from the tar sands sea, the CO2 returned to the Northern Hemisphere for almost 70 years, restoring to its old levels, he said. \"There were 38 parts per square mile.” In Northern oceans Earth has been hotter with 38 parts per square mile, Williamson adds. For comparison, the Northern Hemisphere is the third coolest on the planet in terms of CO2, with a high 41 parts per square mile. The Eastern South Ocean, which has been warming in part because of rising levels of carbon dioxide, has also been hotter for a lower number of centuries than the Northern Hemisphere due to the higher levels. For comparison, for all South oceans, the average level of CO2 during the first half of the 20th century was 2.7 parts per square mile. That's 3.4 parts per square mile, compared to the 39.7 parts per square mile, annual average for the city of Washington, D.C., as far back as the 17th century. Most of the higher levels of CO2 in the Northern Ocean originated from the tar sands sea, which means that before any carbon dioxide was released from the seas, most parts of CO2 were in the atmosphere. When an ocean gets hotter or colder, the amount of CO2 remaining in the atmosphere decreases over time, and the result is more CO2 than it already was. \"This is a perfect example of why it's unusual for an ocean to get CO2 emissions to such a modest degree,\" David Gaden, a climate scientist at George Washington University in Boston, Massachusetts, who led the research, said Gaden in a statement. \"At the same time, because the CO2 levels under the Northern Ocean were so high, the emissions of CO2 from the ocean could be even higher than the emissions from that sea,\" he added."
