        "text": "Let's dig deeper. The U.S. has one of the highest levels of carbon dioxide in the world, which represents 90 percent of the overall global carbon dioxide concentration according to a new report released last week by the Treasury. The U.S. should have more carbon dioxide. But what of the United States' contribution to global warming? Daniel Newton, a professor at the London School of Economics, told the Economist in August that the U.S. should have more carbon dioxide, but just because you agree to cut greenhouse gas emissions doesn't automatically mean that more warming is needed. So, let's take a closer and closer look at what Newton said last month after he'd stepped down as the chairman of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change: This is fundamentally contrary to public opinion. According to the Intergovernmental Panel, \"less than one percent of Americans agree that global warming is necessary to grow the world's population.\" We don't know what that means, but just look at a poll released in August this year -- and made public by the Intergovernmental Panel here -- where Americans seemed to agree that climate change is necessary to grow the world's population by cutting greenhouse gass emissions. So while the global number has fallen by 80 percent, the percentage of the U.S. contribution has risen 62 percent. The U.S. contribution to global warming is growing, and yet we're told that a U.S. contribution to global warming makes the world's people more warming. In short, the U.S. isn't pouring money into the environment, it's pouring more money into the world's efforts to feed it. But that makes sense. The U.S. contribution to global warming isn't actually fueling it. As much as it's not warming, it's not warming. That said, it makes sense to say that the U.S. contribution to global warming is for the environment, not to feed it. The problem here is that the reality is actually the opposite. The U.S. contribution to climate change is against national interests. According to the State Department, the United Nations Convention on Climate Change and Climate Finance states that climate change is a \"free trade deal.\" In other words, it is a free deal: if the U.S. supported climate change, the U.S. would do less harm to the environment than it does today. Again, yes, true. But there is another problem with the American contribution to the cost of climate. Robert Berger, the author of The War on Climate: The Truth to Other Nations' Errors, jokingly said that the U.S.'s contribution to the cost of global warming is bad for the environment: if the U.S. had supported climate change, the U.S. would do a great deal more harm to the environment than it does today. Yes, no, it isn't bad for the environment. And, as Berger notes, the American contribution to climate change is neither good, nor evil. Just because the U.S. has signed the Paris Agreement doesn't mean that it is bad not only for the U.S. but for the other contributing nations as well. A man wearing a military hood is pictured outside a Turkish army jail in Anbar on April 18, 2015. (AFP Photo/Alexodis Jamiz) (AFP/Getty Images) TECHIA - A 29-year-old Turkish education minister was found dead in a military jail in Istanbul on Thursday after an inquiry into Islamist activity in the region by Turkey's foreign ministry. The man, identified as Kamura Isadtiya, had been detained for three days in the city of Anbar, about 2,200 km (3,000 miles) north of Istanbul before receiving a notification of his death last month, Turkey's foreign foreign ministry said. An international investigation team found the man's body. He died of natural causes on Thursday, the ministry said. A military court charged Isadtiya with war crimes, the country's foreign ministry said. Turkish army forces have been accelerating the offensive in the northern part of Anbar, and rebels in the city are fighting to gain control of Turkey, the ministry also said. There had been speculation that rebels in the city may be repulsing Islamist groups operating in the west of Istanbul. Anbar is one of Turkey's poorest regions. Isadtiya had been sentenced to death in the final year of his two-year term while pleading guilty to defaming Islamist groups in a military court in Istanbul, giving clear signs suggesting that he could die anytime soon."
