        "sourceId": 425,
        "text": "A return to the sanctions has come to the table for several reasons. For instance, U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton has adamantly said Iran wants to abandon its nuclear program altogether, expressing fears that U.S. sanctions will undermine its nuclear program. At a press conference in March this week, Clinton went on to say it was “important” for Iran “to turn a positive direction,” adding that an end to the war on terror would be needed, as it remains to be seen when Iran’s Department of Defense releases official information on the war on terror. Nevertheless, a return to the sanctions is likely to be at least a partial step, analysts have said, in a sign that the role of the U.S. in Iran’s nuclear issue has been shrinking. The threat of a return to the sanctions has also eroded in recent months, as indicated by the decision to end Iran’s ballistic missile program earlier this month. Now, as President Barack Obama’s Obama administration increases its sanctions, the U.S. Department of State is dramatically boosting cooperation with Iran, which is also facing a wave of economic sanctions that some leaders blame are responsible for the terrorism attacks against Tehran’s citizens. The impact of the sanctions is still unclear, as Iran’s government recently decided to cut Iran’s electricity supply by some 30 percent, a move that has left the country in the midst of a string of other attacks, from the bombings in Tehran in May to the massacres in Paris and Paris last year. The decision has not set off an immediate political shift, however, with the top Iranian official signing a petition on Twitter on Thursday that called for the annihilation of al-Qaida’s headquarters in Saudi Arabia to bring end to the crisis. Al-Qaida has been the target of the bombings since May 2015, leaving at least 45 policemen dead in the country. In the meantime, Iran has suspended all aid supplies in Saudi Arabia, pointing to a string of terrorism attacks that have killed millions of Iranian citizens and infected key cities like Riyadh, Wahrir, and Jabad. In the past few months, the humanitarian situation has changed dramatically. Bombings in the embattled Iran-held eastern province of Antiqui have killed more than 22,000 people, most of them Shia-prior citizens. This week, Iran’s hard-line President Abu Bachr al-Yasri condemned his administration’s sanctions move, calling a “ disgraceful mistake.” On Thursday, lawmakers objected to the move. “It’s not the decision of the U.S. or our country,” al-Yasri’s deputy, Shah al-Malaki, told The Hill. “It’s not a long-term strategic solution that the government is offering to help solve the problem. It’s a one-man solution to help fix the problem.” Many analysts say that the move is based on political rather than economic reasons. The move certainly reflects a longstanding expectation of a return to the increasing war on terror. In recent months, however, there has been growing uncertainty about the probability of a return to the terror war in the face of intense economic pressure within the country. Most analysts, however, see al-Yasri’s decision as the biggest step for the country since the crisis began in late 2005. The House of Representative plans to declare a 90-day state of emergency before the end of the year and Congress will have 15 days to enact the legislation by Nov. 1, 2014. Al-Yasri hopes to end a 20-day emergency period this fall. Criticism of the sanctions continues to anger leaders in Iran’s most powerful country. Their closest allies, Prime Minister Maher Abd al-Habab al-Yasri and the country’s President Mahmoud Abbas, have imposed a barrage of economic sanctions on Washington and Tehran that will reduce Iran’s role in countering the so-called terror war. Al-Yasri is the target of a proposed “black market sanctions program” by the United States or Congress. He was one of several dozen top-level Iranian officials this month to participate in a program that the House Foreign Committee has identified primarily aimed at reducing the U.S. military role in countering terror. Photo by the Associated Press BEIJING (Reuters) - A high-flying U.S.-made cruise plane crashed into a mountain field in the Tansan province of north-southwestern China on Tuesday, killing four people aboard, an American soldier said. : "
