        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000479",
        "sourceId": 479,
        "text": "Scientists, led by the U.S. Department of Energy at Stanford, have recently taken a major step in the direction of finding a gene responsible for causing Parkinson's disease. The study, published by Dr. Michael Shoe and Professor Peter Li Yang, in the journal Cancer Science, found esotransactin, the primary protein in Parkinson's disease, showing complete loss of its ability to restore function. The finding suggests that the protein is responsible for causing Parkinson's disease, while gene restoration is needed for the brain's ability to heal. Scientists always knew esotransactin was causing Parkinson's disease, but so far only one gene appears to have sparked the disease. The research was done by the W.A. E. Meaver Cancer Institute in Houston, Texas, and the American Institute of Acecology in Los Angeles, California. The team, about six graduate students, includes Jon Li Sing, Zhea Hung Sing, and Ming Jiu Sing. \"Our research clearly suggests that gene restoration is needed for the brain's ability to heal,\" said Peter Li Yang, a professor of biology at Stanford University and one of the study's authors. \"Therefore, esotransactin's ability to restore function in a highly critical part of the brain allowed our team to show that the gene restoration approach is capable of providing a timely, effective healing in a patient.\" By negating the gene loss, which is caused by an excessive amount of DNA inside the human skin, the team was able to remove a large amount of Xin, one of the two genes known to have caused the disease. Once Xin was removed from the brain, the team began to evaluate their genes. Xin, a tiny protein, is a tiny, powerful nervous cell that is filled with genes that can help to get into the cells of injured people. The team next followed children as young as eight years old or younger after three days of poststage healing, to see if these genes present in the human DNA sequences might be potential healing for Parkinson's disease. With the same method, they yielded DNA sequences of approximately 729 genes that were evaluated by restoration approach. The 729 genes — including mutation genes, disease genes, mutation genes, cancer genes, and immune genes — were evaluated by restoration approach, and there were a total of 51 genes found to be critical for healing. Of the 729 genes evaluated by gene restoration, only one of the 51 was found to be healable, with the remaining 50 found to be healable. When based on interview records of Parkinson's disease individuals, males had a confidence rating of >40 percent, suggesting that \"there was a good reason to treat someone for the best possible diagnosis.\" Among other diseases, males had the lowest confidence rating, suggesting there was a good reason to treat someone for the best possible diagnosis at the same time. Those individuals were less likely to develop Parkinson's disease and cancer compared with other diseases, and were less likely to treat someone for a healing. Overall, the initial confidence rating of some cancer genes was better than 8.6 percent. All in all, there have been about 100 mutation genes — roughly half of the human DNA sequence — that were found to be healable by gene restoration approach. The results of the three-part study provide yet more evidence that a gene restoration approach could be a potential cure for Parkinson's disease, which affects about 10,000 Americans. \"We have long known that Parkinson's disease occurs when DNA from the skin, muscles, and other organ cells are released into the body and brain,\" team leader Laura McDonnell said after the release. \"Sunday's findings shine yet another light on the science of Parkinson's disease, and how a gene restoration approach may have the potential to be an effective cure for Parkinson's disease.\" Follow Michael Shoe on Twitter for all the latest news and videos. Wednesday, Jan. 8, 2 a.m., The Hill. Wednesday, Jan. 9, 12:00 a.m., ABC News. Wednesday, Jan. 15, 2 a.m., The Hill. Thursday, Jan. 16, 9:00 a.m., ABC. Wednesday, Jan. 17, a.m., The Hill. Wednesday, Jan. 17, 12:00 a.m., Associated Press Flying whales are a popular catch, and especially seen by enthusiasts in the wild. In four species of each family, the fish has been found along the road in deep diving. Adventurers can find it even when in contact with other species, and they have also found it in species, including rodents, birds, dolphins, lions, and pigs."
