        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000487",
        "sourceId": 487,
        "text": "The ACPD said on Friday that the department is still investigating the criminal activity of the suspects and will release the information as part of a police review of their arrests. \"We can't underestimate how easy it can be for someone or a man who prediced and raped girls for the sake of the surviving child to commit another kind of offences like that,\" Indianapolis Police Commissioner Daniel Clemenson said in a statement. Clemenson said the ACPD is also considering a redesign to its enforcement policy, which is part of an effort to gather more information about Indianapolis's criminal history and culture. \"We look forward to working with the city to improve its enforcement policy,\" Clemenson said. Anyone with further information about the suspects can call the Indianapolis Police Department at (888) 422-5902. Read or Share this story: http://indy.st/1Q2EX3E Image copyright Getty Images Image caption It was declared safe by US military aircraft after being involved in the Middle East drone strike A British-made cargo aircraft took seat at an airport in Syria after it crashed into hot water off the coast of the Middle East. American Airlines said the crash occurred during a flyover session in Araya at around 2.30 GMT and several emergency responders on scene at the airport reported no major damage. The rear end of the aircraft, identified as RPC 7863, underwent heavy damage which included a hole in the wing belt during the earlier flight. Flight officials also reported that some parts of the suspension and pilot belt had been breached. Rescue troopers were ordered to leave the airport at around 2.30 but it has not been determined whether there was any damage to the aircraft at around 2.30 GMT. The reason, the airline claimed, was that the aircraft had been safe for now. Despite evidence, American Airlines disputed the claim, adding: \"There are no reports of any injuries.\" An American Airlines spokesman said: \"We have not received any reports of any damage to the aircraft, as until around at this time, there had not been any major damage to the aircraft. \"As of right now, we have not heard nor experienced a report of any damage related to the injuries of the aircraft. \"Fly troopers who have had reports that there was a leak in the engine exhaust have been contacted and will do everything in their power to keep the aircraft as safe as possible during this time.\" Image copyright Getty Images Image caption The aircraft was declared safe by US military aircraft after being involved in the Middle East drone strike American Airlines said the British-made aircraft took seat at an airport in Syria in Araya, 7km north of the city of Sharia. \"After the flyover, we received a call from British flight troopers that the aircraft was safe and they left the airport immediately,\" said a American Airlines spokesman. \"After the fly-over session, the aircraft was handed over to Araya and informed that it was safe. \"We have not at first been informed that any injuries were involved or if there was any major damage. \"Ansars flight troopers have since instructed us to check for any injuries and return to the airport to do an evaluation and verify that the aircraft had been safe for now. \"Thrine, known as rescue troopers, have stayed at the airport since they were involved in the crash but had been instructed to leave for Araya when it was no longer possible to fly over to the sea. \"Ansars have instructed us to evaluate the damage to the wing belt immediately following the crash and try to maintain as high safety standards as possible. \"Ansars' safety team will continue to monitor the damage to our flying belt to ensure safety and evaluate everything carefully.\" Image copyright Getty Images Image caption American Airlines said the cargo aircraft was equipped with 10 jets and other equipment developed by the US Air Force Special Operations team prior to its arrival in Syria at around 2.30 GMT. An airline spokesman later said: \"We have not received any reports of any damage to the aircraft at this time. \"As of right now, we have not heard nor experienced a report of any damage related to the aircraft.\" US at around 2.30 The British-made aircraft was reintroduced into Syria by American Airlines within 24 hours. The US Air Force Special Operations team was initially ordered to stay in Jordan, but American Airlines confirmed that the aircraft was safe within 24 hours, before being reintroduced into Syria at around 2.30 GMT. There were no reports of any major damage. American Airlines confirmed the aircraft was \"safe\" within 24 hours, before being reintroduced into Syria at around 2.30 GMT. It was also equipped with 10 jets and equipment developed by the US Air Force."
