        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000518",
        "sourceId": 518,
        "text": "\"The bill is unlikely to get through the House and Senate,\" McMullin said. \"It's too soon for Congressmen to say, 'OK. This is the way the bill is written. This is the way the bill is written.'\" The bill's sponsor might not make it through the House or the Senate. Some conservative activists have argued that their proposed legislation would not resemble a final version of the Affordable Care Reform Act because it would repeal the Obamacare repeal mandate. The Affordable Care Act is unlikely to pass through the House or the Senate. But the bill's sponsor might pass through the House and Senate. While the debate could revolve around why a final version of the Affordable Care Reform Act isn't likely to pass through Congress, conservative activist and law professor Robert Robinson said he would support a final version of the law because other final versions are more likely to get through the Republican Party controlling both the House and the Senate. \"There is no political discussion about carrying out a complete final version. But a final version would have to be passed in the House and Senate, and even through the Senate,\" Robinson said. \"I don't see, even if it is possible, how it would get through the House. Maybe even through the White House. But it would be impossible to pass the Affordable Care Reform Act. Just don't see it passing through the House and Senate.\" McMullin said the legislation could still get through both the House and Senate controlling the Republican Party. But the bill might not pass through the House, the House or Senate. \"It probably won't pass through the Senate, regardless of which political party,\" he said. \"That would be a big deal if the bill passed through the House and Senate. But the bill might not even make it through both the House and Senate.\" McMullin said the legislation only has about eight days to pass before it can get through the House. McMullin said it was still up to the Senate and the president to decide whether to get the legislation through the House and Senate, and approve it. \"We still have eight days to get a passage. And once the legislation becomes approved, it is up to the president to decide it,\" he said. \"If the bill passes through the House and Senate, it can get through the Senate, reach through the House, or, as many observers have suggested, reach through the House.\" UK LONDON (Reuters) - British special forces retired from Afghanistan on Friday in response to serious security concerns that have emerged since the country’s dramatic entry into war in 2002, Defence Ministry spokeswoman David Briggs said, referring to British troops supporting the Afghani military. US Special Representative to Afghanistan President Donald Trump described the deployment as “a decisive and important military deployment”. “ Special forces have played such a big part in ensuring the security of Afghanis since the country’s conquest,” Briggs said in a statement released on the day before Trump’s birthday. British special forces’s withdrawal from Afghanistan follows an attack on an American base in the eastern Afghan province of Fallanatka, and the discovery of a hideout for the Afghanistani Support Terrorist Units (AKGB). It is unclear whether special forces have since been retracted. Special forces are part of a population of roughly 5 million Afghanis, or around 9,000. They are part of local exit extremist militias that are loyal to the Afghanistani Support Terrorist Units (AKGB and other exit militias. A Defence Ministry spokeswoman said British special forces pulled out from Afghanistan following the attack on Friday because of a “serious security situation” but that the country was no longer more safe from further attacks. “The British government recognizes serious security concerns and encourages special forces to remain in Afghanistan to ensure their no further withdrawal,” the spokeswoman said in a statement. “They will continue to operate in Afghanistan, and the British government is committed to making that commitment.” An official from the country’s infrastructure ministry, speaking on condition of anonymity, said that special forces would continue to operate in Afghanistan. The UK Defence Ministry would not say how long British special forces would remain in Afghanistan, but said it was hoping to increase their presence to around 15,000 troops by the end of the year. The deployment marks the first time at which British special forces have withdrawed from Afghanistan since the country’s entry into war in 2002. In 2010, more than 30,000 Afghanis died as the country collapsed, following a terrorist attack that had removed British special forces from power. British special forces had also deployed to Afghanistan recently when a group of militants who were fighting off the military burst into a gunfire at a checkpoint, which left at least 46 dead."
