        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000556",
        "sourceId": 556,
        "text": "\"Through a computer simulation it is shown that symptoms of depression causes a decline in levels of anxiety that are associated with higher symptoms of anxiety and depression,\" said Helen L. Campbell, PhD, doctoral student and co-author of the study. The research was conducted with the National Institutes of Health (NIH), Obesity Disease division of the National Institutes of Health (NIH), the National Institutes of Health (NIH), National Cancer Research Institute (NIR) and the National Accident Research Institute of the National Institutes of Health (NIH). \"Obviously, this is a real breakthrough in analyzing the link between anxiety and depression,\" said Campbell, a professor at New York's Manfield University and a lead author on the research. The study was published in the online issue of Neuroscience by U.S. Department of Energy. The findings show that changes in the external cortex associated with anxiety disorders are associated with a decline in preemptive behavior. The study is based on advanced computer simulations of the activities of the frontal cortex, showing that anxiety is associated with higher symptoms of anxiety disorders. \"The findings show that anxiety can be influenced by a hypothesis that changes in the external cortex cause a greater decline in anxiety and depression,\" Campbell said. \"Through computer models and observation of changes in the external cortex have enabled us to understand the direct effect of depression and anxiety on preemptive behavior,\" she said. \"The findings show that a greater decline in levels of anxiety is associated with higher symptoms of preemptive behavior and severer depression symptoms. \"Countless studies have demonstrated that changes in the external cortex can decrease the levels of anxiety that are associated with a decline in preemptive behavior.\" \"Obviously, this study is a real breakthrough in analyzing the link between anxiety and depression,\" an expert in neuroscience and mental health said. \"The hypothesis that changes in the external cortex cause a decline in levels of anxiety, allowing preemptive behavior to improve, has been further supported by this research,\" said Michael F. Shaw, PhD, doctoral student and co-author of today's Neuroscience review. \"This research could help spark new research advances in the fields of behavioural psychology and psychiology,\" said Julie Shaw, PhD, doctoral student, co-author of the study and head of the department of psychiology at the University of Maryland, who is a lead author on the research. \"Cleaning of the activities of the external cortex can help identify diagnoses and symptoms of different symptoms of depression and anxiety. \"This research allows us to further support the hypothesis that a decline in symptoms of specific anxiety disorders can be influenced by changes in the external cortex.\" \"Through advanced computer models and computer simulations of the activities of the frontal cortex, together, the research could help lead to diagnosiss of anxiety disorders that are associated with a decline in preemptive behavior,\" said Shaw. \"This research allows us to understand how depression and anxiety affects preemptive behavior. It is a real breakthrough in understanding the link between depression and anxiety. The research could also help spark new research advances in the fields of behavioural psychology and psychiology.\" WASHINGTON (Reuters) - AT&T, the United Kingdom's second-biggest telecom operator, may have seen an increase in the company's quarterly revenues, assisted by the increase in cash flow associated with the launch of its cell-phone service, according to Britain-based research firm Reuters reported on Tuesday. \"At&T has emerged as a major growth driver for the cell-network industry in recent years,\" said Nigel Blame, chief research analyst at Reuters, according to the report. \"At&T is currently still well ahead of Apple and even Google in terms of revenue both in terms of user experience and cash flow.\" In the Reuters report, the company reported 13.7 percent in cell-network revenues for the quarter, AT&T's third-growing number, behind T-Mobile (T-Mobile, -1.4 percent), Verizon (Nexus, +0.1 percent) and Verizon, +2.3 percent. BEIJING, Sweden (Reuters) - A Swedish judge sentenced former army sergeant Anita Pogierne to five years' probation on Tuesday after she accepted a plea deal in return after she was acquitted on charges that she engaged in sexual assaults while being held in a southern Swedish army prison. Pogierne was found guilty at a women's district court in Stockholm, but has yet to be sent to trial. The three-judge panel granted a plea deal to Pogierne based on medical evidence, but did not decide if she would be eligible to serve two years' probation."
