        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000576",
        "sourceId": 576,
        "text": "It wasn't as easy as many imagined, but free-agent Morgan's playing style hasn't completely changed. He's been practically puck-free at every stage of his career, so the Flyers decided to give Morgan the green light. While he rarely set career-setting goals and continued to score defensively when he was needed, coach Dav Dunbar made sure Morgan stayed focused throughout the game. \"Mim was always the right guy to stop the puck. He was always the right guy to score,\" Dunbar said. \"He'd pull the puck in there and not force the puck back to the finish line. He was the right guy to play defensive and do both things, and he did both things. He was the right guy to play defensive. And because he scored a little bit more often, he drew up the puck into the air more often.\" Morgan's play wasn't necessarily going to help either. His defensive struggles were probably one of the biggest reasons why Flyers coach Mike Janien gave Morgan a second chance in order to keep him off the bench. \"I was a little bit more concerned if (Michae) gets away with the puck, and I think it's going to affect how you play,\" Janien said. \"He needs to keep playing. He needs to stay focused. He doesn't need to get away with the puck. He does need to get away and make use of the puck. He's played better defensively now than when he was a kid. He wasn't off the bench then. He played better now, but he was able to get away a little bit more.\" The Flyers decided to give Morgan a little extra time, and they figured he would improve his efficiency defensively. Because he has better movement and speed at his center and is able to pull the puck in and get down the ice, Morgan will need to improve his outside playing style, Smith said. \"On the one hand, Matt and everybody else knows that on the other hand, he's going to have a hard time doing his own thing,\" Smith said. \"It's really a tough time for Matt. But he's going to end up putting off the bench and scoring every game, and that's exactly what he needs to do. And that's okay, but he's going to end up being a big player. And he's going to be a different person. And no matter what he does, he's going to end up doing the right thing and being a big player.\" \"He's a unique player, not a distraction,\" Dunbar said. \"I don't think Matt ever tells you any difference at all. Like I said, he was kind of just going to be able to do it, but he couldn't do it, he couldn't. And he's just going to be able to do his own thing. And he was going to be able to do it, but he couldn't. And he couldn't do it.\" Russia's Foreign Minister Dmitry Mattis said recent comments by the Russian president were more indication of progress in addressing the country's cross-border crisis and that there will be a \"several new path\" in economic cooperation between the two countries. The comments followed Russian Foreign Minister Dmitry Mattis's comments, which went out to the Russian parliament on Tuesday, during a meeting with Russian President Vladimir Putin over the weekend in Warsaw. It stressed closer ties between the two countries, resulting in stronger economic growth and bolstering trade ties. Mattis said the two countries were committed to improving cross-border cooperation. \"We have been committed to tackling the cross-border crisis for more than two years and we will continue to do so,\" Mattis told reporters on Tuesday afternoon as he was attending the 45th annual International Conference on Cross-border Security. Mattis said the conference was about the \"several path\" on how to tackle the cross-border crisis and on the need for re-establishing the country's economic situation. He added significant progress was being made by both countries to address the country's cross-border crisis. Mattis said Russia and its NATO allies needed to build trust in themselves in order to defeat the crisis. \"I think they got a little bit more conservative this time, because they're still telling the facts,\" Mattis said. \"There has been a lot of improved cooperation between the countries so that they can sort the facts out. I think you can tell them the two countries are under the same economic condition and they're in different conditions.\""
