        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000657",
        "sourceId": 657,
        "text": "When you talked about a Kickstarter campaign, I wanted to talk to people specifically about Soulwave. Where do you think of where you want to focus in the next round of the game? Is there anything else that's something you're working on right now? What kind of Kickstarter campaign are you waiting for? Are you waiting for more money in order to have more funding that you bring? There’s a different approach to having people with more money who want to give more. I’ve been thinking about that for a while, and I think that it’s kind of a great idea, a way of giving people the answers to some of the questions that a lot of people have been asking. What are you trying to do? How much money can people actually give? I don’t know how much they can actually give, but I feel like what I’m trying to build a support for the game is actually making sure the community has all of the support they need, based on the features that are already available. I think that’s really important in the overall development of the game, but I’m sure that’s going to be a big selling point, in terms of how much else we need to give. If you look at the release of Soulwave, you’re going to see a lot of people giving what they need to give to the community. We’re currently working on a new Soulwave title for the PS, but we haven’t gotten to work on it yet. That’s a big part of actually developing the game, and I’m sure a lot of people haven’t seen it hanging around as long whether we’ve developed it or not. Is there any other thing that you have that you’d like to see that will play an even bigger part in the development of the game? I’d like to see some of that foothold that a lot of the gamers already have. I’d like to see and talk to as many more people as possible to spread awareness of where the Soulwave is coming from and shape that in such a way that both gamers and developers are going to be able to realize that they can use it and that it is already there when it officially comes out and that’s going to help a lot. So every developer is going to have to try how the system works and what it actually looks like when it comes out and try to actually be able to see what the content is when it comes out. And if that’s true, there’s going to be a lot of awareness that this is going to be an important part in making sure the game works in a good way, so we can make sure more and more people are still getting into the game and still playing. That’s one of those things I’m really excited about, like sandbox, karo’s release, and other things that are moving in that direction. It’s a big part of that. It’s just a question of what’s going to happen next. It’s just a question of what’s going to happen next. It's a week since the first round of changes to President Trump's energy and climate change budget went into effect. The single most significant of them is the wind and solar strategy, whichaims to eliminate approximately $135 billion in greenhouse gas emissions by the end of 2016. But the bigger part of that is that the most significant round of policy changes has taken place so far. The president's latest budget aims to create an additional 23 million jobs by 2050. The Congressional Budget Office estimated just last week that the fossil fuel industry, which accounts for 80 percent of the United States' greenhouse gas emissions, will generate a net value of $2.7 trillion over the next decade. Many environmental groups have called for Trump's budget, to help address what the president describes as a \"serious\" problem with the country's electricity industry, which leads to global warming and climate change. But so far, three strategies have been devised. The first is the wind strategy, which aims to add 1,000 megawatts of solar capacity -- roughly one-third of the existing wind capacity. The second is the electricity strategy, which aims to eliminate 250,000 megawatts of solar capacity by 2050. That plan promises shifting between 60 percent and 70 percent of the country's electricity generation to fossil fuels, which leads to global warming, global warming and climate change. The third, the Republican-led clean energy strategy, centers around a plan that promises to steal all that clean energy from the electricity industry. At the moment, that plan has, at its peak, over half a trillion dollars in operating value. Trump's team said in a statement issued on Friday that they could no longer run those companies."
