        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-16ea6daeb24f-000900",
        "sourceId": 900,
        "text": "“This is a very divisive bill. I’m sure this is a very divisive bill because it has a hugely negative impact on wages and on the well-being of businesses,” said Michael J. Hensen, senior vice president at Business Action Coalition, a business think tank at New York’s Chamber of Commerce. “It’s such a bad deal for the economy.” New York’s roughly 120,000 workers will be out of work when they turn 65 years old when the Senate Appropriations Committee passed the House bill on Monday. Opponents argued the legislation would kill funding for the state’s school programs, and would force states to lay off teachers who can’t afford to pay or have decent children. The teachers’ minimum wage is $344 an hour, or $636 an hour. Opponents say the bill would guide the state into recession because it has nothing to do with slowing the economy. “Republicans have stopped talking about the ‘school dollars’,” said Fund for Growth, a conservative advocacy group. Opponents say the legislation would have little impact on Governor Tom Mulvey’s budget. The high price of oil — the crudes generated by the oil pipeline — would have a big impact on businesses, they say. They say it would cost each worker more than 30 percent of their jobs. The legislation, which cleared Mulvey’s desk in April, would allow eight months for an state employee to renew a wage when she is making less than $50,000 a year as a private employee. Proponents claim it would reduce benefits for private employees, increase incentives for businesses, and encourage immigrants to come to New York. “If the governor had not crafted a corporate tax plan, that wouldn’t have happened,” said Patrick Green, a Republican fellow at the U.S. Business Action Coalition who wanted to see the bill passed as a measure in the state Senate. It was signed in July. The House bill would have created a corporate tax credit, allowing people who are making less than $50,000 each year to pull out of the state’s funds without being told when they will return to work. Activists say the bill would have shut down local governments and have hurt the economy in the state. Critics say it would have guided what they say is a broken financial system into recession. Officials say it’ll take up to six years for economic research to find a cure, or it can take 10 to 15 years for economic growth to recover. Officials say the Senate bill will be harder to pass into law if it receives 59 percent of a vote, which compares with 52 percent in favor. Opponents argued the legislation would kill future funding for the state’s school programs. It would boost the state’s university programs, and force states to lay off teachers who can’t afford to pay or have decent children. “The legislation won’t come to the General Assembly for good, but a great deal of work has to be done before it can become law,” said Edward Curtis, Mulvey’s spokeswoman. Curtis said the legislation would cost more than $1 billion and create tens of millions of dollars in lost revenue. A federal agency would need to start a disciplinary investigation. The Obama administration has denied such disciplinary investigations and said it plans to pursue them when the legislation returns to the General Assembly on May 2. The U.S. Center for Civil and Demic Rights said in March the legislation would only affect local governments that do not comply with federal law. Verizon decided not to acquire T-Mobile in the wake of the recent data breach in the telecommunications network. The FCC warned that its action may harm Verizon’s reputations and reputation as a result of the move. “T-Mobile and AT&T are two important competitors in America, and the FCC was concerned today about a possible Fourth Amendment breach as the FCC moved to acquire a competitor as a result of the recent data breach,” a spokeswoman said in a statement. The FCC largely vetoed Verizon’s move, arguing that its action was contrary to the Fourth Amendment. “The Fourth Amendment prohibits any person or entity from communicating, receiving, enforcing with, or sharing any form of cable or Internet communication with any person or entity that may have the intent of pos a threat to the existence, function or operation of a telecommunications network,” FCC spokesman David Sotomayor said in an 11-page statement. “In the end, Verizon wants to pay off its net debt by ensuring everyone has the right to pay it,” he referred to the fact that T-Mobile shares have surged in recent weeks."
