        "text": "\"I just thought, 'I don't think he's going to cause any problems.' And I thought 'Could you figure it out?'. You see all the talk, and that all just bled out right there,\" said Gates, who featured in Saturday's opening shooting as a character who's a school boy. The film follows the climax of his first film \"Piem & Son,\" which opened in September on Netflix and sold nearly 1.5 million copies. Boyle is played by a student named Niya from Azka, Russia. \"Some of the strange things ever happened,\" Gates said about the character in an interview leading into the shooting. \"Perhaps I don't know any, but I know a lot of things about him. The fact is that he's basically a kid who will go on and on when he gets picked up, but he's a school boy, so I don't know what his name used to be. Because I kind of memed him up as a kid, it's incredibly weird when you have someone like that. You see all this other stuff that he's involved in, and it all just kind of bled out there.\"'real reason for the death' Gates explained that he became first aware of Boyle between 2005 and 2006 when he was filming for his movie \"Dashura\" in that region, when movie director Sara Sakoya said the character \"is an animal that lives in a desert.\" Although Gates wouldn't go into the specific reason for Boyle's death, he said he suspected it was due to an actor who became interested in the character while working as a teenager in the beginning of 2006. \"I just thought it was a suicide, because I wanted to make such a movie,\" Gates said. \"I just shook my head. I thought, 'If I want to make such a movie, then maybe people should be asking me to do something interesting.' That was the real reason. I felt like I was still dead, and that never went away.\" Gates added that Boyle's death may not have been caused by a suicide, though the way he saw his character during shooting may have been different from what he experienced during his time filming for Netflix's \"Piem & Son.\" Gates, meanwhile, acknowledged that Boyle \"got so angry\" soon after seeing his character that he felt a momentous feeling of emotion. \"I don't think he felt that feeling of emotion,\" Gates said. \"I mean, since he would sit on a camera and give a word to everybody in the scene, when he saw such a great character, I don't think he ever felt that kind of emotion.\" Click on images for a slideshow of the interviews that were conducted with Gates and Boyle prior to shooting. The pro-Iraq Brotherhood on Saturday announced plans with the United States, Iran, Turkey, the United Arab Emirates, Israel, Saudi Arabia, and the United Kingdom for a renewed fight against Islamic militants in Syria, following an attack at a Daesh nightclub in central London on Saturday that killed 22 people and injurred dozens more. A Syrian state news agency, however, reported the attackers used car bombs and tear gas. The Iranian-backed al-Shabaq group claimed responsibility for the attack. The country’s official news agency reported that the group had not revealed the location of the attack, but had been interviewed by journalists. The agency also reported that Daesh, an Islamic city located along the Syrian border, was liberated in July 2014. “If we want to be a city, we have to be a city,” said Janir al-Nusra, the Brotherhood’s deputy-chief and general commander. “They will take us to the first spot,” he added. “They say they will take us to the second spot. Since they control the liberated territory, they take us to the second spot.” But Iran’s ambassador to the United Nations, Mahmoud Mohammadi, singledd out the atrocities by accusing it of “war crimes.” “Iran does not commit a war crime, and the United States has, with the backing of its partners, committed war crimes,” Mohammadi wrote in an email, summing out the plans to free Daesh. In a Facebook post published on Thursday, the leader of the Brotherhood, Abu Abd al-Madab, declared: “We will do everything in our power to officially end this war.”"
