        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000234",
        "sourceId": 234,
        "text": "Jackson also praised City of Oakland's \"Make Community Jobs First\" program, which provided job advisers for Kyle Whiteburn, then assistant deputy secretary in the Department of Employment. That effort aims to not only create jobs in all communities, but also open up jobs in other sectors in the city. Health care, day care, manufacturing and information services are mentioned as potential areas to consider for those sectors, he said. Jobs like engineering, engineering, communications and programming are among the areas under consideration for the young founders, and those jobs are constantly opening up new opportunities for people to find great work. Cool jobs are rising in new sectors, Jackson told us, with more than 200 million people projected to become a part of the labor force. For its part, the purpose of the project focuses on promoting those new jobs, as well as moving the industry in new directions. \"We're looking at the prospects for growth -- in the heart of Oakland and the heart of the entire South -- where we've seen a 50 percent increase in jobs in recent years,\" said Jackson. With the Oakland City Business and Development Association and the launch of a home-building website called SkipPad, Oakland is taking the next step in efforts to create meaningful jobs. \"When you're a business or building  an office complex, you want to build innovative businesses. So when you have the founders who want to build hotels, build restaurants, businesses, build women's homes, it's a real motivation to encourage them to do these great things,\" said Jackson. \"In many sectors, I've seen certain jobs that contribute to energy efficiency. For example, you couldn't generate electricity to run on the roof of a car, you couldn't have a restaurant, you couldn't have kids to sleep with, and you had to have proper transportation. So that's just a start.\" There is a lot of progress yet to be made toward smart technology, for example, the initiative launched by Michael Jackson and venture capital firm Venture Capital in 2015. The proceeds of funding was donated to a venture capital initiative that contributed to $1.9 million in Jackson' budget. For Jackson, the project is an effort to try to bring smart technology to a sector where wages are seriously lwindling. \"Really, our main goal is to create a platform that can be used to drive job creation with smart technology,\" he said. Jackson emphasises that the auto industry has historically moved with self-driving cars and has shifted to smart technology. He disagrees, and while electric-car driving has been a more common phenomenon, there's been a dramatic reduction in traffic accidents nationwide, according to Whiteburn. \"My point is that going forward we need to develop a strategic approach that can help us move forward with innovation and create a sustainable economy. As more people innovate in goods and services, we need to be able to afford better transportation systems, improved infrastructure and access to emerging technologies. We rely now on smart technology and that's the key,\" said Jackson. \"For future generations, our inventions need to come from advanced technologies, including electric power plants, water storage, generators, fire power, emergency management systems, electrical systems, all those kinds of equipment that make them easy to use and easy to manage.\" Brazil’s next plan: How to lower the gender gap between born boys and females Brazilian-European boys and women combined have born more than 1230,000 babies over the past decade – an 18 per cent increase among pregnant females, just 11 per cent among males and just over 10 per cent female. However, boys are the only example of that increase in maternal age, but they are also among generations that excel at their size and creative abilities. The image of the baby boy by the Brazilian painter Aguido Leonardo (1831-1844), best known to President Lucios Castro, paint a beautiful picture of how Brazilian society is changing. In 1960, there were 15,365,000 born babies in the provincegua – 21 percent more than a decade earlier. In 1970, Leonardo launched the ad featuring 16,65,000 babies from artist Aldo Guvarán, moving the maternal age from 17 years to an average age of 39 years. There were now 8,03,000 born babies including 897,500 women. The median age for born females is 2.8 years compared to 0.25 years for men, 1.6 years for women and 2.5 years for women. The same gender gap – 2.8 years longer for women – also means a mean median age of 27 years. For pregnant women, now an average age of 32 years. That is way lower than the median for born women in Brazil, where the median female median maternal age was 30 years. Moreover, it is well below the median for pregnant women."
