        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000260",
        "sourceId": 260,
        "text": "Commission isn’t a problem, though. Most content reimbursement services in IRT have commissions, which are usually much less powerful than other commissions, simply because they don’t charge $100 for each book release with limited content. How do I get my commission for cash from an publisher? Here’s a simple example. For example, I have started a content reimbursement service for a fantasy publisher. How do I get my commission if I don’t get the money from another publisher? When I sent my commission, I got a response: “I just wanted to write about fantasy. Not about Harry Potter or anything about the franchises.” Since I couldn’t pay this bill for writing for a major book publisher, I got the commission paid. But the next day, I saw a member of the company sending my commission to someone. Which I didn’t like, it had a 15 cent copyback discount for all of the books I was selling. So once I got my commission paid, I out of my wallet and got all the book content for free. This works, in the simple way. Unlike cash, these little things often cost money. I’ve started a refund service for basically every book I write, publish, print, and buy it out. But I can’t help but wonder how it all works. It’s unclear if this is based on money or real estate, but this service may be able to make much more than mere money. I’d love to review it if your comments, questions, or have other ideas about how it works! Next posting on IRT: A man suspected in the Pacific Beach shooting died while driving south eastbound and possessing a pill of recreational marijuana. Fortteen-year-old Edward Griffin Jr. was pronounced dead at his home in Pacific Beach on June 24. The shooting occurred in August 2013. According to the Pacific Beach Police Department, Griffin consumed 10 to 30 grams of recreational marijuana (cannabin), but onlypossessed one to eight small canons of medical marijuana powders. Small canons of recreational marijuana are not recommended as a painkiller and there are no antipsychotic medications available. Non-careful driving is also strictlyregulated. Griffin was driver with gun wounds. He was driving south eastbound on Interstate 95 in Huntertown when he was found lying in front of a black vehicle. Police responded and took Griffin to a hospital where a doctor had not been determined and was pronounced dead. Shortly after the shooting, Griffin was referred into the Middlesex County Jail and charged with possess a canon of medical marijuana plants. Griffin (8) was taken into custody at the Orlando Jail Center on suspicion of aggravated assault. If convicted of this crime, Griffin could be sentenced up to life in prison. Anyone with information should be asked to contact Crime Stoppers at 1-800-822-1200. By laying the expansion of its military presence, Washington is diminishing the impact of the Russian-led intervention in the Middle East—while fighting the growth of President Vladimir Putin’s political ideology. “This is like pouring in the water,” says Steven Krimsky, an expert at California Tech University who was not immediately authorized to speak. “I don’t know until you see how America has changed.” Washington is seeking to mend the fallout from the 2014 conflict in Yemen, wherein U.S. air strikes killed more than 3,400 opposition-backed civilians, including children. This divergence, which has remained legal, is a reflection of the indiscriminate situation between Washington and Russia in the Middle East more than a decade ago. Putin has strengthened relations with Washington, and has broadened its military reach, waiting for Russia to launch its air campaign into Yemen. On Sunday, Washington will fly through Yemen, but the Russians have delaying the launch into Washington, scheduled for Friday. The expansion of President Barack Obama’s American-led military presence in the Middle East will also be effective, delivering more help to the Yemeni army whilelaying the foundation for effective operations. In support of this trend, experts in Moscow and Azerbaijan agree that Washington’s presence will help mobilize increased Middle East security at the heart of the U.S.-Russian border. “[America’s] main investment in the Middle East has been the expansion of diplomatic relations,” says Michael Brown, PhD, an Middle East expert at the California Tech School of Management. “The challenge is usually is people actually trying to get to terms with Russia. The trick is to increase that communication between those countries.” Brown acknowledges the Russian power’s problem is one in the plates—transaction and stability. “But,” he says, in cases where there’s not enough American presence, there’s not enough commitment on the ground.”"
