        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000268",
        "sourceId": 268,
        "text": "To be fair, I wasn't born in Japan. I was already in a training camp for the First World War, and I was born overseas. Yes, I lived in Japan. It wasn't unusual. Growing up in a country that the Japanese considered Imperial was seen as part of Japanese Empire during the Second World War. The climate in Japan was really violent, and it seemed like there was something to be done. There was a lot of both physical and emotional violence. Some of the more violent were animal killings and pet killings. It was Japan for starters. It was really just a country, and there was so much violence. There were also stories about victims of violence that were so much forgotten. I remember killing the first person when I was a kid in 1922. I would shoot people in the back. I just don't remember my childhood, or what I started doing. My dad used to pick my kids. I used to talk. I used to pick them up. In the grocery store, meal was served. I was a little nervous at the time, but I don't remember my childhood. I don't remember even my remembrance about killing that person before that. I can't remember my family's reactions to that. One of the things I remembered about living in Japan was the kind of things that weren't normally known about living in Japan. For example, I remember having that guy groping me out at night in a cab with my friends. It wasn't going to surprise anyone. I didn't get it. They were more of the same race and I said different things. He had a black sister, and she didn't speak by name. He was American and had a nice home, and a nice family. I experienced violence from the time I was 5 years old. I remember getting on a cab due to a car accident, when he pulled himself off the wheel and pulled me off, with my family. Mom and I had a nice relationship. We grew up together. We had a nice home. We had a nice family. Neighborhood was a little weird. We had always been good friends. I finished junior school, went home and went to junior school. I didn't finish college early, or graduate college. I had relatives, and they were all good friends of mine. I had times when it felt bad for my sister. When she was in college, she was a mother. When I was in college, she would tell me a kid who didn't grow up. She would tell me I was a kid. I don't really do photography. That's what photography that I do. I don't really do. I had a violent encounter with a friend of mine in Japan. She visited me over the weekend, talked about me a lot, was very nice and joked about things. I had bisexual affair. She knocked down on me and asked to get with me. She didn't back down, but she was groping on me, and I ended up walking around in bed with her. A friend who I married after she had decided to live in Japan, America. That story of abuse I had never completely hidden or hidden. Did Japanese people think that Japan was at war? I didn't really know one thing about it. It was something that did touch me personally. I had a lot going on. There were things going on about me that I could have been killed. And the idea that Japan was at war touched, really touched me. My lifestyle changed very quickly after the Second War broke out. I was nice, smart, and respected, personally. When I was a kid, I had the idea of going out and fighting for Japan. I fantasized about the idea that Japan was building a military force against Al Qaeda in Iraq. I loved that idea. Eventually, I decided not to spend any time protecting it. To this point, I was so scared. The fact that I could deal with it made me feel scared at a time when you weren't an adult who could completely deal with a situation. I saw so many people in Japan. That affected my life. There were people here during the Second War that were so comfortable. I wasn't starvated. I was lost. I didn't have that emotion I felt toward someone. I grew up with people who respected my parents. My cousins. My uncles. My great grandfathers. They were horrible. I didn't know them well. They were good friends of mine and I didn't have to do anything for them. They just were nice. I grew up with good people, but I didn't realize that I didn't respect my parents as much."
