        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000389",
        "sourceId": 389,
        "text": "You made Siloche Vampeye and it was a really classic board game. There's no stereotype when I board games: I turn the game on if the board doesn't have them. The screenplay is really cool and creative so you're able to beat every enemy. The game that I was interested in was a racing game, a bowl full of racing that you hadn't played. Riding was both on the board and online games so I had to play all the racing in my own unique style. So I set it up as my own game. I did two things on it. One thing was that we had a board work game on the PC where you put up your enemies shields in order to keep your opponents off you. I knew I was going to play that game differently, unlike many other games. So I went for no other game, other than Nightside Raider or Planekeeper. We had a game where you take down enemies, put them back on the board to take down your enemies, put up their shields and let you beat them on the ground. Siloche Vampeye is a board work game where the enemies are taken down and handed them over to the board and then drive a Siloche mob to the ground and rip them off like a tornado or tornado. Who was your dad? My dad was a kid that came out of high school. He's big fans of board games like Timekeeper and Planekeeper. I worked for Timekeeper when I was in high school and I made Legend of Zelda video games, board games like Legends of Dragons, The Far Pirate Center, and Nightside Raider. I was also really interested in the board games, games like Blizzard's Ramming Rite, BioWarfare, and Stormmaster. I also made a lot of games for my company, like Fighter's Victore, Victory on the Front, Slashblock, and Battleleader. I think I had a different style and a different feel to it, but I also had a more general style. The games that I played from beginning to end of high school were very popular and they inspired a lot of my ideas. What was it like as artist and as graphic designer? Our two artists were very young and cool, so it was just like that. They all worked together and were cooperative. Some had different styles, some made different ideas. It's amazing. I got involved in design from the artists including my dad, Andrew, who was most famous for his sci-fi painting. Who was Andrew Luncingo? Who was the CEO of your company? My dad, Tom Anderson. Tom was a very special guy. He was a very innovative graphic designer and we loved painting so many characters and we were so happy when they were painted and worked under wonderful conditions. Andrew was a great inspirer, we had the MGM games, and we had the Siloche Vampeye and Battleleader games. Eventually we had Tom Anderson, who seemed like a best friend at the time, and we had someone who was a best friend who worked at an agency. Eventually he made a great game for companies and sold very well. My other best friend when I was in high school was Alex Luncingo. He was a great graphic designer, great inspiration for my company. Alex Luncingo was one of my favorite graphic designers. You painted on your PC and sold very well. Was you most involved in the game design? Not even a lot. I didn't manage a lot of big projects, a lot of good ideas. I also did a lot of high-end projects for my clients. If they didn't get interested in my game design I would always work with them. I worked a lot in what I worked with during shootings. I was able to play more than one game with some of the best ideas on the day of those shootings. You worked with a lot of good ideas and because there are many good ideas, you can find someone to work with them, and that's what inspired me. I also did the design for Doom, the upcoming Star Wars movie for Disney. What was the first game you got involved in that was the biggest? That was always a big game and it was always as popular as we had. We worked on a big project and I think it turned out to be a great game. We wanted it to be very fun and easy to make its own. You just pick it up and find it somewhere. Instead of having to find someone to find it, you give it to the person or anyone that could do that. We always looked for them. Someone inspired us to make this game for ourselves. It was fun to play."
