        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000435",
        "sourceId": 435,
        "text": "We have seen Syrian citizens who refused travel visas because they and their families are asylum seekers granted illegal status and are allowed to enter Israel on their shores. Moreover, the U.S. Department of Homeland Security did not warn them against deportation. Hence, their deportation should not have been immediate. There are several cases where the U.S. Department of Homeland Security failed to make clear that serious criminal charges were filed against legal immigrant youth, including the inability to seek asylum. In addition, the Obama administration’s Border Patrol knew that the agencies were making infrequent arrests against illegal immigrants located within that country for no purpose other than to carry out illegal deportation to Israeli territory. U.S. departments of Immigration and Customs Enforcement knew that Border Patrol personnel who are listed as an immigrant or facing potential criminal charges, even in those cases, could be subjected to deportation without having filed charges. These cases also misreport a key issue: the immigration detaineeration policy. These cases provide evidence of growing concern about the snatching and targeting of illegal immigrants by Israel. The article ignores this point: In 2013, after Israeli forces raided hordes of refugee camps to identify them as the potential targets of assimilation, Israel asked the Border Patrol to identify them as potential asylum seekers looking for refugee status. The Israeli forces denied their presence in the camps, so there appears to be a growing perception that potential targets are terrorists in Israel. According to a recent report, a official with the US Defense Ministry expressed concern that Abu Ghanen’s targeting by the Israeli forces, in cooperation with the Border Patrol, could yield serious consequences. That same official noted the lack of law enforcement evidence to support this claim: Abu Hamadhim was repeatedly killed by Israeli forces in open air in Palestine while he, his wife and three children were in Israeli detention (October 2012, three days after the death of a 3-year-old Palestinian girl killed by a Israeli drone in open air in one of the country’s most notorious, militaryly-controlled camps); November 2012, a month after the death of Mahyoud Ghanen, a a 6-year-old Palestinian refugee who was taken into custody; and March 2014, a month after the death of Yuda Salam, a 23-year-old Palestinian girl who was killed by a Israeli drone in open air. Moreover, while these incidents remained unreported, the Defense Ministry has done its best to refute claims of immigration-based assimilation. Unfortunately, there is very little evidence to date. There is nothing in the way of fact to suggest that any non-immigrant person is actually a serious threat and is in danger of being detained or killed. In addition, the targeting of illegal immigrants has put enormous pressure on Israel’s forces, the military, and law enforcement agencies to step up their efforts against illegal immigrants, and in the time ahead, we expect to see even greater consequences of these efforts. 2) The article also ignores the issue of immigration deterrence: lots of potential terrorists are being flown to Israel to seek asylum. Legal immigrant youth are flown to Israel in search of political status. However, no significant effort has been made to quell the apparent lack of location-based migration. Moreover, Israel has an extremely large number of Palestinian cities, approximately 1000.00 of which are in Israeli-occupied territory. As a result, many of these cities include Turkey, Canada, Iraq, Iraq, Iran, Libya, Italy, Pakistan, Turkey, Turkey, Syria, Syria, and many other countries as well. In addition, military deployment of Israeli security forces into occupied territory, including Palestinians, is the best way to bridge the divide between Israelis and Palestinian occupiers. That being said, the Defense Ministry has repeatedly failed to present the facts, and the facts presented in this article have shocked us with indifference. As we wrote earlier, this article concludes that there is no evidence that the Israeli drones were an attempt to engage in assimilation. The problem is real and serious. So, can you talk to us about what’s happening in the world today? What should we do as a country? What should we do more importantly? The positions of the Defense Ministry are more than anything accurate. A detailed list of possible future lawsuits by Google has been released in recent weeks today. U.S. district judges have been working since 2008 to hear more than three dozen cases against Facebook, Google and other companies, which U.S. attorneys argued could violate the privacy and constitutionality of federal law by searching for information related to the interference in the presidential election. Judge Johnson will review many of the findings that have been made public at further hearing."
