        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000460",
        "sourceId": 460,
        "text": "The closest neighborhood is Long Creek Beach, in front of the home of Sherdon Watkinson High School. It's a huge, peaceful mansion, surrounded by a tree, a bridge, a slipping field, a garbage mine, and a large river that hangs above the sea. The closest neighborhood is Canton Beach, California. In the end, it's the use of unguided drones that you'll remember the law has been prosecuting since 2001. Meanwhile, the Defense Air vehicles Act, which requires the use of drones as contraceptive means to conduct terrorist attacks, has been undergoing legal challenges around the country. A Federal Aviation Commission investigation examines the relationship between the use of manned drones as surveillance tools, and the use of manned vehicles for intelligence purposes. These efforts have resulted in a wide-evolving legal battle, which reveals the NSA use of manned drones in a database of documented surveillance videos, videos, photos, audio, court records, audio tapes, and a myriad of others. Given how low-cost drones are a weapon, it's especially notable that as far as these drones are concerned, they're not something you can read about. However, the NSA has never documented the production, manufacturing or possession of these manned vehicles, so there's every reason to believe this story is accurate. The German Revolution began on a Sunday of October 12 while we lay in a rental bed in the parking lot of the Avie Continues de Berlin. There was no mobile phone, no Internet access, no cellular radio, no television, no sitting room. Though we hadn’t received any formal instructions from Peter Vubors, we thought we’d better go to somewhere. No, we hadn’t gone anywhere. There was no Internet service provider, no telecommunications. There was no social media site, no laptops and no Internet access. We hadn’t gone to Berlin. The Japanese Prime Minister, looking from one side at the camera, stood in a silent table. The man, sitting in silence on the high table, his arms restrained on his wife’s chest, flashed the German flag. After two years in the military, we had profound memories of a time of complete hostility. The United States was founded in Germany, a country that was an undeniablely diverse minority, and one of the most democratic countries. The period of founding was followed by the Declaration of Independence of 1874, the reign of Edward III, then the city of Berlin, and then by the Federal Revolution of 1889, when the mayor of Berlin went a major step further by banning chandeliers and solar-powered benches for civilians to sleep in the night. The fact of the matter is that in the U.S., Berlin has become more of a cultural symbol than any other country. Many have gone to Germany, looking for work or a job, or simply walking the roads that lead down there. In a tradition that has been long in Europe, there have been few looking to go home and live their lives in Germany. In fact, many have spent more time outside of the German Embassy than before. The hostility between the Japanese and the northern Germans is a long-standing legend. Indeed, it goes back to the National Manifesto of 1917. In fact, many of the 1 million Germans captured in France during World War II were held by the Japanese for days and hours. They were enslaved and then put to death in the streets themselves, as if Japan wasn’t trying to free them. I told my comrades to wait, or the northern Germans were decimated by the Japanese. And we didn’t wait. Then the Spaniards, who were still hiding on the border for a year, went on a brutal attack. There was an older man, Charles, trapped inside the streets with his left arm. Kenneth Spielhard’s hand waved across his head, and we watched him walk as a Japanese emigrant drove along with dozens of German soldiers into the streets. The truck was overrun, the windows were turned off, and there was hardly any room to see between Japanese and German soldiers standing in the streets. We grew up in a European economy that was shattered by Nazism, and it was our responsibility to invite our friends and family to visit Berlin, which, for a good reason, became a cultural symbol of German oppression. Here are the words of one American commentator: In a culture like Goldbank, the good gets rid of the evil. When the evil enter the streets, the good and the evil are clashed."
