        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000768",
        "sourceId": 768,
        "text": "In those years, Martinez's diet was more moderate. As a kid, he slept with his twin brothers three to four hours a day for a few months, while his brothers lived, mostly at night, around their parents' Glen Oak apartment. As a young boy, Martinez lived in bolstered neighborhoods. As a junior, he'd take cars at city parks, clean up the parking lots and take trips to the parks. He also'd walk out along the Interstate Bridge to watch basketball games and attend college athletic programs at State College. His mother, Margaret, was from Glen Oak, a rural suburb. South Florida won national championship games when Martinez was in middle school, but middle school offered a boy the opportunity to play football. \"It was like a lot like most kids loved football,'' his mother, Margaret said. \"It just kept coming. It just felt like the same way the Florida kids loved football, just like the Florida kids loved football, it felt the same.'' As a true freshman in middle school, Martinez played for the NFL teams at the University of New Mexico and Columbia. He became committed to football in 1991. He was signed by the San Bay Sabres as a free agent signing and earned a commitment at Florida State in 1993. At quarterback, Martinez became one of the Tigers' top prospects, scoring one of the greatest TDs ever recorded in the AFC. He had 1,354 yards, 48 touchdowns and 45 interceptions in 291 games. The dream resonated as much to him as to his parents. They met in Florida City in 1997. Together they would play football every week. By the time they got into college, they were able to score a touchdown. \"If he wanted to do it, you know, he could stay fresh and get the ball ready to go on target,\" said Jim Brooks, the Sabres' youngest quarterback of the decade. \"He'd drop the ball, and his arm would go up. It was kind of a dream coming as a kid to look at it. And I thought it was a miracle.\" Martinez helped the Tigers win the championship last year. \"I thought, 'All right, but I don't know if even the first few seconds of the game, it's been the longest I've ever been there,'' he said of his fate. \"[But] in my mind, was it a determination, a drive to say, 'Come on, let's see what we can do.'\" Martinez scored two runs in the first half to lead the Tigers to a 20-0 lead in Denver, but Brooks decided to use right-hander Evarden Martin in the end zone in the second half. Not always impressed by Martin's performance, Martinez took a stunning comeback and hit a two-run double in the eighth to take the Tigers to a 29-7 lead. He earned a decisive score on a beautiful ground run in the 19th inning, which secured a 17-7 victory. In the eighth, running back Jason Peyes recorded a end zone hit. \"In the end, obviously, I was trying to be like, put bread on your butt,'' Martinez said. \"I just -- I didn't know where the world was coming from right then, I thought it was happening. I would go, you know -- I would wander around the world, but I would get everywhere to find me. I just -- that wasn't going to the case.\" Martinez underwent a complicated acetymology procedure when he tore his right arm. It's been years since he suffered a car accident in California in late October, and it was surgery that has left his heart trembling with emotions. \"It was crazy,\" he recalled. \"I'm crying. But I couldn't believe it in my 30 seconds. I didn't know what was going on. My dad and I were covered in tears.\" One of his dad's greatest memories of those years came when he was 17, when he was a high school senior in Santa Clara, California. His father, former Hall of Fame pitcher Andy Waters, had heard the news of his dad's car accident and was deeply surprised that his friends were marching out on the street to show up to their favorite games. \"When they didn't know what's going on, I was like,'' Waters told the Tigers, his mother Casey said. \"All of my friends were like, 'Wow, what did you do?'\" Waters said. \"So I thought, 'All right here, I was so surprised.'"
