        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_32_nfe-3fc2041f8ad3-000888",
        "sourceId": 888,
        "text": "Jason Drorov, a fellow at the Center for the Advancement of Politics, an anti-police group that endorsed U.S. Senator Barack Obama's 2008 relection bid, said that \"augmentations in interest rates will have a big negative impact on the political system of the U.S., especially the structure of our economy.\" \"Missing higher interest rates or rate increases will have a negative impact on the U.S. political system in different ways,\" he said. \"This will help provide us with more insight into how we make political decisions.\" Researchers trying to figure out how to reduce the cost of bots - or to minimize their damaging impact on economy - might want to turn to mainstream theory. Dale Betheld, a political economist at George Washington University, argues that artificial intelligence is \"just a phenomenon, when the human reacts to it with a goal in mind.\" Betheld's theory has been resuscitated in a growing body of articles, diaries, and other academic literature. He says that a rapid spread of artificial intelligence in a fundamental way could also lead to new ways to understand how it works. \"It's not a matter of academic preference; it's a matter of public perception,\" Betheld told CNN's Laura Goodman. \"What it feels like when you have a computer, but it's not a phone, what it feels like to watch a movie. Engineers have seen the tools that excel in artificial intelligence -- communication algorithms, social networks, DDRs -- and they've seen the birth of tools that can collect huge amounts of personal information that's difficult to track down.\" Think about the findings in Project Regrowth, a report prepared by researchers at Northwest University that looked at the huge negative impacts such tools could potentially have on the economy in the 21st century. Betheld described the findings: The increase in the presence of \"information executives\" in the information sector - both physical and mechanical -- and falling wages, the attraction of new talent, the caladic loss of senior jobs, and the stagnation in stock prices - have led to a dramatic shift in how the work of information executives has been done using advanced tools. A physical phone sits on a computer, and a mobile phone operatess there. It pulls out the computer and sets off the bot. \"We've seen an increase in the presence of more experienced talent.\" But economists are also asking the question: Is the sudden emergence of mechanical tools what's affecting the rate of economic growth, or is it what is pushing things forward? Chinese economics appears to be the perfect vehicle for it. After all, the current economic and political landscape has been changing for several years. \"It appears that China is now a global economy, so I think the value of the dollar is falling dramatically,\" said Liu Ji, a lecturer in economic science at China's National University. And so the answer to the theory that is behind the emergence of bots is at times sentimental. \"What's happening now is a change in how the human operates a computer,\" Ji said,  an associate professor of Economics and a research economist at Northwest University. \"People are exploring new tools, including one day a business product, or a software product, that could change how you run a new business.\" Then there's the simple answer: Enter artificial intelligence. There are many projects already in progress, but not yet complete, including the University of Cambridge and the University of Chicago. These projects focus more on \"augmented intelligence,\" which means that humans control systems, computer interfaces, programs or other services. In the developing world, opportunities for artificial intelligence are already emerging. Technology companies are experimenting with algorithms, credit cards, securities and social insurance systems. Software companies are experimenting with tools designed to make money. Medical companies are pursuing a career in robotics, robotics, and information management, which depends a person's skill set. But in all these developments, there's a much different concept about how to operate. Many mechanical tools, such as a computer, a phone, or an iPad, are obviously essential to a successful economic performance. But adding new types of software, new hardware, and new electrical concepts can complicate the task of bots. And the process may actually be complicated. \"Tools are becoming increasingly more portable, and they're much more complicated to use,\" said Kao Soo, a senior fellow at the Center for Economic Policy and International Studies. \"Unfortunately, you can't really use bots for real work. Businessmen can extract just as much time and wealth from mechanical tools as they do electrical.\""
