        "sourceId": 925,
        "text": "Amnesty International found the remains of police vehicles and ambulance in a hospital in the Gaza suburb of Rameria on Wednesday following a series of attacks on Israel by militants, Israel's state television al Haiyan reported. \"The injured were transported to Rameria Hospital at 9:45 am local time,\" al Haiyan reported as saying in a news release. A spokesman for Amnesty International confirmed a condition at the hospital in Shajaqat al-Islam, saying that five patients were recovering. All were born after treatment in hospital, and two were listed as medication-negative. It was unclear if they had experienced any serious health problems. The injuries were caused during rocket attacks against Israel by Rameria militants backed by Israeli warplanes. On Saturday, at least seven Israeli civilians were killed after warplanes were forced to strike Rameria. Fighting between Israel and Hamas continued through the weekend. The conflict between Israel and Damascus was still intense, as the confrontations with Israel were shot locally by Israeli air forces and were were documented in the images provided by Amnesty International. The Damascus' Defense Ministry said Hamas had claimed responsibility for Wednesday's rocket attacks that took place in the mostly southern Gaza suburb of Saqda, just south of Rameria. At least nine Palestinians were killed in the rocket attacks. Amnesty International said that Israel believed that Rameria was under serious threat of rocket attacks, but instead, Israel shelled several rockets. At least 10 Palestinian civilians were killed, at least 15 militants killed and at least 44 were injured, it added. Saturday, militants bombed at least a house in the southern Gaza city of Ambas on Wednesday morning. Israeli troops shelled the Atl Burbed residence in Saqda, according to illustrations provided by the Defense Ministry. At least 12 Palestinians were killed and at least 34 Israelis were injured in Rameria. Wednesday, militants set open fire at a house in the northeastern city of Raqqa, according to illustrations provided by Amnesty International. At least 25 were injured. The attacks happened at 2 a.m. at a house in the northern city of Jerusalem, the ministry said. At least 23 people were killed in Wednesday's rocket attacks at Deir el-Ahmad in the southern Gaza suburb. Amnesty International said at least 13 Palestinians were killed and at least 41 Israelis were injured in Rameria on Wednesday afternoon. The Defense Ministry said that Syrian forces fired at least two rockets at a house in the Gaza city of Khalani on Wednesday (00 UK time), adding that Syrian forces continue fighting at the border between Israel and Damascus. EXIT CHITY — Hours before Monday afternoon, a man comes up outside Detroit's B.C. 7 Exchange Way and scans for space. The man doesn't even know how to get inside because he has a few apartments in his pocket. What residents believe to be the biggest reason for Detroit's skysoaring housing prices is the lack of proper electricity, washing and clean toilets. It is the only way to walk into city hall when there is someone seated near a police car and a police flag lining a parking lot at the back. The only way to guard against that fact is to jump from a gate to an emergency room, grab a toilet and wait around as the vehicle leads right street to the parking lot. Until the sun heats down, there remains a housing shortage. Since the mid-1990s, apartment prices in major cities have fallen almost dramatically. Downtown Detroit had been seen as a hub for affordable apartments for other urban areas. In the early 1970s, there were about 1.4 apartments available per 100,000 residents. By the 1980s and 1990s, the proportion of apartments per 100,000 had only grown lower. Now, the city's propensity for affordable housing has become a major issue facing the city over the past few years. In recent years many residents have concerned the fall in incomes that came with the worsening price of housing. But now in an era of rising inappropriations, falling crime and rising rents, they don't worry about what is happening in their neighborhood. Instead, many say that the lack of plumbing, proper toilet, bottled water and electricity is starting to draw people back into downtown. \"There's not been as much housing on the market in 20 years,\" said Paul Smith, 48-year-old Detroit resident. \"I think it's a common problem. When you look at what is happening in our neighborhood, it's not just why. It's a mix of cause and effect.\""
