        "text": "During the Democrats’ six months in office, Republicans pushed through a variety of policies including eliminating Obamacare; extending Medicare’s subsidies; enacting laws to ensure that the majority of Americans have affordable health insurance; lowering the cost to get health insurance for pre-payers; cutting insurance premiums in so far as it affects those who receive insurance subsidies; and repealing tax credits. The House overwhelmingly approved a filibuster of Obamacare laws on six months, and on February 11, 2011, Republicans had the chance to pass a narrow but meaningful repeal of the president’s sweeping health care reform plan. But Democrats failed to repeal the act, the House refused to pass it, and ended up being another filibuster. On March 17, 2011, Americans were told to repeal Obamacare even if individual premiums were replaced by a base credit. And on January 29, the House leaders launched a filibuster campaign (like the one the Senate Finance Committee did) to increase coverage for two individuals rather than a single person. With the Affordable Care Act here, there is not that much room for this policy to explode. The Democrats’ chance to pass these laws will give Republicans in Congress more power to address the increase in health care costs. The repeals of these bills will give Republicans more power to influence voters in order to change their policies. In fact, this issue has been a central part of politics for nearly thirty years. And even if Obamacare bills pass the House floor, it will undermine Democrats’ ability to move forward with health reform policies. The costs of health care reform bills will vary considerably for various reasons. The most recent bill on the House Appropriations Committee, for instance, is less expensive than the one passed by this week because it is “within the legal limits in implementing as set out in the law.” (In other words, the Affordable Care Act, as Time points out, “proposs several key provisions that will be included in the passage of the president’s health care reform.”) These bills offer many costs, but one bill’s potential costs differ considerably from the others. To understand the costs, let us take a look at some of the highest costs of health care reform bills to date and how much these bills are worth spending. 1. Care Costs and Funding: Since 2000, health care reform bills have cost American taxpayers nearly $2.7 trillion a year. Currently, they are spending about $1 trillion less on these services. Moreover, health care reform costs have also skyrocketed with the help of taxpayers. In many cases, the amount of coverage provided by middle-income households is on average of the amount provided by the federal median budget for health care reform bills. Middle-income households face health care reform costs that are 50 percent higher than lower-income households. In many cases, they include $138 million in payment, $40 million in refunds, $91 million in rehabilitation costs, $1.85 million in tax credits, and $100 million in alternative program coverage. 2. Health Care Reform Costs: In 2010, health care costs included $80 million, $163 million in payment, $449 million in reimbursement and a $10,000 premium for a doctor. These costs together account for about 87.1 percent, or about half of total health care reform spending by American taxpayers. In 2010, costs totalled $24.4 billion. Since 2013, the total total costs have jumped by $2 billion. Considering the enormous financial burden that American taxpayers face when paying for additional health care costs, we should also note that health care reform costs have actually more than doubled over the last decade. The Treasury Department estimates that in 2010, paying health insurance premium for premiums accounted for $13.6 billion in revenue, while in 2013, paying health insurance premiums accounted for $2.6 billion in revenue. Moreover, health care reform bills in 2010, continue to provide affordable coverage for the poorest. But health care reform bills will continue to cut funding for programs such as Medicare and Medicaid, even though these bills have included reduced funding for premiums for those who can not afford health insurance, according to research firm Ere Garten Research. (It will hopefully “help make people pay lower costs” by advocating against some of these bills). 3. Pre-Payer Health Insurance Has Be Increasing. Beginning since 2000, more than two-thirds of adults in the United States have pre-payer health insurance. But the cost of prepayments has steadily increased among adults around the country. According to Statistics Canada’s Health Care Data Center, about 70 percent of all adults have one or more prepayments in their health care. In D.C., the average cost is $2.85 million for each adult who has multiple prepayments. Those numbers have increased by $1.8 billion. In 2010, equivalent to a 50 percent increase in the health care cost."
