        "text": "\"At the same time, there must be a comprehensive and independent investigation. It is urgent that the IAEA takes steps appropriately and does everything it can to ensure a fair collection of information,\" said Steven Jacobson, Australia and chairman of the National Committee of the Organization of Australia, who is head of the Working Group of the Committee on Wildlife Welfare and Conservations at the World Wildlife Society. The study, published in the journal Wildlife Welfare, Wildlife, Health and humanity, was part of the largest international investigation into the loss of the world's most endangered wildlife by the United States, France and Australia. The development comes in the wake of the 2009 Podobra disaster, in which more than 7,000 different wildlife species across the world were lost or removed offshore. A new international commission has unveiled the IAEA's own findings, which concluded that climate-driven rises, drought and rising temperatures contributed to the global loss of wildlife. Ellis said the United States, New Zealand and Australia had been in close cooperation to prevent the global loss of wildlife, and stressed that central issues of wildlife welfare, health and humanity had been addressed and that additional steps should be taken. He said US President Donald Trump and France's President Franco Françoise agreed there was strong evidence that increasing temperatures had contributed to the loss of wildlife. \"The key question is the impact on ocean conditions resulting from droughts and the rises in sea level,\" he said. The study concluded that \"the extent to which climate changes and meteorological events have contributed to the loss of wildlife remains well understood, and that the current research and review are critical in the crafting those conclusions and future findings.\" \"There are also significant environmental impacts of wildlife conservation, such as the loss of ocean water life, increased ocean frisation, and the destructive environmental impact of carbon dioxide emissions,\" said the report's co-author, David Malpatson, of the University of Adelaide. \"Whilst additional research has been conducted, there is a need for studies to take place around the globe to evaluate the potential effects,\" he said. Australia's membership to the Indian National Monument, the global status of the Indian Monument, and growth in the wildlife industry are key concerns of the report. Brian Brown, the general manager for wildlife affairs at the Australian Wildlife Foundation, said that relations between the international communities needed to be deepened across the globe to promote wildlife rights and focus on promoting common wildlife conservation practices. \"The International Union for Wildlife Conservy is a nongovernmental organisation dedicated to the conservation of indigenous, protected and preserved wildlife across the globe. It, in addition, is a global body concerned about wildlife rights,\" he said. \"Equal steps must be taken to ensure that these concerns are met, starting with the building of the international cooperation of wildlife conservy organisations.\" According to findings of the study, the amount of wildlife that was removed from the ocean grew by 58% around the globe in the last 1000 years. Co-author of the report are Elise Sullivan, chief executive and executive director of ISA-Australia; Kate Gordon, chief executive officer of ISA-Australia; Karen Webber, president and executive director of the International Union for Wildlife Conservation; Jean Junner, president and CEO of the International Union for Wildlife Conservy; Tom Carter, president of ISA-Australia; Phillipe Danningson, president-elect and CEO of the International Union for Wildlife Conservation; and Michael Sullivan, president of the Inter- Wildlife Conservy Association of Australia, are also co-author of the report. The comprehensive report reviewed current trends and trends in the areas of conservation, animal welfare, wildlife, wildlife welfare and human health. The study highlighted the need to take a number of steps to ensure the health and welfare of wildlife around the globe, including a review of international laws governing access to waters and resources. Among its findings, the commission advised governments to priorize wildlife conservation practices and to ensure environmental protection, including protection of fresh water sources and fresh water sources. \"The need for wildlife protection must be measured relative to the individual's everyday daily activity, regardless of the age, race, religion or other ethnic background,\" the report concluded. The report also urges people to be careful to refrain from dietscontaining health-perfect ingredients, and to refrain from diets with healthy or health-perfect ingredients. KILLY FLANURY, Ohio - A State Public Schools principal says school districts shouldn't pressure kids to throw away toys that they shouldn't be using. In fact, the State Public Schools says kids shouldn't worry about about any toys they take while at school. But principal Shana White says a kid encounters a season or two of toys that he probably shouldn't use, and if he does, may become ill. White says he's teaching kids about the toys well before kids get enrolled in school."
