        "text": "SAN FRANCISCO (AP) — A man is behind bars in a deadly shooting on a Santa Clara home that killed a woman and injured at least four, raising concerns about a possible fatal threat, an attorney said Sunday. Nathaniel Leirman, 69, was arrested in Alameda County without bail after Sheriff's Department released a report of a fight inside the home that left the victim slammed, bloodied, grozed, neck beaten and burned to death. Two other Leirman victims -- Elijas Martez, 36 — are held in a jail where he was charged with manslaughter since March. Leirman was found alive shortly after 3 p.m., court papers show. Investigators released a video of Leirman striking the other victim with a assault shotgun and then hitting him with his hand on the back of the head. It was unclear if or when Leirman was not injured in the shooting. Investigators said drug use was the primary factor that led to Leirman's death. \"We're unsure of who was on drugs at the time of that shooting,\" Leirman's attorney Fred Gold told reporters Sunday. \"Our client was being on the drugs of a regular basis.... Of course, if they were being hit by a drug, that would indicate that that client was being on drugs of a regular,\" Gold added. Leirman was being held on a projected bail bond of $200,000, according to police. Leirman's victims, who were involved in the two shootings that killed Jose Maria Lutra and Jenna Valmez, reportedly reported them to police, according to the Sheriff's Office. Leirman, facing 10 counts of manslaughter and two counts against Elijas Martez, was in custody for a time and was briefly questioned about Valmez and Elijas Martez, according to police. Police said a confrontation began early Sunday after Leirman went to see a woman walking north of Madison Avenue. She yelle for Leirman to get out, police said. When she refused, Leirman hit her on the back of her head several times, then punched her on the side of the head, police said. One of Leirman's victims told police they followed the victim from a nearby residence, where they saw a cloud of black blood streaming outside. Leirman told police that an attacker ran to jail and he hit him in the head, court papers show. After the shooting was filmed, Leirman was searched by a white woman named Morales who entered her home in the 400 block of North Madison Avenue, police said. Police said Leirman killed her multiple times during the search. He reportedly followed her through a nearby front yard as she departed the home, police said. She came back to the yard and managed to escape once her body wounds were found under her clothes, before disappearing from the yard and striking the ground, police said. \"He tried to help other people,\" Capt. Christopher S. Ayokowski of the FBI, told reporters, \"but he never tried to get the victim out of there.\" Morales's defense team said she was not 18 to 20 years old or young at the time of the shooting. Investigators are focusing now on Jose Morales, the 69-year-old man who was charged in last month's murder of his 50-year-old daughter, whom he later found alive in his apartment in the neighborhood. Leirman reportedly was standing next to a victim when she was fatally missing. Morales was arrested without bail on March 15, 2016. On Sunday, a 32-year-old man was arrested for the murder of a 51-year-old woman in the neighborhood. He was accused of striking and killing victims with a 16-year-old boy, among other counts, the Alameda County Sheriff's Office said. The suspect's court date will be determined in Santa Clara Circuit Court. During an initial hearing, police learned that around 10:15 p.m. on Sunday, a victim entered a home in the 400 block of North Madison Avenue and shot her in the head, striking the woman with an assault rifle in her hand. When Leirman got inside, the victim forced himself out of the home, court papers show. When he awoke to the woman and found her alive on a pier outside of his apartment, he shot at her. His wounds recovered, according to a warrant."
