        "sourceId": 49,
        "text": "Hacking into your cell devices, of course, is no fun — you can sit down in the office, play your games, spend time with your friends, travel by bus to the grocery store, have a hot drink with some friends, watch the HBO broadcast or head off to the bus for a ride. But unless one of you has a thing to do, you don’t need to. The world of the mobile phones hasn’t changed much over the years. A recent release of new data from the U.S. Consumer Product and Information Administration (PCA) provides further evidence for this trend. The IMB notes that within the last 12 months, the minimum hourly wage for adults has increased from $123.79 to $15.26 an hour. Conversely, as the IMB notes, the number of working minimum hourly wages aged 18 to 24 has increased by 11 percent, although just 15 percent of those were aged 22 to 24. Overall, the IMB notes that the number of U.S. adults who earn less than hourly at an average wage rose by 25 percent, from $123.79 to $15.26 for adults. Similarly, for workers in Asia, the Philippines and Great Britain, the minimum hourly wage grew by 70 percent, from $117.54 to $15.21 for adults. During the last 12 years, UK workers have seen a consistent increase in minimum hourly wage in the U.K. by nearly 30 percent, and much of that increase was relegated to the U.S. And the IMB notes that the “future hourly wage increased within the same period.” The IMB’s data, of course, also provides some insight into where the modern world is with mobile phones — from Samsung to Apple. If you’re wondering exactly where your phone came from, if you’re following the transition from Samsung to Apple, here’s just a few good examples of the IMB’s whereabouts. But something to be noted about the IMB’s data is that while there’s a very long way to go for mobile phone maker, it’s a huge leap forward for the industry. It is the technology innovation that has had its biggest impact on the industry. While prices are declining, of course, consumers are wasting fewer time downloading apps or games — typically about 30 minutes a day. In line with the IMB’s data, consumers are also spending more time playing a mobile game — which has allowed them to make informed decisions instead of pouring money at them. Percents haven’t changed, but the maker of mobile phones appears to be heading in the right direction. Alan Tibs is the senior editor of FoxNews.com and a contributor to the San Francisco Chronicle. Follow him on Twitter (@AlanTibs), or contact him on Facebook (@ALANTIbs). The IMB’s report can be found here. Staffer Syldon Alex, outside a company headquarters office with a dignitary, reviews the plant at Solarie headquarters in San Toribo, California, on May 7, 2017 in Oakland. San Francisco staffer Syldon Alex, outside a company headquarters office with a dignitary, reviews the plant at Solarie headquarters in San Jose, California, on May 7, 2017... more Photo: The Chronicle Photo: Photo: The Chronicle Photo Photo: The Chronicle Image 1 of / 1 Caption Close GM envisions first chance in U.S. solar energy in 2020 1 / 1 Back to Gallery Solarie, which built a large range of solar panels in partnership with the San Francisco-based Solar Power Group, has opened a plant in the center of its global headquarters in San Jose. The company's plant will allow companies to recruit up to 1,000 people, and the company anticipates the plant will help recruit another 1,000 people, in part to increase its talent base. Solarie spokesman John Hutch told The Chronicle that the company is looking forward to this new plant even though it's a long way yet to go. \"We're definitely looking forward to its opening,\" said Hutch. Solarie will also offer a range of solar office units, which the company said will help people recover from work, improve their quality of life, and improve the quality of life. Solarie spokeswoman Eugene Püter said the company's new plant could create jobs and bolster management if staff feel they don't understand how they need to be employed. \" Solarie staff need to get out of work to go and take a break and run their home,\" Püter said. \"They do their clean, clean, they ride a bicycle, do whatever their body needs to do.\""
