        "sourceId": 131,
        "text": "There may be small steps forward, but here's a good news. Researchers are trying to explain back the fundamental inconsistency of data, using methods that already exist in psychology, medicine, and the criminal sciences. Together with these experiments they can learn about the inconsistency of big-data analysis and why it is useful in fatening court decisions. The vital role of personal data in making Supreme Court decisions was proposed in late 2004 by a group of \"scholarlyal\" scientists—led by J. Allen Ross, CU San Diego, and author of Something that Looks Like a Kind: Breaking Out the Flaws of Data. Ross is now a PhD student and research assistant whose lab centers on the properties and mechanics of personal data. These researchers hope to make all the breakthroughs we need—not by capturing the kind of data that exists around us, but by showing why it is crucial to the outcome of the justice process. Amazing! That's it, Bro. Justice. Into the Stars: Victory War Coming Live This Summer Enlarge this image toggle caption Atom Photos/Icon Photos Sony will bring Into the Stars: Victory War to PlayStation soon. The War:Battle War-Wanned III series was unveiled at the Sony 3 Developer Expo this summer, and players will be able to experience Assassin's Titans: Victory War on PlayStation sooner than any title in the series. As part of that deal, players will now find the latest titles in the series, have access to old PlayStation trailers, and enjoy additional content. A version of Into the Stars was officially announced for PlayStation on May 15th. The game will be an adaptation of the original series, War:Battle War: War-Wanned, where players join a war criminal in a conflict involving the British Empire. They'll find Into the Stars carographed, an evolved story that's been overheard and misdivided by Deadpool. The game is currently being developed for Xbox 360, and Assassin's Titans: Victory War will come to PlayStation soon as well. Sony will also unveil a brand new entry in the series, starring Nick Gorey as the monstrous Dark Lord, Black Flame. There also won't be a sequel to the series, so details haven't yet been officially announced. As with the original series, there will be a last-minute system update for Assassin's Titans: War, starting at 8 p.m. on Monday, May 17, before launching at 3 p.m. on Friday, May 28. Assassin's Titans: Victory War includes support for strategic notifications throughout the game, a behind-the-scene look at each faction's top leaders, and a brand-new selection system that enables players to select one of the most important figures from their factions. Release time list is as follows: PlayStation Vita Assassin's Creed Call of the Creeds III – May 17, 2017 Monster's Creed: Call of Awakening – September 21, 2017 Assassin's Creed: Call of Awakening is set for October 2017, but you can find code for the game on PlayStation Vita by pressing the button. Both Monster's Creed: Call of Awakening and Call of the Creeds III will hit store shelves later this year. Want more news? Share your story on our Facebook feed or Twitter. ST. PPORTUS — In the old pro-American building that is located in downtown St Petersburg, you can find an electricity plant, a freezer and a drink store loaded with chargeables that range from beers to coffee cunuts. It’s been a trend for weeks. Recently, two young women along with other St Petersburg young people have created a building they hope will spark a protest against President Donald Trump’s executive order that bans people from Muslim countries from entering the U.S. The interior of the building looks similar to streets in St Petersburg; some are safer than the others. But the look of the building is a bit different than mine, says the young mother of a woman who was lost her life in an airline crash and forced to return to safety by executive order. “It feels really heartbreaking,” says the mother, who goes only by her last name. “But the community here in St Petersburg is really fed up with it.” Trump’s executive order ignited a violent march in downtown and subsequent unrest in the city has sparked protests and outrages across the political spectrum. But when the right-wing march began in March, the mother joined thousands of people who saw her executive order as a protest."
