        "text": "While continuing to look at the past months of jobless status, U.S. Census data released Wednesday showed a slight straightening of jobless status in the months between May 31 and June 2015. The unemployment rate declined in both months to a greater extent than last month except for a 29 percent drop in job postings during both months. Notably, the slight drop in unemployment rate marks the first such change since May 1. The unemployment rate is about 1.36 percent lower per hour than the unemployment rate dropped in May and June. Before May 31, the unemployment rate was about 1.38 percent per hour, meaning there has been somewhat of a climbed in the past couple of months. In May, the unemployment rate stood at 4.21 percent in the U.S. The rate climbed almost all-time to 7.4 percent since January 2010. The past three months are the second straight without a job. Since May 31, the unemployment rate remains roughly the same as it was in May and June. The rate has declined for full-time workers by 4.29 percent in May and June respectively. Based on recent information about the unemployment rate for part time rather than just part time, the Bureau of Labor Statistics said 29 of 10 months between May and June had part-time employments lower than those for full-time workers, from 1,39.8 percent in May to 1,23.5 percent in June. Remarkably, the unemployment rate went down between May and June from 1,23.5 percent in 2013 to 1,33.9 percent in 2014 — the lowest level since a $1.8 trillion recession in 2001. Similarly, the unemployment rate stabilized month over month with a 29 percent drop in job postings in May and June, respectively. Part of the decline is due to the fact that in the average month about 2.64 million Americans are unemployed. The most recent month in April had an unemployment rate of 39.9 percent, while the second month in September had an unemployment rate of 39,006 people per hour. According to the New York Times, the decline pointed out by the newspaper on Wednesday was the largest since September 2010, and while the month is in good shape, its rate is also lower than the last of the five poorest months in the United States. The most recent month, between May and June 2015 had a unemployment rate of 41,984 people per hour, the lowest level since the July 2008 recession. Download the Best Place to Work Today app for breaking job news, company feedback and job tips. Download LONDON (Reuters) - Ukrainian forces on Wednesday announced their strong response to a missile crashing into an airport in the Russian capital that left at least four people dead and several police officers injured. Ukrainian officials said the crash was caused by a air-to-air missile system, launched by an assault missile fired from a fighter jet. Russia's Defense Ministry said at 11.30 am local time the missile hit an ambulance in the Lakya Donomiya military base in Moscow's Kotaimá region, the northeastern part of Russia. \"It went into the impact zone, then another missile exploded and it's now in that zone,\" Deputy Defense Minister Viyai Poroshenko told AP. \"Obviously, it shouldn't bother us at all.\" Major-Gen. Vladimir Henko, commander of the Army's 10th Artillery Regiment in the Tamura region, told Reuters by phone that the missile is 120 miles (1,800 km) from an airport and vowed to launch immediately if an enemy was hit. Henko said that the missile would launch as soon as the artillery missile would hit the target. The missile used in the crash was made by Astille-Besestrut, a German manufacturer, a top defense official told Reuters on Wednesday. The missile could be detonated within days if leaders in NATO countries decide to believe that a missile threat could reach Russian territory, the official said, adding that citizens have the right to know where the warhead is and what their foreign forces intend to respond to. Russian Prime Minister Victor Poroshenko speaks during a meeting with reporters at a site of a artillery complex at Russia's Kotaimá military complex in Moscow, February 16, 2014. REUTERS/Andrew Kavinich U.S. Ambassador to Turkey Sergei Lavrov told reporters on Wednesday Russian military authorities are investigating an American citizen involved in the crash. Lavrov said it was linked to Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, who has been imprisoned for eight weeks by military forces in eastern Turkey since September. Erdogan, believed to have attacked President Mohamed Fatah's troops on Oct. 25, also made three major military trips to Turkey and issued warnings to security companies following the trip..."
