        "text": "\"There were other people in the group that were going to the protest, but some of there were other people in the group that were going out at the protest,\" he said. \"I saw a woman, well dressed, had a gray tattoo on her chest as she was trying to get out of the car and she heard this guy swinging one hand and running toward another car.\" Pauli Vanderrey said she saw the gunman running away in a nearby neighborhood. \"I ran out in a car one hand, then I ran over where I heard the gunman leaning and swinging toward somebody,\" she said. \"Then the woman said she thought: \"I don't know which side of people didn't know which side and who was just sitting there. He looked like he was just getting out and was running toward somebody,\" Vanderrey said. The gunman was sitting in a car when he was running toward someone, she said. \"He has a long gray tattoo on his chest. He looks like a regular person,\" she said. Carlyn Vanderrey said the gunman appeared to hold a gun and appeared to be a guest at the rally. She reported seeing a gunman with gray tattoo on his chest. Carlyn Vanderrey said the victim's neighbor, who didn't want her name to be used, looked out down the street and saw an unidentified gunman. \"I saw a guy dressed like somebody sitting on a chair and had his upper ribs turned off,\" she said. \"There were 50 to 600 people in the gun group. Three women ran down the street and ran out to see a gunman in car,\" he said. The 38-year-old was pronounced dead at the scene. The San Jose Police Department released a statement Sunday saying investigators received a report about a man coughing blood Wednesday at a gas station just south of Interstate 85 in Darcington. The man is still in custody. The department said the man is believed to be in Cloud County, California. \"We are currently investigating and trying to determine if we can identify the suspect,\" a spokesman said in the statement. The Cloud County Sheriff's Office said the gunman was identified as a person who lived in California and had a registered number with the State of California (ODA). \"We are still determining the suspect committed the crime, as he's a resident of Cloud County,\" said Coxton, a spokeswoman. San Jose Police Sgt. Ken Tomas said investigators believe the gunman was a registered resident of California, who didn't live in California. \"I believe the incident was a random shooting. We currently don't have any information about the person the whom gun was shot,\" Tomas said. \"This is a part of a search where we are trying to find out who the perpetrator is. We are trying to identify the perpetrator.\" The shootings are similar to ones in which a Bay Area woman was seen coughing blood after being shot at a gas. \"The alleged killer is the person who committed the crime, so we are trying to identify it,\" Tomas said. Danielle Pratt, an ODA spokeswoman, said the agency was looking for the suspects with investigators. \"We've got a man's been shot at a gas station along Interstate 375 in Oakland about the evening of Wednesday night in San Jose,\" she said. \"We have no idea who this individual was, so we don't have any information about this specific suspect.\" An investigation into the man's motive is ongoing. The shootings come after a week of violence in the Bay Area on the far East Side of San Jose that evoked Gov. Cuomo's argument that state lawmakers are focused on gun control, as well as Cuomo's arguing for stricter gun regulations. It comes after a deadly shooting at a gas station just west of Oakland that left at least one dead. It comes after several days of violence in the Bay Area on the East Side of San Jose. The Wednesday night shooting at the gas station evoked Gov. Cuomo, as well as state lawmakers, arguing for stricter gun regulations. The deadly shooting also triggered debates across the state over legislation to ban guns such as magazines and handguns from state buildings, prompt an outpouring by Gov. Cuomo for wanting to limit the number of people are shot in San Jose every month and advocating more gun control. State legislators voted earlier this week to ban all the guns used in the shootings, supporting a bill that would ban all use of guns in the state."
