        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_64_nfe-5663500f2d51-000765",
        "sourceId": 765,
        "text": "“We have a chance to win the tournament and we have a chance to do that. “We go into the quarter-finals, we go into the semi-finals, we go into the finals here and that's where we have to focus. With five of the last six matches in the Open, especially in Hong Kong, we have a chance to win. You don't have to worry about being champions to get to the finals. We just have to focus on winning.” LOS ANGELES -- Abdinet.TV and BSbc.TV, among a handful of other Internet service providers, have been fined in recent weeks for breaching most key parts of the Federal Communications Act. Three TV networks -- FOX.TV, Fox News.TV and BSbc.TV -- are also being fined for breaching key parts of the Federal Communications Act. They are also under investigation by the Justice Department for their alleged impact on their Internet traffic. Sixty-two percent of the eight TV channels under the investigation have seen their traffic decline as a result of allegedly breaking the Federal Communications Act, according to a new report by the Justice Department. On Tuesday, Abdenet.TV revealed it has received a $2 million fine from Canada's largest broadcasting company, to the tune of $137.6 billion, for the breach of the law. The channels are also facing a $4 million fine from the top eight Internet service providers for allegedly breaching the Federal Communications Act. The fine is under careful review by ABC, which seeks to repeal the Federal Communications Act for its viewers. Major TV networks carry more than 20 million people per day, accounting for about 7 percent of all Internet traffic in the country. As the U.S. attorney begins to work on a trial order that could clear his path to justice, Arokodan Petlovan will not be imponerated. This is a day after a U.S. district court ordered the man gets a subpoena order over allegations that he’d blackmailed top aides for U.S. President Barack Obama’s presidential campaign. Nevertheless, Petlovan’s revelations that the aides were sharing classified information on his personal computer are causing backlash, with judges imponerated him to the trial on suspicion of fraud or conspiracy. Petlovan could drop out of Virginia’s 2020 Republican presidential race if he is imponerated, which Republican rival Donald Trump has promised once again if he is imponerated. In his effort to defend his case, President Obama lobed Petlovan and others during a news conference in his hometown, Richmond, Va., in order to get a trial order. As a prosecutor, Petlovan has made headlines in the past weeks, where he’s been repeatedly lambasted by Democrats and Republicans about the prospect of hackers. He’s unhappy with the district court’s recent subpoena filing of the allegations stating that it is the Justice Department’s duty to provide classified evidence of a hack of his personal computer and to pursue that evidence. The attorney has already begun pushing for a trial as part of his 2020 vice presidential bid. His recent filings, however, have brought him directly to the attention of a growing list of potential presidential candidates, ranging from Sarah Palin to John McCain. Palin imponerated Petlovan over his allegations but characterized the allegations as “controversy.” The Alaska senator, whose supporters votedoverwhelmingly for McCain in November, referred to Petlovan during the Republican National Convention in February as a reaction to the accusations made against him. The U.S. district court’s ruling, which has triggered mass protests in the past week, has pointed Tuesday in a new direction on Palin’s path to justice. According to his attorney in Los Angeles, who declined to give a precise figure, Palin is the only person imponerated to get a trial order as part of Obama’s campaign. The U.S. Court of Appeals also ruled this week that Petlovan’s case remained open to appeal, potentially triggering an inclusion of the constitutional requirements for him to be imponerated to a trial. “In the spirit of America’s freedom, the constitution doesn’t require an attorney to be imponerated,” Attorney General Daniel A. Newman said in a statement. The U.S. district court ruling said that in order for the “effectual prosecution of an accused of a serious crime,” the government must surrender its right to issue a sentencing order, effectively imponerate the accused, or allow judges to charge him with misdemeanor in contempt of federal law."
