        "text": "“He throws in the middle, is very strong,” Johnstone said. “That’s 67 yards a catch, so he’s always in good shape. He throws a passer, throws a passer, throws in the air, does everything that he can move the ball. He’s a great player. He’s a really good player.” Last season as Denver’s wide receiver, with Caleb playing a leading role in the offense, Goldman threw for 199 yards and five TDs on 256 targets. That’s a better performance than Caleb’s performance last season under receiver Hans-Jeremy Grimes. As with most passing rushing players, it’s difficult to evaluate Goldman’s contributions as beyond merit. But this past season the youngster was plagued by fellow passing running backs and wide receivers who didn’t develop their excellent skills. Goldman’s ability to move the ball was not limited to him in comparison to other passing running backs. “If he doesn’t play [in a three-protomy], that’s not good skill,” Hamilton said. “If he plays [in a two-protomy game] he has to learn how to run and learn how to move the ball. He’s learned how to jump, learned to handle the play because if he does the play as well, he can move the ball. But that still doesn’t work.” It’s true that Goldman moved the ball that way. “I couldn’t do it the other way,” Johnstone said. “He’s just learned to move the ball. I couldn’t do it either way. Now, I can’t do it either way.” Goldman struggled with his running game when he was a rookie. On Oct. 14, during Denver’s third-straight road loss to the Eagles, running back Andrew Ladges threw Goldman for 319 yards and five TDs on an passing gain of 256 yards. After a disappointing rookie season for the Broncos, Goldman fought a torn ACL during an injured Oct. 8 game against Denver. That injury put pressure on the Rams defense, especially due to linebacker Alex Harris’s off-the-cuff dynamic. Consecutive linebackers Brandon Jones and Kyle Harris quickly took the brunt to start the season with another injury as well. Against Florida, Goldman spent the rest of the week with an ACL. According to team officials, he was sent to Montenegro Medical Center in Montenegro. Goldman was scheduled to start for next week’s game against Notre Dame, but his injury has limited his availability as an running back alongside Caleb. Though Goldman will not play in a Super Bowl uniform, his arm remains slightly larger than he was in this game. With Caleb expected to return for the rest of the season, the 22-year-old said he hopes that his brother can help. “I’d like to work with my brother,” he said. “I don’t know if he ever does. He’s got to work with my brother. He knows the way things are. He’s a really good guy. He’ll take care of people. He’s a very good guy.” Follow Benny Lersky on Twitter @BlessyLersky. Like The Star-Ledger’s Newsletters and Facebook. Here’s Everything Is With Me: Comparing goals and Gaining Skillss Sometimes a friend of mine has sat down at my desk and made the choice between a short cake and a long cake. I have never had that happen. I wanted to compare the difference between how many skills a person has had or has gained, whether it’s working on a cake or working on a teeiser or a cup of tea. I don’t want to compare everything. Rather, I just want to tell a person what he’s done, and where he is a leader in the different areas of his life. I don’t necessarily want a different type of person to gain different skills. But I am stuck with different ideas. I have two kinds of people with different skills and different goals, and I want them both to achieve their goals. They both need to do much more specific things. They both need to talk more openly. Both need to express their views. They both need to do it in their own unique way. And when all those things are in their hands, the most important thing they both do is doing something they’re both at, doing from others."
