        "sourceId": 979,
        "text": "\"His handling was great because he has a lot of technical tackles. I can almost see his hand from the corner of his neck and I can see how brave and intelligent in what he does he is. His decision-making was great,\" Motley said. \"He's a well trained footballer who is doing what he does because he knows what to do and has the intelligence for what he does. \"He's a hard-working player so he doesn't look like a big star, but I'm happy to see where he scores.\" Motley scored Germany's first goal in eight matches last season - including the Champions League game against Colombia. He also scored Germany's first away goal in a 4-1 victory over South Africa at the Champions League final in Copenhagen. The World Cup champions return to the Premier League on Sunday. Motley scored Germany's first away goal in eight matches last season - including in the Champions League game against Colombia \"He just had a great away goal,\" Motley said. \"In the second half, he's been a great scorer for us. I'm very excited about the result. I'm really excited about playing against Canada and the motivation is great. \"We've got our own mind and we'll have to work harder as we're in the Premier League. We're trying to put it the right way so we did the right things and we had a good result.\" WASHINGTON (AP) - A jury approved additional felony counts Thursday against a Gainesville man who fatally shot a police officer, while keeping a close eye on what happened in the home of his estranger. Robert E. Barrard Jr., who is 35, was shot as he walked up to the roof of a 3-bedroom apartment on the porch of the 900 block of North Hill Drive in front of a parking lot at about 1:30 a.m. and dumped a knife from the window. Sgt Robert Ering, a Gainesville County Sheriff’s Deputy said he was shot because Barrard’s cell phone appeared to be holding the gun, which can be seen from police surveillance video of the home. Ering said the officer was trying to get the gun out of the home when he noticed that Barrard, 34, was holding a gun in his neck. Barrard hesitated, took his hand from his neck to his left, and tried to pull the gun out of his chest to give it a shot, Ering said. He then pulled the gun out of the home and dumped the gun in his chest, releasing his knife from the right side of his neck. Ering said the officer struck Barrard once in the chest, then killed him, before bringing the weapon out of the home. Police said they could not find evidence of the weapon on Barrard, but that the weapon was verifiable. Ering said a cell phone found on Barrard’s phone contained the name of the officer and that police did not find the weapon. They would not release the name of the cell phone, but it was impossible to determine why police were investigating Barrard in this case. Officer Isaiah Schultz, 20, was on duty after leaving the Barrard station on the night of the shooting. Police said Barrard was retired from the police department in the spring of 2014 following a change in his dress code. An expert in criminal law, he graduated in 2014 from the American Civil Liberties Union’s John H. Murray Law School. In December, he graduated from law school and was enrolled with a master’s degree in handcuff management. During the hearing, Barrard’s lawyer, Kelvin Nichols, said that the shooting did not amount to a hate crime. He did not believe the shooting was a hate crime. “I don’t think anyone would get shot for that, but the fact of it is that there’s no hate in this case,” Nichols said. The jury heard statements made by a woman in Barrard’s apartment in November 2014 involving a paramilitar officer, who told her they had shot with grenades launched in the home. Police said the woman pulled the man over and demanded that he did not move. The woman then approached Barrard and jumped him, where she ultimately threw Barrard onto the roof, where she shot him and killed him. Police said the woman then threw Barrard out of his apartment, where she jumped on to the roof and shot him and killed him. The jury approved that Barrard faces five additional counts of armed assault in connection with the shooting. Please contact police immediately."
